\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US jobless rate rises to two-year high while wage growth slows \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Julia Kollewe \par \uc2 4,327 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2024\par 15:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US economy adds 275,000 jobs, more than expected but that strength is partly offset by downward revisions to past months\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 and the Nasdaq have hit new intra-day record highs, with the tech-heavy index rising 1%, after labour market data showed an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/mar/08/matchesfashion-enter-administration-stock-markets-rise-rate-cut-hopes-us-jobs-non-farm-payrolls-business-live?page=with:block-65eb15508f08bce210001683#block-65eb15508f08bce210001683"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unexpected rise in the unemployment rate to a two-year high  }}}\uc2  and slowing wage growth, which bolstered expectations that the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  could start cutting interest rates by the middle of the year.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/mar/08/us-jobs-february-growth-layoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US adds 275,000 jobs in February as labor market continues to grow  }}}\par \par \uc2 However, over here, the FTSE 100 index has dropped 0.5% and European stock indices are languishing.\par \par \uc2 In other news, \uc2 Crispin Odey\uc2  has been accused of rape in a civil lawsuit in the latest and most serious claim to be made against the prominent City financier.\par \par \uc2 The allegation came to light on Thursday when the high court in London heard an application to decide whether three women could join two others in a legal action alleging misconduct by the founder of \uc2 Odey Asset Management\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Odey, who left OAM last year amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jun/08/morgan-stanley-cuts-ties-with-crispin-odey-after-sexual-assault-allegations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allegations that he sexually assaulted or harassed a number of women  }}}\uc2 , has previously denied any misconduct.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2024/mar/08/city-financier-crispin-odey-accused-of-in-new-civil-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 City financier Crispin Odey accused of rape in new civil lawsuit  }}}\par \par \uc2 the UK luxury clothing retailer \uc2 Matchesfashion\uc2  is cutting 273 jobs \u8211\'20\'13 more than half its workforce \u8211\'20\'13 after its new owner \uc2 Mike Ashley\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/frasers-group"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Frasers Group  }}}\uc2  called in administrators.\par \par \uc2 Matches was acquired by \uc2 Frasers\uc2  three months ago for \u163\'00\'A352m in cash from the private equity firm \uc2 Apax Partners\uc2  . \uc2 Frasers\uc2  said it was not willing to fund a turnaround after the business had \u8220\'20\'1Cconsistently missed its business plan targets\u8221\'20\'1D and made losses.\par \par \uc2 Specialising in luxury labels, from Gucci to Dr Martens, online and through three London stores, Matches generates most of its revenue internationally, delivering to 150 countries outside the UK. It employs 533 people at its head office and stores.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/mar/08/luxury-clothing-brand-matchesfashion-to-enter-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Luxury clothing brand Matchesfashion to enter administration  }}}\par \par \uc2 Here are our other main stories:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/mar/08/and-daughters-suffix-british-company-names"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18In construction, it stands out\u8217\'20\'19: how \u8216\'20\'18& Daughters\u8217\'20\'19 became a firm favourite  }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/mar/08/we-definitely-messed-up-why-did-google-ai-tool-make-offensive-historical-images"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We definitely messed up\u8217\'20\'19: why did Google AI tool make offensive historical images?  }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/mar/08/oil-industry-has-sought-to-block-state-backing-for-green-tech-since-1960s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oil industry has sought to block state backing for green tech since 1960s  }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/mar/08/boots-orders-support-staff-back-into-office-five-days-a-week-from-september"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Boots orders support staff back into office five days a week from September  }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2024/mar/08/spending-accidental-subscriptions-doubled-in-year-uk-citizens-advice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Spending on accidental subscriptions has doubled in a year in UK  }}}\par \par \uc2 Thank you for reading. Have a fab weekend \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19ll be back on Monday. Bye! \u8211\'20\'13 JK\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Duvel halts production after cyber attack\par \par \uc2 A cyber attack has forced the Belgian beer company Duvel to halt production at four breweries in Belgium and a site in Kansas City in Missouri.\par \par \uc2 The company said the suspected ransomware attack happened on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Five of its production facilities were shut down initially but one is back up and running, in Puurs-Sint-Amands.\par \par \uc2 Duvel said it was currently unable to give further details\par \par \uc2 as the investigation into the cause of the cyber-attack is ongoing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The built-in command systems and alarms in the IT-system worked well, so our IT department was immediately informed of the attack.\par \par \uc2 The servers were immediately shut down, which also shut down production at the four Belgian production sites and the production site in Kansas City.\par \par \uc2 Beer drinkers need not fret, though. A spokesperson told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nieuwsblad.be/cnt/dmf20240306_93861112"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 local media  }}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 We have more than enough beer in stock to cover this production stop.\par \par \uc2 The company brews the well-known Duvel in Breendonk, along with Vedett and Maredsous beers. Its other sites are in Oudenaarde (Liefmans), Antwerp (De Koninck) and Achouffe (La Chouffe).\par \par \uc2 In a ransomware attack, hackers threaten to block or leak files unless they are paid.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Labour market conditions are easing despite employment strength, said \uc2 Andrew Hunter\uc2  , deputy chief US economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  , said:\par \par \uc2 The 275,000 rise in non-farm payrolls in February may, at face value, add weight to the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s view that there is no rush to start cutting interest rates, but the downward revisions to previous months\u8217\'20\'19 gains leave recent growth looking less strong than previously thought. Alongside the rise in the unemployment rate to a two-year high and a much weaker rise in wages, there is less reason now to be concerned that renewed labour market strength will drive inflation higher again.\par \par \uc2 The headline gain wasn\u8217\'20\'19t far from our above-consensus 250,000 forecast and partly reflects the unwinding of January\u8217\'20\'19s weather-related distortion. Although payrolls weren\u8217\'20\'19t obviously hit in January, the cold snap at the start of the month left an unusually high number of people unable to work because of the weather. The return of those workers in February helped boost the headline payrolls figure, and may explain the stronger gains in sectors like construction, retail and in particular food services.\par \par \uc2 In a further sign of weakness, the household measure of employment fell again, by 184,000, pushing the unemployment rate up from 3.7% to 3.9%, its highest since January 2022. The rise in the unemployment rate was also partly due to a solid 150,000 increase in the labour force, which should provide a bit more confidence to Fed officials that the supply-side\u8217\'20\'19s strong performance over the past year continues.\par \par \uc2 Average weekly hours worked, which had been hit by the early-January cold, rebounded last month to 34.3. And because that January weakness was concentrated in low-wage sectors, that rebound also explains why average hourly earnings rose by a muted 0.1% m/m, in line with our own forecast. Although the annual growth rate only edged down to 4.3%, the decline in the job quits rate to below its pre-pandemic level suggests wage growth will slow a lot further over the coming months.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US jobless rate uptick, slowing wage growth boost hopes of rate cuts\par \par \uc2 An uptick in the US unemployment rate and slowing wage growth have boosted expectations that the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  could begin cutting interest rates sometime this summer.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate rose to 3.9%, the highest since January 2022, against expectations that it would stay at 3.7%, while average hourly earnings rose 0.1% in February from January, less than the 0.3% growth forecast by economists.\par \par \uc2 The data from the \uc2 US Labor Department\uc2  also showed the economy added 275,000 jobs last month, more than the expected 200,000.\par \par \uc2 However, that strong reading was partly offset by big downward revisions to December and January\u8217\'20\'19s gains, with the latter now at only 229,000, a big reduction from the initial estimate of 353,000.\par \par \uc2 European stocks, government bonds and the euro rose after the data, with the exception of the FTSE 100 in London, which is trading 0.3% or 24 points lower.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2  shares plunge after profit warning\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2  plunged more than 40% today after it warned that annual profits will fall short of expectations.\par \par \uc2 The German meal-kit company said it now expects to post earnings of between \u8364\'20\'AC350m (\u163\'00\'A3298m) and \u8364\'20\'AC400m this year, down from the \u8364\'20\'AC568m analysts had forecast.\par \par \uc2 The share price fell 42% to \u8364\'20\'AC6.84.\par \par \uc2 Berlin-based \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2  also ditched its revenue and profit targets for next year because of higher costs in the development of its \u8220\'20\'1Cready to eat\u8221\'20\'1D division.\par \par \uc2 The warning comes after the recipe box firm was fined \u163\'00\'A3140,000 by the \uc2 UK\u8217\'20\'19s Information Commissioner\u8217\'20\'19s Office\uc2  for sending millions of spam emails and texts to customers.\par \par \uc2 Along with rivals such as Gousto and Mindful Chef, the company benefited from booming demand during the Covid-19 pandemic when people were stuck at home. But customer numbers dropped off after restrictions were removed, and as the cost of living crisis forced many people to tighten their belts.\par \par \uc2 HelloFresh\uc2  , which was founded in 2011 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/forbestreptalks/2015/10/14/the-swedish-startup-that-inspired-meal-kits-speaks-out/#25006afb2fc6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inspired by a Swedish startup called Linas Matkasse  }}}\uc2 , delivers boxes with recipe cards and ingredients for \uc2 Instagram\uc2  -worthy home-cooked meals, such as balsamic steak with red cabbage and potato wedges (\u8220\'20\'1Cno shopping, no planning\u8221\'20\'1D).\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 IMF\uc2  head \uc2 Kristalina Georgieva\uc2  to run for second term\par \par \uc2 The head of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  , \uc2 Kristalina Georgieva\uc2  , has announced that she is running for a second term as fund\u8217\'20\'19s managing director.\par \par \uc2 A Bulgarian economist, aged 70, she has run the fund since 2019, and has already received the backing of the French finance minister \uc2 Bruno Le Maire\uc2  for a second term.\par \par \uc2 The campaign has been kicked off by the \uc2 Bulgarian central bank\uc2  governor Dimitar Radev, who said:\par \par \uc2 Kristalina Georgieva\uc2  has earned the great respect and trust of the \uc2 IMF\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s member countries during the years of her mandate as the fund\u8217\'20\'19s managing director. Under her leadership, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  has supported the global economy with large scale financing nearly 100 member countries as they dealt with the COVID pandemic, the war in Ukraine and the cost-of-living crisis.\par \par \uc2 Many of my colleagues, the governors of the \uc2 IMF\uc2  , would like to see her continue as the managing director of the institution for a second mandate \u8211\'20\'13 a view also expressed by France\u8217\'20\'19s finance minister \uc2 Bruno Le Maire\uc2  at the time of the of the recent G20 meeting in Sao Paolo.\par \par \uc2 Consultations will continue with our European partners on a common view about a potential nomination in accordance with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s process of selection of the MD of the institution.\par \par \uc2 Under Georgieva, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  has deployed $1 trillion in reserves and liquidity since the Covid-19 pandemic. It lent $35bn to 40 emerging market countries and presided over a five-fold increase in interest-free lending to 56 low-income countries. The fund also agreed $1bn in debt relief for the poorest countries after the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The fund has adopted its first ever climate strategy, and has raised $42bn so far for the Resilience and Sustainability Trust, which helps vulnerable emerging and low-income countries cope with climate change and pandemics.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s High Pay Centre has described the \u163\'00\'A38m pay package for \uc2 BP\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s chief executive as \u8220\'20\'1Ca damning indictment of an economic model which is failing consumers and the planet\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 at a time when millions of households in the UK are in fuel poverty.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Speke, spokesperson for the think tank, which is focused on pay, corporate governance and responsible business, said:\par \par \uc2 This huge pay award for \uc2 BP\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s CEO is a damning indictment of an economic model which is failing both consumers and the planet. While households in the UK have faced rocketing bills, which have plunged millions in fuel poverty, \uc2 BP\uc2  somehow can afford to pay its CEO a figure close to 250 times the typical British worker.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile at a global level we are already starting to see the damaging impact of climate change. In such a context governments need to accelerate the transition to clean energy, because the alternative is companies like \uc2 BP\uc2  enriching their executives and shareholders, while households and the planet pay the price.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.58am GMT\par \par \uc2 BP\uc2  paid its boss \u163\'00\'A38m last year; clawed back \u163\'00\'A31.8m from dismissed CEO Looney pay\par \par \uc2 BP\uc2  paid its chief executive \uc2 Murray Auchincloss\uc2  \u163\'00\'A38m last year \u8211\'20\'13 and clawed back \u163\'00\'A31.8m from its former boss \uc2 Bernard Looney\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s pay package.\par \par \uc2 Looney was dismissed in December for failing to disclose to the board personal relationships with colleagues.\par \par \uc2 The pay details were disclosed in the oil giant\u8217\'20\'19s annual report.\par \par \uc2 Auchincloss received a salary of \u163\'00\'A31m for 2023, as well as benefits worth \u163\'00\'A3338,000, a cash allowance in lieu of pension of \u163\'00\'A3190,000 and an annual share bonus of \u163\'00\'A31.8m, and performance shares worth \u163\'00\'A34.7m.\par \par \uc2 In January, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/17/bp-murray-auchincloss-chief-executive-bernard-looney"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the oil company appointed Auchincloss  }}}\uc2 , its former chief financial officer, as its new chief executive after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/sep/13/bp-shares-fall-after-shock-departure-of-chief-executive-bernard-looney"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shock departure of Bernard Looney  }}}\uc2  last year. He had acted as interim CEO since September.\par \par \uc2 The campaign group \uc2 Global Witness\uc2  said Auchincloss\u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A38m paycheck would take an average UK worker 230 years to earn.\par \par \uc2 Alice Harrison, fossil fuels campaign leader at \uc2 Global Witness\uc2  , said:\par \par \uc2 The millions paid out to \uc2 BP\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s CEO contrast with the millions of Brits in energy poverty, showing the sickening reality of our broken energy system. People everywhere, struggling to feed their families or heat their homes, have every right to be angry at \uc2 BP\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s huge profits and payouts.\par \par \uc2 BP\uc2  and its CEO count among the biggest winners of Russia\u8217\'20\'19s war in Ukraine. \uc2 BP\uc2  , which still owns a big chunk of the Russian oil company \uc2 Rosneft\uc2  , profiteered massively from the resulting turbulence in energy markets, and now the firm has decided to give its CEO a multi-million, fat cat pat on the back whilst most people are living paycheck to paycheck.\par \par \uc2 The government is missing the opportunity to introduce a serious windfall tax and CEO bonus tax.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Frasers Group\uc2  fell nearly 2% this morning, after it announced that it was putting \uc2 Matchesfashion\uc2  into administration, less than three months after buying the luxury online retailer from the private equity firm \uc2 Apax\uc2  for around \u163\'00\'A350m.\par \par \uc2 Victoria Scholar, head of investment at interactive investor, said:\par \par \uc2 The financial woes facing \uc2 Matchesfashion\uc2  highlight the broader slowdown in the luxury goods market. High-end luxury demand has been waning amid a weak post-covid recovery in China as well as broader global macroeconomic pressures. While luxury brands typically raise prices without much of a problem for demand, perhaps now price hikes have gone too far as consumers\u8217\'20\'19 propensity to spend wanes. In another worrying sign for the sector, \uc2 Burberry\uc2  issued a profit warning in January with its shares down by more than 50% over the past 12 months.\par \par \uc2 The speedy transition by \uc2 Frasers\uc2  from its purchase of \uc2 Matchesfashion\uc2  to its entry into administration suggests the financial woes facing the online fashion platform are clearly much worse than \uc2 Frasers\uc2  had thought, landing the brand in an unsalvageable situation. It also shows how even the e-commerce sector, which is relieved of many bricks and mortar retailers\u8217\'20\'19 cost pressures, is still facing immense financial stress amid the weak global growth backdrop. Its rival \uc2 Net A Porter\uc2  similarly struggled with losses in the first half of last year. A lack of management continuity with a series of quick succession CEO changes in recent years haven\u8217\'20\'19t helped \uc2 Matchesfashion\uc2  either.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Frasers Group\uc2  are under pressure, trading down nearly 2%, bringing its year-to-date slide to more than 10% in a disappointing start to the year for its investors after a strong run since the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 European shares are drifting lower as traders await the non-farm payrolls jobs report at lunchtime.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 is down some 10 points, or 0.14%, while the Dax has lost 0.2%. France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC is unchanged and Italy\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE MiB edged 0.1% higher.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 More on the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  , which has hinted that its first interest rate cut could come in June.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the central bank cut its forecast for price growth this year from 2.7% to 2.3%. It now expects inflation to fall to 1.9% in the summer of 2025, and stay there until the end of 2026.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  struck a cautious tone, saying more evidence was needed before the \uc2 ECB\uc2  cuts rates.\par \par \uc2 There is a definite decline (in inflation) which is underway and we are making good progress towards our inflation target. We are more confident as a result, but we are not sufficiently confident.\par \par \uc2 Investors have pencilled in three, or possibly four, cuts this year to the rate the \uc2 ECB\uc2  pays on bank deposits, which would take it from the current 4% to 3.25% or 3.0%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.36am GMT\par \par \uc2 Here is our full story on \uc2 Matchesfashion\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/mar/08/luxury-clothing-brand-matchesfashion-to-enter-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Luxury clothing brand Matchesfashion to enter administration  }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.22am GMT\par \par \uc2 Richard Hunter, head of markets at interactive investor, has looked at the financial markets, and ahead to the US jobs report at lunchtime.\par \par \uc2 With little to upset the applecart, US markets powered to new record highs as peak optimism continued to drive buying interest.\par \par \uc2 Investors have been reassured over recent days following Federal Reserve chair Powell\u8217\'20\'19s comments to lawmakers, which have contained no negative surprises. Most pertinently, the Fed stands prepared to reduce interest rates this year when circumstances dictate, and implied that such a move is not far away, even though there is no immediate rush to ease monetary policy in light of consistently favourable economic data.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the relatively benign backdrop will be put to the test again today with the release of the non-farm payrolls report. Investors will be hoping to see employment beginning to slow, while remaining solid, while also keeping an eye on wage growth which itself is inflationary. The data is expected to show that 200,000 jobs were added in February, following the previous month\u8217\'20\'19s blowout number of 353,000, while unemployment is likely to remain unchanged at 3.7%.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, technology stocks continued to forge ahead in anticipation of the easier monetary conditions to follow and after what was a generally pleasing reporting season. The semiconductor index was a particular beneficiary of investor interest, as buyers sought to ride the current euphoric wave surrounding the potential for all things AI, with \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  enjoying further gains. In the year to date, the Nasdaq has now added 8.4%, closely followed by a gain of 8.1% for the S&P 500, while the Dow Jones is also in positive territory to the tune of 2.9%.\par \par \uc2 Turning to Asian stocks, he said:\par \par \uc2 Asian stocks largely joined the investment party, with most central banks increasingly agreed on the downward trajectory for interest rates, and with Japan\u8217\'20\'19s Nikkei index continuing to attract overseas buying interest. This was despite the fact that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  could be heading in the opposite direction in exiting negative interest rates, which sent the yen higher after a recent spell of weakness, with wage growth rising to a level which could prompt the central bank to begin tightening policy.\par \par \uc2 China markets were mildly positive, although the disappointment of this week\u8217\'20\'19s National People Congress is still dampening sentiment. The lack of any announcements regarding an aggressive stimulative stance in reviving the economy at a time of high unemployment, low consumer confidence and an embattled property sector remains a source of despair for investors. The authorities appear keen to let the economy recover at its own pace and will have taken some solace from the latest import and export growth numbers, which came in ahead of expectations.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 Packaging firm \uc2 Mondi\uc2  buys \uc2 DS Smith\uc2  ; wealth manager \uc2 Mattioli Woods\uc2  to be taken private\par \par \uc2 There has been a flurry of takeover deals (or offers) in recent days and weeks, and today is no different.\par \par \uc2 The packaging firm \uc2 Mondi\uc2  , based in Weybridge, has pounced on smaller rival \uc2 DS Smith\uc2  and agreed an all-share deal worth \u163\'00\'A35.14bn, which will create a paper and packaging group worth more than \u163\'00\'A310bn. Both companies said:\par \par \uc2 The combination is an exciting opportunity to create a pan-European industry leader in paper-based sustainable packaging solutions.\par \par \uc2 The London-listed company is to be bought out as wealth manager \uc2 Mattioli Woods\uc2  agreed to a \u163\'00\'A3432m offer from the London-based private equity firm \uc2 Pollen Street Capital\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Private equity firms and some industry firms have been snapping up British assets because they are considered cheap, as sterling remains weak following Brexit, the Covid pandemic and the disastrous mini-budget 1 1/2 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Nationwide building society agreed to buy its high-street rival \uc2 Virgin Money\uc2  for nearly \u163\'00\'A33bn in the largest UK banking deal since the 2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The two lenders have reached a preliminary agreement on the key terms of the takeover, which \u8211\'20\'13 if approved \u8211\'20\'13 would narrow competition with the big four banks. It would create a combined group with \u163\'00\'A3366bn in total assets, nearly 700 branches and more than 23 million customers.\par \par \uc2 Buying \uc2 Virgin Money\uc2  , the UK\u8217\'20\'19s sixth-largest retail bank, would also solidify Nationwide\u8217\'20\'19s position as the second-largest mortgage lender behind \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2  , according to data gathered by the \uc2 industry body UK Finance\uc2  on outstanding home loans.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/mar/07/nationwide-to-buy-virgin-money-in-29bn-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nationwide to buy Virgin Money in \u163\'00\'A32.9bn deal  }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.35am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 Matchesfashion\uc2  was launched by a couple, Tom and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/fashion/2013/may/18/ceo-matches-tips-summer-wardrobe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ruth Chapman  }}}\uc2  , who opened their first store in Wimbledon in south London in 1987.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2017/sep/01/husband-wife-chapman-bank-400m-sale-matches-fashion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 They banked \u163\'00\'A3400m  }}}\uc2  after selling their online designer empire Matchesfashion.com to private equity investors in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Funds advised by \uc2 Apax Partners\uc2  , a firm that owns Karl Lagerfeld and previously invested in Tommy Hilfiger, agreed to buy a majority stake in the business in a deal that valued Matches at \u163\'00\'A3800m. The Chapmans, both in their 50s, cashed in the vast majority of their 67% stake, and stepped back from day-to-day involvement but retained an advisory role.\par \par \uc2 However, the firm has struggled recently amid a slowdown in the luxury sector, and made a loss of \u163\'00\'A333.5m last year.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.43am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: \uc2 Matchesfashion\uc2  to enter administration; stock markets rise on rate cut hopes\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Mike Ashley\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Frasers Group\uc2  is putting \uc2 Matchesfashion\uc2  into administration, just weeks after buying the online luxury clothing retailer, putting hundreds of jobs in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 Frasers\uc2  bought the firm \u8211\'20\'13 which sells fashion brands including Balenciaga, Gucci, Prada, \uc2 Stella McCartney\uc2  and Valentino \u8211\'20\'13 for \u163\'00\'A352m from the private equity firm \uc2 Apax\uc2  in late December. According to reports, \uc2 Teneo\uc2  is being appointed as administrators.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to the stock exchange, \uc2 Frasers\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 Since \uc2 Frasers\uc2  acquired Matches, the business has consistently missed its business plan targets and, notwithstanding support from the group, has continued to make material losses. Whilst Matchess\u8217\'20\'19 management team has tried to try to find a way to stabilise the business, it has become clear that too much change would be required to restructure it, and the continued funding requirements would be far in excess of amounts that the group considers to be viable.\par \par \uc2 In light of this, \uc2 Frasers\uc2  has been informed that the directors of Matches have taken the decision to put the Matches group into administration. \uc2 Frasers\uc2  remains committed to the luxury market and its brand partners.\par \par \uc2 The news was first reported by Sky News.\par \par \uc2 enltrExclusive: \uc2 Mike Ashley\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Frasers Group\uc2  is putting \uc2 Matchesfashion\uc2  , the luxury online clothing platform it bought in December for \u163\'00\'A352m, into administration following weaker trading and the departure of a number of brands; a stock exchange announcement is likely tomorrow. More soon.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Mark Kleinman (@MarkKleinmanSky) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MarkKleinmanSky/status/1765797619228348869?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 March 7, 2024  }}}\par \par \uc2 Asian shares have hit a seven-month high, after investors were cheered by hints for potential interest rate cuts in the summer, and ahead of a key US jobs report today.\par \par \uc2 They followed in the footsteps of higher US stocks, and the S&P 500 hit a record closing high of 5,157.34, up more than 1%, as the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  chief indicated rate cuts are likely to come this year.\par \par \uc2 MSCI\u8217\'20\'19s broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan peaked at 538.47 points, its highest level since August. Japan\u8217\'20\'19s Nikkei rose 0.2% while Hong Kong\u8217\'20\'19s Hang Seng gained 1.3% and the Shanghai Composite climbed 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  kept interest rates unchanged yesterday and its president \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  , who had been pushing back against the idea of early rate cuts, said at the press conference:\par \par \uc2 We did not discuss cuts for this meeting, but we are just beginning to discuss the dialling back of our restrictive stance.\par \par \uc2 She hinted strongly that this could happen at the \uc2 ECB\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s 6 June meeting, when wage data for the first quarter will have been published.\par \par \uc2 We will know a little more in April, but we will know a lot more in June\par \par \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  struck a similar tone on the path of US rates. He said yesterday the US central bank was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot far\u8221\'20\'1D from gaining the confidence it needs in falling inflation to begin cutting borrowing costs. He told the Senate Banking Committee:\par \par \uc2 I think we are in the right place. We are waiting to become more confident that inflation is moving sustainably down to 2%. When we do get that confidence, and we\u8217\'20\'19re not far from it, it will be appropriate to begin to dial back the level of restriction so that we don\u8217\'20\'19t drive the economy into recession.\par \par \uc2 In Germany, industrial production rose by 1% in January from the previous month, but December\u8217\'20\'19s decline was revised to 2% from 1.6%.\par \par \uc2 Construction recovered alongside the chemical and food industries and machine maintenance and assembly, while carmakers posted a 7.6% slump, official data showed this morning.\par \par \uc2 The Agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am GMT: Eurozone fourth-quarter GDP third estimate (forecast: 0%)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm GMT: US non-farm payrolls for February (forecast: 200,000)\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240308ek380012z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
