\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US adds 275,000 jobs in February as labor market continues to grow\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani in New York \par \uc2 619 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2024\par 15:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Layoffs climb sharply with last month\u8217\'20\'19s rate the highest of any February since 2009 but expert says \u8216\'20\'18job gains remain solid\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Employers added 275,000 jobs across the US last month, as the labor market continues to grow at a clip in the face of the high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Job growth exceeded expectations of 200,000 on Wall Street in February and rose from the previous month\u8217\'20\'19s revised rate of 229,000.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment stood at 3.9%, according to data released on Friday morning by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  (\uc2 BLS\uc2 ), a 0.2% increase compared to January. The unemployment rate has now been under 4% for 25 consecutive months, the longest such streak since the 1960s.\par \par \uc2 Industries that saw an increase in jobs include healthcare, government employment and food services and drinking places.\par \par \uc2 While jobs continue to be added each month, activity in the labor market has cooled. The change in employment for December was revised down by 43,000, from 333,000 to 290,000, and January was revised down by 124,000, from 353,000 to 229,000.\par \par \uc2 Job openings and the number of workers quitting their jobs fell slightly in January, according to official data released earlier this week. The number of workers who resigned from their jobs in January, at 3.385 million, was the lowest since January 2021.\par \par \uc2 Layoffs have, meanwhile, climbed sharply. Outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2  reported yesterday that layoffs last month were the highest of any February since 2009, at 84,658: up 3% on the month and 9% on the year.\par \par \uc2 But jobs growth has remained robust. Private sector payrolls increased by 140,000 last month, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/adp-national-employment-report-private-sector-employment-increased-by-140-000-jobs-in-february-annual-pay-was-up-5-1-302081418.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from payrolls firm ADP, an increase on January.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJob gains remain solid. Pay gains are trending lower but are still above inflation,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , chief economist at ADP. \u8220\'20\'1CThe labor market is dynamic, but doesn\u8217\'20\'19t tip the scales in terms of a Fed rate decision this year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Economists have been closely watching jobs and inflation figures as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  tries to guide the economy towards a so-called \u8220\'20\'1Csoft landing\u8221\'20\'1D, where price growth is normalized and a recession is avoided.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago the Fed embarked upon an aggressive campaign to bring down inflation. It peaked at 9% in June 2022, and has since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/feb/13/us-inflation-rate-january"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 retreated to 3.1%}}}\uc2  in January.\par \par \uc2 The Fed, which wants inflation to fall to 2%, increased interest rates 11 times to between 5.25 and 5.5% \u8211\'20\'13 the highest in over 20 years \u8211\'20\'13 as it battled to bring it down.\par \par \uc2 Officials at the central bank are \u8220\'20\'1Cnot looking for better inflation readings than we\u8217\'20\'19ve had\u8221\'20\'1D, its chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/mar/06/jerome-powell-interest-rate-cuts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told Congress}}}\uc2  this week. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re just looking for more of them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Powell also implied that the Fed will hold interest rates steady until at least the summer, shutting down any expectation that officials would cut rates anytime soon. Powell told Congress that he has \u8220\'20\'1Cno reason to think\u8221\'20\'1D that the US is in immediate danger of entering a recession.\par \par \uc2 When jobs figures came in higher than expected last month, \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/feb/02/us-jobs-report-january"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  that \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica\u8217\'20\'19s economy is the strongest in the world\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In his annual State of the Union address Thursday night, the president struck a similar tone, promising that the \u8220\'20\'1Clanding is and will be soft\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI came to office determined to get us through one of the toughest periods in the nation\u8217\'20\'19s history,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make news, but in a thousand cities and towns the American people are writing the greatest comeback story never told.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240308ek380025u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
