\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 We must start preparing the US workforce for the effects of AI \u8211\'20\'13 now\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,303 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 February 2024\par 14:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Artificial intelligence can make companies more efficient and workers happier \u8211\'20\'13 or cause mass misery. The choice is ours\par \par \uc2 As artificial intelligence spreads rapidly across America\u8217\'20\'19s economy, there\u8217\'20\'19s a lively debate about how it will transform the future of work. What many people fail to realize is that AI has already changed millions of workers\u8217\'20\'19 jobs \u8211\'20\'13 often for the worse.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , some {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theverge.com/2019/4/25/18516004/amazon-warehouse-fulfillment-centers-productivity-firing-terminations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warehouse}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/features/2021-06-28/fired-by-bot-amazon-turns-to-machine-managers-and-workers-are-losing-out"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 delivery drivers}}}\uc2  complain that AI-driven bots have fired them without any human intervention whatsoever. At some companies, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/08/14/business/worker-productivity-tracking.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surveillance apps}}}\uc2  track how much time workers spend in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/5dgn73/internal-documents-show-amazons-dystopian-system-for-tracking-workers-every-minute-of-their-shifts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trips to the bathroom}}}\uc2 , with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mississippitoday.org/2022/08/08/maximus-workers-in-hattiesburg-strike-again-calling-out-timed-bathroom-breaks-and-covid-19-policies/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 some workers protesting}}}\uc2  that the time limits are too strict.\par \par \uc2 Many corporations gather information about their employees through webcams, keyboards, internet activity and phone logs, and that can include private information about workers\u8217\'20\'19 political opinions, sexual orientation, union activity and even their emotions and mental state. Inside many call centers, AI tools {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cio.com/article/1285848/how-ai-can-boost-customer-satisfaction-retention-and-loyalty-in-the-contact-center.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 monitor the calls}}}\uc2  that agents make, and based on factors {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://techhq.com/2023/07/customer-support-contact-center-ai-automation-solutions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 like the agent\u8217\'20\'19s or customer\u8217\'20\'19s tone}}}\uc2  of voice, those tools often send messages to an agent\u8217\'20\'19s computer, directing them to talk with more empathy or energy.\par \par \uc2 And then there are the forecasts that AI will wipe out millions of jobs. The \uc2 McKinsey Global Institute{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mckinsey.com/mgi/our-research/generative-ai-and-the-future-of-work-in-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimates}}}\uc2  that by 2030, tasks that account for up to 30% of the hours now worked across the US could be automated, and that AI will push 12 million American workers out of their jobs. \uc2 Goldman Sachs{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gspublishing.com/content/research/en/reports/2023/03/27/d64e052b-0f6e-45d7-967b-d7be35fabd16.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 predicts}}}\uc2  that AI will disrupt 300m jobs worldwide by 2030.\par \par \uc2 Workers worry that AI will mean more stress, surveillance, and layoffs. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to be that way\par \par \uc2 C-suite executives can\u8217\'20\'19t wait to deploy more AI in their offices, warehouses and factories, but many workers, white-collar and blue-collar alike, worry that AI will mean only bad news for them: more stress, surveillance, and speed-ups, and more layoffs, too. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to be that way.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ecommons.cornell.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/a0ac9f50-5a22-4b3d-a9d9-2cc06824e31d/content"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Germany and several other}}}\uc2  European countries, workers often have a voice in how their employers roll out and use artificial intelligence. German law requires that companies notify their works councils (worker-management committees that exist in most German workplaces) in advance about any plans to introduce AI. Those councils often then discuss how to use AI, typically with an eye to having it complement workers and make them more productive, instead of replacing them.\par \par \uc2 Virginia Doellgast, a \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  expert on AI and work, told me that the German model, with its works councils and strong unions, leads to less employee resistance to AI, smoother rollouts of AI-related technologies, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/10242589221143044"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased productivity}}}\uc2  that benefits companies and workers alike.\par \par \uc2 But in the United States, a country where workplace decisions are typically top-down with employees having little or no input, workers often feel like victims and guinea pigs when it comes to AI. Indeed, many workers don\u8217\'20\'19t even know when their employers are using AI to surveil them or speed up their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Labor leaders wish that US workers could have a strong voice in how AI is introduced and implemented, but that\u8217\'20\'19s wishful thinking in a nation where just 6% of workers in private industry are in labor unions. At unionized workplaces, but not non-union ones, employees have a right not only to negotiate about AI when it substantially affects working conditions, but also to push for protections against some of AI\u8217\'20\'19s worst effects. Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.poynter.org/commentary/2023/writers-guild-wins-protections-against-artificial-intelligence-newsroom-unions/#:~:text=AI%20was%20one%20of%20the,Guild%20summary%20of%20the%20deal."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writers\u8217\'20\'19}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/policy/technology/4307400-sag-aftra-releases-agreement-details-ai-protections/#:~:text=Members%20of%20the%20SAG%2DAFTRA,agreement%20with%20studios%20released%20Monday."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 actors\u8217\'20\'19}}}\uc2  unions won important protections on AI in the negotiations that settled their lengthy strikes last year.\par \par \uc2 One labor leader told me that unions certainly recognize that new technologies can increase productivity and overall prosperity. But he added that AI-related technologies often \u8220\'20\'1Cspeed up the work, deskill the work, make workplaces more stressful and make jobs more demanding\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At some companies, AI-powered tools keep tabs on whether employees meet their production quotas, and those tools sometimes automatically increase the quotas of employees who regularly meet them. With employers increasingly using AI to screen r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9s, AI has faced complaints about discriminating against {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/daily-labor-report/eeoc-settles-first-of-its-kind-ai-bias-lawsuit-for-365-000"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 older applicants}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/amazon-built-ai-to-hire-people-discriminated-against-women-2018-10"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 women}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thomsonreuters.com/en-us/posts/legal/ai-enabled-anti-black-bias/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 people of color}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fastcompany.com/91001379/ai-pregnancy-discrimination-harassment-how-government-fought-workplace-discrimination-2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 workers with disabilities}}}\uc2 . At many call centers, AI-powered apps review the transcripts of agents\u8217\'20\'19 conversations with customers and sometimes give warnings or demerits to agents for straying too far from the assigned script.\par \par \uc2 Some economists warn that if corporations can roll out AI with little input from workers, then AI will increase production and profits for corporations, with few or any gains for workers \u8211\'20\'13 all {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theconversation.com/ai-will-increase-inequality-and-raise-tough-questions-about-humanity-economists-warn-203056"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 while increasing}}}\uc2  the nation\u8217\'20\'19s overall {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/unregulated-ai-will-worsen-inequality-warns-nobel-winning-economist-joseph-stiglitz/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 income inequality}}}\uc2 . Workers will suffer from speed-ups, stress and layoffs, and society at large may have to finance tens of billions of dollars in unemployment insurance, health coverage and perhaps food and rent assistance for many who lose their jobs to AI.\par \par \uc2 To minimize AI\u8217\'20\'19s negative effects on America\u8217\'20\'19s 160 million workers, there are many things government, labor and business should do. Government should require companies to notify their workers when they\u8217\'20\'19re using AI tools to monitor them.\par \par \uc2 Recognizing that workers aren\u8217\'20\'19t in a position to assess the downsides of AI, \uc2 Liz Shuler\uc2 , president of the \uc2 AFL-CIO union\uc2  federation, has called for having a government agency {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/newsletters/weekly-shift/2024/01/22/what-labor-advocates-want-from-ai-policy-00136906"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vet}}}\uc2  AI technologies to determine whether they\u8217\'20\'19re harmful to workers \u8211\'20\'13 just as the \uc2 FDA\uc2  vets drugs to make \u8220\'20\'1Csure drugs don\u8217\'20\'19t kill people before they\u8217\'20\'19re put out into the world\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 With AI threatening to cause large-scale layoffs, the government should create incentives for employers to embrace four-day, even three-day workweeks to spread the work around and minimize layoffs. For example, if AI makes a 1,000-employee company consider laying off 200 people, that company could avoid layoffs by shifting its 1,000 employees to four-day workweeks.\par \par \uc2 Labor unions need to snap to attention on AI and educate workers, both union and non-union, about AI\u8217\'20\'19s harms and benefits \u8211\'20\'13 it can mean more invasiveness, but also increase productivity and hopefully workers\u8217\'20\'19 wages, too. Unions should also educate workers about how to press their employers to reduce AI\u8217\'20\'19s harms and to share the fruits of AI so that workers, and not just shareholders, benefit from the increased productivity and profits it produces. (The AFL-CIO\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/tech-institute"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Technology Institute}}}\uc2  is already doing some of this educating.)\par \par \uc2 Unions should press companies to use AI in ways that complement rather than displace workers, and that means pushing for enhanced training to help employees work with AI. Unions should of course keep up their efforts to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2023/04/27/unions-tech-industry-labor-youtube-sega"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unionize tech workers}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As for business, Michael Richards, policy director at the \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://americaninnovators.com/advocacy/letter-to-ostp-on-automated-worker-surveillance-rfi/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 technology engagement center}}}\uc2 , said that \u8220\'20\'1Cwhile there are clear benefits\u8221\'20\'1D to AI, \u8220\'20\'1Cthere are legitimate concerns\u8221\'20\'1D surrounding the technology. Richards said companies \u8220\'20\'1Crecognize that engaging in an inclusive dialogue about using new technologies is critical\u8221\'20\'1D. Such a dialogue could reduce workers\u8217\'20\'19 distrust of AI and encourage employee input, for instance, about ways to tweak AI tools to have them work more efficiently alongside workers.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s little doubt that AI will bring big changes, good and bad, to America\u8217\'20\'19s workplaces. To reduce AI\u8217\'20\'19s harmful effects, President Biden should create a panel of government, union and business officials, much like the tripartite boards that President Franklin Roosevelt created to maximize arms production during the second world war. This new panel should make recommendations about how best to use AI in America\u8217\'20\'19s workplaces, all with the goal of maximizing AI\u8217\'20\'19s benefits for the nation and sharing the gains from AI while minimizing the pain for workers.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Steven Greenhouse, a senior fellow at the \uc2 Century Foundation\uc2 , is an American labor and workplace journalist and writer\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240229ek2t001k0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
