\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 US unions target the housing affordability crisis as their \u8216\'20\'18biggest issue\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,350 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 February 2024\par 12:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Organized labor across the country is now setting its sights on housing costs as rents and mortgages continue to soar\par \par \uc2 As housing has become a top issue in strikes and protests in recent months, US unions are pushing for change and backing innovative solutions for the housing affordability crisis.\par \par \uc2 With US house prices and rents rising in recent years, and high interest rates and inflation taking their toll, housing affordability has become a major issue at the bargaining table for US labor unions. Many workers are facing 60-, 90-, even 120-minute commutes to work because they cannott afford to live near their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Housing has been a big issue in the recent rolling strikes by thousands {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2023/10/15/1205951003/la-hotel-workers-strike-for-better-housing#:~:text=One%20of%20the%20reasons%20thousands,the%20hotels%20where%20they%20work."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 of Los Angeles hotel workers}}}\uc2 . In Oregon, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.koin.com/news/oregon/not-able-to-afford-housing-yamhill-county-employees-association-kicks-off-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 400 Yamhill county government employees}}}\uc2  went on strike in November because, the union said, \u8220\'20\'1Cmany workers are not able to afford housing\u8221\'20\'1D. In the Twin Cities, worker dismay about large rent hikes is fueling plans for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://workdaymagazine.org/what-could-we-win-together-labor-in-minnesota-gears-up-for-a-major-escalation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a multi-union strike}}}\uc2  by up to 30,000 workers in March. When San Francisco hotel workers hold contract talks later this year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/housing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 housing}}}\uc2  affordability will be a top issue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHousing is a very important issue for our members,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anand Singh, president of the \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  hotel workers\u8217\'20\'19 union in San Francisco. \u8220\'20\'1COur members can\u8217\'20\'19t absorb the sudden rent increases they\u8217\'20\'19ve seen. They\u8217\'20\'19re evicted from their homes. They\u8217\'20\'19re pushed further and further down the housing ladder.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Milagros Vela, a housekeeper at the Hilton San Francisco Union Square, said her monthly rent jumped from $1,800 to $2,800 during the pandemic, and as a result, she moved to a less expensive town, Antioch, 45 miles (72km) east of San Francisco. \u8220\'20\'1CMany days it\u8217\'20\'19s a two-hour commute each way,\u8221\'20\'1D Vela said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very frustrating. It prevents me from spending time with my daughters and grandkids.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The affordability crisis has spurred many responses. Last November, the \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers\uc2  and other unions helped win approval of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nwpb.org/2023/11/29/tacoma-tenant-protections-measure-passes-now-what/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ballot initiative in Tacoma}}}\uc2 , Washington, that bans cold-weather evictions between 1 November and 1 April and bars evicting households with students or educators anytime during the school year. The measure also requires landlords that raise rents by 5% or more to offer two months\u8217\'20\'19 relocation assistance to tenants \u8211\'20\'13 and for rent increases of 10% or more, three months\u8217\'20\'19 assistance.\par \par \uc2 Other unions have taken the issue head on, for instance, by lobbying for the building of more affordable housing. With the teachers\u8217\'20\'19 union complaining that educators can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to live in the Miami area, the Miami-Dade county school district {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/local/miami/2024/01/29/dade-workforce-housing-schools-development?link_id=16&can_id=6342b6b27a7dc11d0ba689e86dada96f&source=email-afscme-fyi-wednesday-january-31-2024&email_referrer=email_2189425&email_subject=afscme-fyi-nil-wednesday-january-31-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is planning to convert several schools}}}\uc2  into teacher housing. In Las Vegas, the Culinary Union, which represents 60,000 hotel and restaurant workers, has increased {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://culinaryunion226.org/affiliates/housing-fund"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its down payment assistance}}}\uc2  to $25,000 for union members buying their first homes.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Bahn, a crisis counselor for the San Jose school district, wishes she could afford to live in San Jose, but many modest, 1,200-sq-ft homes there can often cost $1.2m. Bahn instead lives 90 minutes away in Monterey county. \u8220\'20\'1CI would love to live closer to where I work, but it\u8217\'20\'19s almost impossible,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The San Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Teachers Association is urging the school district to build subsidized housing for teachers on unused or underused school property. \u8220\'20\'1CHousing is probably the biggest issue for us,\u8221\'20\'1D said Renata Sanchez, the union\u8217\'20\'19s president. \u8220\'20\'1CIt totally messes with people\u8217\'20\'19s work-life balance. We are at our schools a minimum of seven hours a day, and when you add in up to four hours of commuting time each day, that\u8217\'20\'19s already half of your day. Our members are having to make hard decisions: am I going to do what I want to do for my students or do I go home to be a partner or parent to my own family?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One possible model for San Jose is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.santaclarausd.org/about-us/departments/facility-development-and-planning/teacher-housing-foundation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Casa del Maestro}}}\uc2 , a 70-apartment complex in nearby Santa Clara which that community\u8217\'20\'19s school district built. It rents apartments to teachers at 80% below market rate for up to seven years, helping them save up for home ownership.\par \par \uc2 Margie Wysocki, president of United Teachers of Santa Clara, said market rents are so high that some educators have taken second jobs. \u8220\'20\'1CSome teachers have to take roommates. Some live with their parents,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. And the housing crunch is often worse for school janitors, teacher aides and cafeteria workers.\par \par \uc2 In the Twin Cities, labor leaders are threatening {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://workdaymagazine.org/what-could-we-win-together-labor-in-minnesota-gears-up-for-a-major-escalation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an innovative, multi-union strike}}}\uc2  next month with housing affordability a leading issue, along with higher pay and improving schools. Unions representing 1,000 airport workers, 2,500 security guards, 4,500 janitors, and thousands of teachers and support staff have coordinated their contract expiration dates so they can all go on strike in early March.\par \par \uc2 The unions want Minneapolis to enact a law limiting rent increases and are asking for guarantees that if government money in the Twin Cities is used to convert office buildings to apartments, those projects will produce affordable housing.\par \par \uc2 Eva Lopez, a janitor at a major corporation\u8217\'20\'19s offices in Minneapolis, complained that rent for her two-bedroom apartment recently jumped by $350 to $1,600, a 28% increase. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very difficult to keep up with the rent increases with the wages we\u8217\'20\'19re earning,\u8221\'20\'1D Lopez said.\par \par \uc2 Greg Nammacher, president of an \uc2 SEIU\uc2  local representing Twin Cities janitors and security guards, said: \u8220\'20\'1COur survey of our members found that by a multiple of two housing was their biggest issue outside of wages and benefits.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s labor activism about housing is in many ways a return to the past. \u8220\'20\'1CDuring the first three-quarters of the 20th century, unions were a strong voice and advocate for affordable housing for working families,\u8221\'20\'1D said Peter Dreier, an urban policy expert at Occidental College in Los Angeles. \u8220\'20\'1CThey built their own union-sponsored housing. They fought for decent public housing. They fought for better code enforcement to rid cities of slums.\u8221\'20\'1D Their focus on housing faded in the 1980s as unions declined and laid off many staffers.\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles unions are leading the way in pushing innovative ideas to finance affordable housing. Working with many community groups, LA unions won passage of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/opinion/story/2023-11-08/editorial-how-about-if-people-stop-fighting-measure-ula-and-see-how-much-good-it"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cmansion tax\u8221\'20\'1D ballot measure}}}\uc2  with 58% of the vote. It creates a 4% real estate transfer tax on property sales exceeding $5m and 5.5% on sales above $10m. Supporters say the tax, which hits a tiny percentage of sales, will collect at least $600m a year to finance affordable housing and provide subsidies to prevent evictions.\par \par \uc2 Even as LA\u8217\'20\'19s real estate industry has challenged the tax in court \u8211\'20\'13 it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailynews.com/2023/10/26/judge-upholds-measure-ula-to-pay-for-housing-homeless-prevention-programs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost the first round}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 officials in several other cities have expressed interest in adopting a similar scheme to finance housing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn LA there is an extreme need for housing. Thousands upon thousands of Angelenos are sleeping on the streets every night,\u8221\'20\'1D said Chris Hannan, president of the California State Building and Construction Trades Council, a union group. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need a well-rounded approach to get people housing and make housing truly affordable. That\u8217\'20\'19s absolutely something the labor movement should be involved in.\u8221\'20\'1D Hannan said LA\u8217\'20\'19s mansion tax \u8220\'20\'1Cshould be a model across the country\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As part of its wave of strikes, the LA hotel workers\u8217\'20\'19 union demanded that hotels create a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2023/10/15/1205976886/a-hotel-workers-3-hour-commute-tells-the-story-of-las-housing-crisis-and-her-str#:~:text=The%20idea%20is%20that%20hotels,its%20high%20cost%20of%20living."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 7% tax on guests}}}\uc2 , with the money helping finance affordable housing for hospitality workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne hundred of our members said they lost their homes during the pandemic,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kurt Petersen, the union\u8217\'20\'19s co-president. \u8220\'20\'1COur fight is about who can live in LA, about who can afford to live in LA.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 His union failed to persuade hotels to agree to the 7% tax, but it won large raises to help union members afford rising rents. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a long way to go in solving the housing crisis,\u8221\'20\'1D Petersen said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe feel our union can play a part in solving it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240216ek2g0025u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
