\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It has really gotten out of hand\u8217\'20\'19: wage theft rampant in US construction\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,705 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 February 2024\par 12:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US regulators are cracking down on business practices that mean workers are not paid their due, a problem exacerbated in building industry by use of labor broker system\par \par \uc2 For years, Cristian C\u233\'00\'E9spedes worked as a foreman for a construction contractor, Unforgettable Coatings, in Las Vegas, where he and his co-workers often worked over 60 hours a week without overtime pay.\par \par \uc2 He recalls having to tell workers who were injured on the job that they had to deal with the injury and pay for medical care themselves. He knew the treatment and conditions imposed on himself and his co-workers were wrong, but he didn\u8217\'20\'19t know he could do anything about it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI always knew stuff was wrong. I just didn\u8217\'20\'19t know where to go to seek help,\u8221\'20\'1D said C\u233\'00\'E9spedes, who recently became a union organizer and said a wage theft investigation was kicked off after workers started meeting with local union organizers and learning about their rights.\par \par \uc2 Wage theft is a pervasive problem facing workers throughout the US. According to a 2014 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/epidemic-wage-theft-costing-workers-hundreds/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , workers lose over $50bn a year to wage theft from employers. Wage theft includes tactics from stiffing workers on pay, failing to properly pay workers for overtime, minimum wage violations, misclassification of employees as independent contractors, and not providing workers with mandated break times.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe didn\u8217\'20\'19t know our rights or anything like that. We were working over 60 hours a week and not getting paid overtime, and here in Nevada over 40 hours is overtime, so we would work 60 to 80 hours a week and sometimes we had to work for free on Sundays just to finish up a project,\u8221\'20\'1D said C\u233\'00\'E9spedes.\par \par \uc2 US regulators are cracking down on wage theft. A 2013 investigation by the Department of Labor recovered $47,393 in unpaid overtime wages to 21 workers in Utah. Last year, the department ordered Unforgettable Coatings and its owner Cory Summerhays to pay 593 workers over $3.6m in stolen wages, damages, interest and penalties.\par \par \uc2 A reasonable employer might try to avoid repeating a bad and costly decision,\u8221\'20\'1D the department noted, but Summerhays \u8220\'20\'1Cchose instead to double down, and then some.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20230130-0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investigation}}}\uc2 , Unforgettable Coatings falsified employment records to deprive workers of overtime pay, intimidated workers from speaking up about the practices and forced workers to volunteer to work on weekends without pay. The intimidation included threatening to call immigration services on workers and reducing workers\u8217\'20\'19 hours and pay if they were suspected to be cooperating with the department\u8217\'20\'19s investigation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEmotionally, it took a toll on me. I didn\u8217\'20\'19t know that because I was an immigrant, my employer was allowed to take advantage of me and my co-workers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Samuel Castillo, one of the workers at Unforgettable Coatings who received backpay as a result of the investigation. He now works in the construction industry as a union member, where he says the differences are stark compared with the low pay, lack of benefits, and working conditions he previously worked under.\par \par \uc2 The department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blog.dol.gov/2023/02/08/a-few-brave-workers-spoke-up-now-hundreds-are-receiving-36-million-in-back-pay"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 credited}}}\uc2  workers who spoke up and assisted in the investigation despite the threats and intimidation they received from their employer, including C\u233\'00\'E9spedes, who has since joined the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.iupat.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 International Union of Painters and Allied Trades}}}\uc2  (IUPAT) as a member and union organizer working to organize other workers throughout the construction industry who are being subjected to similar systematic labor abuses and wage theft.\par \par \uc2 The fines don\u8217\'20\'19t seem to have deterred others. IUPAT is currently assisting workers in holding another local construction company accountable for wage theft and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/28-CA-322060"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 retaliating }}}\uc2  against workers, Spectrum Construction.\par \par \uc2 Carlos Funez worked at Spectrum Construction and was fired along with a few other co-workers after his employer found out they were meeting with IUPAT organizers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI noticed from co-workers that they were stealing our money. I would ask why aren\u8217\'20\'19t you saying anything, we\u8217\'20\'19re not getting paid overtime for working on weekends, there was no overtime being paid,\u8221\'20\'1D said Funez. \u8220\'20\'1CWe were asked why we were having those meetings, I said, well, our rights are being violated and the women are being discriminated against.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Veronica, another construction worker at Spectrum Construction who was fired after meeting with the union and requested to remain anonymous for fear of further retaliation, explained the firings took a toll on her and the other workers. The workers currently have pending charges filed with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  over the firings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI realized what was happening was wage theft and it was not right,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe company found out we were having meetings with the union, and they threatened to fire us. We kept having meetings and eventually they fired us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know it\u8217\'20\'19s kind of hard for most of these workers to come forward. Obviously in the past nothing has been done. That\u8217\'20\'19s not going to keep on continuing like that. That has to change in one way or another and I feel like this is the time,\u8221\'20\'1D added C\u233\'00\'E9spedes.\par \par \uc2 IUPAT has launched several campaigns aimed at organizing workers in the construction industry to not only unionize, but to push back against a trend of wage theft and misclassification that union organizers argue has been worsening in the construction industry, especially toward immigrant workers.\par \par \uc2 Shane Smith, general vice-president for organizing at IUPAT, said the system of construction contractors using labor brokers, who misclassify workers as independent contractors, driving down pay, benefits and working conditions had grown in recent years around the US, particularly in the south.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMore and more employers around the country are adopting this business model of misclassification and the utilization of this labor broker system. In the last six years in some parts of the country this is almost exclusively the business model,\u8221\'20\'1D said Smith. \u8220\'20\'1CBasically what employers do, they go out, find two to three labor brokers who are usually former employees who have connections in the immigrant community. They recruit folks, hire folks on 1099s, usually working them more than 40 hours a week.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 1099s are tax forms for independent contractors, where tax liabilities are shifted on to the individual from the employer.\par \par \uc2 He explained the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.agc.org/sites/default/files/Galleries/labor_member_files/PEOs%20&%20Labor%20Brokers%20in%20the%20Construction%20Industry.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 growing labor broker system}}}\uc2  often also takes advantage of the brokers, who aren\u8217\'20\'19t well informed about what they\u8217\'20\'19re getting into, or the amount of costs and labor required for the job sites they\u8217\'20\'19re put in charge of finding workers for. This system, Smith said, allows employers to get away with not paying overtime wages, taxes, avoids unionization efforts and disenfranchises employers who do things the right way.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt has really gotten out of hand,\u8221\'20\'1D said Smith, who said they had seen multiple cases where immigrant workers on the same job sites were being funneled to working as independent contractors through labor brokers with construction workers, who are white, being directly paid as employees for the same work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s worrisome to me that if someone robs a bank, they go to jail for 20 years, for instance, but if someone robs millions of dollars from workers, they get nothing but have to pay restitution and usually they settle for a quarter of what they actually steal and its mind-boggling to me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For victims of wage theft, recovering lost wages can take years as enforcement agencies at the state and federal level {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nelp.org/wp-content/uploads/Workers-Lose-Billions-Unpaid-Wages-Every-Year.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lack the funding and resources}}}\uc2  to pursue investigations and the consequences for employers are minimal. And just a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/wage-theft-2021/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  small portion}}}\uc2  of the amount of wages stolen from workers by employers is ever recovered.\par \par \uc2 Charles Jones has worked in construction for years but in 2023 started working for a glazier contractor called Fredericksburg Glass in Virginia. Glaziers are skilled construction workers who cut and install glass, metal and other hardware in buildings.\par \par \uc2 Jones discovered in speaking with local union organizers at IUPAT district council 51 that he and his co-workers were victims of wage theft as they were not being paid any overtime or given any breaks while working 10-to-12-hour shifts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was under the notion I was with a company that wanted to see me achieve, grow, accomplish and secure financial stability. Then to find out that I was essentially being robbed by those who I trusted, those who I intentionally helped to build their company, it was devastating. I was let down,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jones.\par \par \uc2 Rolando Bravo Tello also worked at Fredericksburg Glass, and had been involved in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iEvmgI52pUI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unionization effort }}}\uc2  last year aimed at pushing back against wage theft issues at the company. Workers were not being paid for the full time they were working at job sites, were not being paid overtime, and were being misclassified as independent contractors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I found out that they were not paying me what they were supposed to pay me and other people were getting paid more and I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t, I felt like it was a knife to the back to say the least,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bravo Tello. \u8220\'20\'1CI was afraid of speaking up or saying something because I didn\u8217\'20\'19t think I would gain anything out of it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He and Jones recently received a wage theft settlement of $34,000 from Fredericksburg Glass and now work as union members in the construction industry, but union organizers argue their case just scratches the surface of the rampant issues facing workers in the construction industry as numerous {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/05-CA-332292"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 charges}}}\uc2  filed with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  involving allegations of retaliation and intimidation related to the union drive and wage theft investigation are still open.\par \par \uc2 Unforgettable Coatings and Cory Summerhays, Spectrum Construction and Fredericksburg Glass did not respond to multiple requests for comment on this story.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are companies out there where their main business is exploiting workers for their lack of education, immigration status, or lack of knowledge of labor laws,\u8221\'20\'1D said Herbert Zaldivar, organizing director for IUPAT district council 51. \u8220\'20\'1CThe resources are out there but trying to get workers to come forward because the penalties aren\u8217\'20\'19t severe or the companies are doing it by structure, it\u8217\'20\'19s not there yet. The law needs to go harder. It\u8217\'20\'19s happening everywhere.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240214ek2e001jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
