\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California fast-food workers launch new union: \u8216\'20\'18They have to listen to us\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 822 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 February 2024\par 17:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Move builds on victory in late 2023 to boost pay of more than half a million workers in sector to $20 an hour\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of fast-food workers met in Los Angeles on Friday to kick off the launch of the California Fast Food Workers Union, a statewide union aimed at organizing cooks and cashiers at fast-food companies across the state.\par \par \uc2 The new union comes in the wake of a victory at the end of 2023 to boost pay for California\u8217\'20\'19s more than 500,000-strong fast-food workforce to $20 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Fast-food workers with the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Fight for $15 and a Union movement led a legislative effort to get a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/business/2022/12/14/california-fast-food-law-blocked-voters-decide-2024/10891862002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bill}}}\uc2  passed in California to raise minimum wages for workers in the fast-food industry and create a fast-food sector council with worker representation in September 2022.\par \par \uc2 The fast-food industry responded by blocking the measure from going into effect by gathering signatures for a 2024 ballot initiative to repeal the law. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://calmatters.org/politics/2023/09/california-fast-food-deal/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 settlement }}}\uc2  was reached between labor and industry groups in September 2023 to avoid what was anticipated to be a very expensive, hotly contested ballot measure in November 2024.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2023-09-11/labor-business-strike-deal-to-end-referendum-over-landmark-california-fast-food-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 settlement}}}\uc2  included raising the minimum wage for fast-food workers at chains with more than 60 locations to $20 an hour in California, which is set to go into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.ca.gov/2023/09/28/california-increases-minimum-wage-protections-for-fast-food-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 effect }}}\uc2  on 1 April this year.\par \par \uc2 The formation of the California Fast Food Workers Union is the next step for the Fight for $15 movement in the state, after more than a decade of efforts and hundreds of strikes aimed at raising wages and improving conditions for workers in the fast-food industry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/18/california-labor-law-fast-food-industry-referendum"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 These efforts}}}\uc2  have been led in California over the last decade by primarily Black and Latino cooks and cashiers who have been fighting and have been able to show when they come together what kind of power they can have to really take on massive corporations like \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  and \uc2 Burger King\uc2 , which have done everything to crush their workers and crush the idea of them pulling together a union,\u8221\'20\'1D said Joseph Bryant, international executive vice-president of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In California, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/californias-fast-recovery-act-is-a-victory-for-fast-food-workers-and-a-model-for-state-labor-policy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over 80%}}}\uc2  of fast-food workers are people of color and two-thirds are women. In recent years, workers at fast-food chains throughout the state have held strikes in response to poor workplace conditions including excessive heat exposure in the workplace, sexual harassment and retaliation against workers, child labor violations, and wage theft.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m very proud to say that we\u8217\'20\'19re going to have a union. This is like a dream come true for me to be able to say this,\u8221\'20\'1D said Angelica Hernandez, who has worked at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s for 19 years in Los Angeles, California. \u8220\'20\'1CMcDonald\u8217\'20\'19s and other brands like Carl\u8217\'20\'19s Jr, Chipotle \u8211\'20\'13 they have to listen to us. They\u8217\'20\'19ve been making millions and millions of dollars in profits, and they want to keep their employees working at minimum wage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hernandez said she herself had been a victim of wage theft and sexual harassment in her workplace, had faced issues including working with and around broken equipment, and had had to do the work of two to three people due to short staffing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very important for us to have a union, so we have some kind of support, so we have backup,\u8221\'20\'1D added Hernandez. \u8220\'20\'1CSo we have dignity in the workplace and so workers coming into the industry don\u8217\'20\'19t have to face all of the issues we\u8217\'20\'19ve encountered.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 July Monroe, who has worked at \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s for more than 10 years in Los Angeles, said she had experienced poor working conditions such as working without adequate air conditioning in the summer, and experiencing sporadic work hour cuts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you go to the \uc2 Burger King\uc2  and get a combo meal, you don\u8217\'20\'19t know what\u8217\'20\'19s going on with the people who are taking your order. It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter, they have to smile for you, even if they aren\u8217\'20\'19t getting their breaks,\u8221\'20\'1D said Monroe. \u8220\'20\'1CWe deserve more than $20 an hour. We employees do the work of three people while the boss is getting the salary of several people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Monroe argued the opposition to the Fight for $15 movement and legislative efforts from the fast-food industry and wealthy corporations were indicative of the power workers have in joining together, learning their rights at work, and speaking up to enforce them so abuses against workers aren\u8217\'20\'19t left unchallenged and allowed to perpetuate as standards in the industry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re starting to get scared of us because we\u8217\'20\'19re learning our rights and we\u8217\'20\'19re valuing ourselves,\u8221\'20\'1D added Monroe. \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t have to be afraid to have a seat at the table.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240209ek29002pa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
