\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US hotel workers still fighting for basic benefits after \u8216\'20\'18hot labor summer\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,024 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 January 2024\par 20:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of hotel workers in southern California have gone on strike over 100 times since last July, with some still fighting for new union contracts\par \par \uc2 Last year was a big year for the US labor movement with historic wins and gains for workers at UPS, automakers, and for writers and actors in Hollywood. But for thousands of hotel workers, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://boldlatina.com/majority-latina-los-angeles-hotel-workers-strike-for-better-wages-and-working-conditions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 majority }}}\uc2  of whom are Latina women from immigrant communities, their fight has continued into 2024 without garnering the same amount of public attention.\par \par \uc2 California\u8217\'20\'19s hotel workers have led {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/business/story/2023-06-09/southern-california-hotel-workers-approve-potential-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one of the biggest strike waves}}}\uc2  to ever hit the US hotel industry to secure wage increases they say they need to afford to live in the areas where they work. Their actions coincided with the \u8220\'20\'1Chot labor summer\u8221\'20\'1D that saw ultimately successful union campaigns from Hollywood writers, actors and others.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of hotel workers throughout southern California have gone on strike over 100 times since last July in demand of new union contracts with workers seeking historic wage increases, benefits, and protections commensurate with the intense workloads and issues hotel workers have experienced in the wake of the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 They have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thenation.com/article/economy/los-angeles-hotel-worker-strikes/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 utilized}}}\uc2  similar tactics to the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 successful \u8220\'20\'1CStand-Up\u8221\'20\'1D strike strategy \u8211\'20\'13 staggering short-term strike actions against targeted hotels rather than an all-out walkout.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-eight hotels throughout southern California have reached agreements with \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11 so far, but a handful of hotels are still holding out from agreeing to similar contracts, including the Doubletree DTLA, Doubletree San Pedro, Sheraton Park Anaheim, Holiday Inn LAX, Santa Monica Hampton Inn, Santa Monica Courtyard Marriott, Hilton Pasadena, the Blackstone-owned Aloft, Fairfield El Segundo, Hotel Maya, Hyatt Place Pasadena and the Hyatt Regency at Los Angeles international airport.\par \par \uc2 The workers, represented by \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11, have been engaging in picket protests, strikes and launching {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/UNITEHERE11/status/1736095667725181058"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  boycotts}}}\uc2  to pressure the remaining hotels to agree to new contracts with the workers.\par \par \uc2 Yesenia Reyes, a housekeeper at the Hyatt Regency for nearly 10 years and a single mother of six kids, works two full-time jobs. She said she is seeking improvements to wages and protections to compensate for the immense workloads she faces, workloads that worsened due to services being cut throughout the hotel industry during the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s been extremely difficult since the pandemic. They were no longer servicing the rooms every day, so after cleaning rooms that haven\u8217\'20\'19t been cleaned for three or four days, the workload gets very heavy and the hotels are still making money. Meanwhile our workload has been getting harder,\u8221\'20\'1D said Reyes. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re human, like everybody else, we have families we\u8217\'20\'19re trying to support and to take care of.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe picketing, everything we\u8217\'20\'19ve done, we want to deliver a message that what we\u8217\'20\'19re fighting for is fair and just. A lot of us rent, and rent keeps going up. We have landlords who sell the building then new owners come in and jack up the rent. All over the place, it\u8217\'20\'19s becoming increasingly impossible to live.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At least 18 workers have faced disciplinary action for picketing in December at the Hyatt Regency at Los Angeles international airport, according to \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11.\par \par \uc2 Ricardo Blanco has worked in the food and beverage department at the Hyatt Regency for nearly 24 years and was one of the workers written up for participating in a picket protest.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve always felt secure based on what I know, knowing my job, and knowing what I\u8217\'20\'19m asked to do. But right now I feel up in the air,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe message they\u8217\'20\'19re saying to us is if we keep on doing this, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to get fired,\u8221\'20\'1D said Blanco. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re just human beings looking for what is fair to be able to survive and make a living in California. We\u8217\'20\'19re just looking for respect and dignity, not intimidation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He noted that the hotel is owned by a different union, the pension fund for the Southwest Carpenters Union, and argued they should understand why the workers are pushing for retirement benefits and livable wage increases.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of my co-workers, including me, we\u8217\'20\'19re over 50, so we\u8217\'20\'19re really concerned and worried about our retirement plans. We\u8217\'20\'19re just getting old, but the company is not moving forward,\u8221\'20\'1D added Blanco. \u8220\'20\'1CInstead of getting answers, we get disciplinary actions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Several \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  locals and other labor groups signed a letter to the trustees of the Southwest Carpenters Pension Fund to take a leadership role in resolving the contract dispute with the hotel. The union and pension fund have not responded to the letter and did not respond to multiple requests for comment on this story.\par \par \uc2 Kurt Petersen, co-president of \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11, criticized the ownership and role of this hotel\u8217\'20\'19s public and union pension funds and how those funds have been used to deny workers at the Hyatt Regency fair wages and discipline workers for exercising their rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe idea that our money is being used not only to withhold a living wage from workers, but also to punish them for exercising their rights should not happen,\u8221\'20\'1D said Petersen.\par \par \uc2 He also emphasized the gains other hotel workers have received and the immense public support for the strikes. The details of the tentative agreements haven\u8217\'20\'19t been made public yet, but Petersen argued the contracts bring the city\u8217\'20\'19s hotel workers to a new level.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt has been a transformational campaign both for the industry and our members,\u8221\'20\'1D added Petersen. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not hyperbole to say that folks are more militant and determined today than when they walked out in July. I think the industry knows it. I fear they have lost a generation of employees, to their loyalty of any kind, because rather than listening to their employees\u8217\'20\'19 demand that they earn a wage that allows them to live in the city, they have fought.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hyatt\uc2  did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240127ek1r001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
