\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Dark forces\u8217\'20\'19: how US corporations turned to courts in fight against unions\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,852 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 January 2024\par 20:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Companies including \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  are using new legal playbook against unions, experts say\par \par \uc2 A multi-pronged legal attack under way by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , large corporations, business groups and anti-union litigators threatens to \u8220\'20\'1Craise havoc\u8221\'20\'1D with US labor law and hobble a resurgent labor movement, according to experts.\par \par \uc2 So far efforts to scale back or undermine workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights through the US courts have centered on the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) \u8211\'20\'13 the US top workplace watchdog and overseer of union elections. But other laws \u8211\'20\'13 including trademark and property rights statutes \u8211\'20\'13 are also being used.\par \par \uc2 Both Musk and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  are pursuing cases that would undermine the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Musk\u8217\'20\'19s company \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  filed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/science/2024/jan/04/spacex-countersues-ceo-elon-musk-nlrb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lawsuit}}}\uc2  championed by the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  and other conservative groups against the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  in January. The lawsuit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/technology/space/spacex-sues-us-agency-that-accused-it-firing-workers-critical-elon-musk-2024-01-04/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claims}}}\uc2  the board is unconstitutional because its members can only be removed for cause, not at will, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/daily-labor-report/spacexs-bid-to-upend-nlrb-follows-signals-from-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claims}}}\uc2  the board violates due process protections. The suit was filed in Texas by Musk\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lawcha.org/2021/02/02/12-facts-about-morgan-lewis-amazons-powerful-anti-union-law-firm/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 union avoidance law firm Morgan Lewis}}}\uc2  in response to a board complaint that \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  fired workers in retaliation for writing a letter over concerns about Musk\u8217\'20\'19s behavior.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is one of the most audacious and bold attempts to reverse the decisions of the 1930s and the way in which American labor law has operated for nearly 90 years,\u8221\'20\'1D said William Gould IV, Charles A Beardsley professor of law, emeritus, at Stanford Law School and chairman of the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  from 1994 to 1998. \u8220\'20\'1CThis would raise havoc with the effective administration of labor law in our country which is designed to be for the benefit of workers and the collective bargaining process.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is challenging the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ruling that the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest coffee chain must rehire seven fired union activists in Memphis, Tennessee. The board won an injunction ordering \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to reinstate the workers with backpay and a lower court ruled the firings probably discouraged other employees from exercising their rights under US labor law.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Workers United union has won elections at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/01/05/business/starbucks-union-workers-boycott.html#:~:text=More%20than%20two%20years%20into,workers%20and%20resisted%20contract%20negotiations."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 350 stores}}}\uc2  but has yet to agree a union contract at any location. \uc2 NLRB\uc2  regional offices and administrative law judges have found dozens of labor law violations committed by \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  since the union campaign began in late 2021.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  will now hear the case, which centers on whether the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has the right to ask courts to reinstate workers while litigation is ongoing \u8211\'20\'13 referred to as 10(j) injunctions. Unfair dismissal cases can take years to hear and the agency \u8211\'20\'13 which asked the supreme court not to take the case \u8211\'20\'13 has argued removing the power will have a chilling effect on union organizing.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/12/starbucks-supreme-court-fired-union-workers-case"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 praised}}}\uc2  the decision: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are pleased the supreme court has decided to consider our request to level the playing field for all US employers by ensuring that a single standard is applied as federal district courts determine whether to grant 10(j) injunctions pursued by the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesperson said in response to the decision.\par \par \uc2 Gould argued this case involves the most important tool that the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  currently has at its disposal to enforce the National Labor Relations Act (NLRA).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy labor board, when I was chairman in the 1990s, used this provision more than any other labor board in the entire 90-year history of the board. This is the most effective weapon we had,\u8221\'20\'1D Gould said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe ramifications are enormous,\u8221\'20\'1D he continued. \u8220\'20\'1CEveryone has recognized labor law reform is necessary to make the NLRA statute work. All Congress has given in the nature of labor law reform is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/what-we-do/investigate-charges/10j-injunctions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 section 10(j)}}}\uc2 . The effect of this is to make wrongdoers less concerned about engaging in misconduct because they will not be brought to justice in the foreseeable future. The only way that the statute can work effectively is to operate judiciously so that workers when confronted with a choice about whether to join a union or not, will not be faced with a choice that means that they will have to wait two, three, four years before their rights can be realized. Section 10(j) is a way of providing for effective enforcement of the statute and this will discourage, I\u8217\'20\'19m afraid, unionization, and will discourage workers who are interested in unionization.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Given the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative make-up, and recent decisions and opinions from some of the court\u8217\'20\'19s most rightwing judges, Gould warned that the justices will likely rule in \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s favor. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a court that is hostile to unions, working people, and also my belief is it\u8217\'20\'19s hostile [to] elected representatives altogether predicated upon the view that the unelected judges know more than the other branches of government,\u8221\'20\'1D he concluded.\par \par \uc2 But it is not just the watchdog that is under attack. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://seattle.eater.com/23923490/starbucks-workers-united-union-lawsuits-copyright-trademark-israel-hamas-palestine-social-media"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed}}}\uc2  a lawsuit late last year against Starbucks Workers United, alleging trademark infringement. Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s filed a similar lawsuit against the union Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s United, which a judge in California {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/judge-tosses-trader-joes-trademark-complaint-against-union-in-brutal-fashion_n_65a2eb39e4b07bd6950d029a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recently threw out}}}\uc2 . Medieval Times also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/judge-tosses-medieval-times-trademark-lawsuit-against-union_n_65182e08e4b00eb6fb38528c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed}}}\uc2  a lawsuit against its workers\u8217\'20\'19 union, claiming trademark infringement, which was also thrown out by a judge.\par \par \uc2 Seth Goldstein, a labor attorney representing Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s United, said the trademark suits were part of an \u8220\'20\'1Cemerging toolbox\u8221\'20\'1D of anti-union law firms \u8220\'20\'1Cwhere they are really using everything they have to try to stop the new organizing movement\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think that there are some dark forces here trying to stop the emerging organizing movement by perhaps throwing out the National Labor Relations Act, and the right of unions like Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  or Medieval Times to have their own identification because they know they are losing the union elections and more young people, gen Z and millennials, are attracted to unionizing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s said in a statement: \u8220\'20\'1CTrader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s consistently takes legal action to protect our brand when we become aware that someone other than Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s is selling merchandise using our trademarks and trade dress. We will continue to take all appropriate action to protect our brand.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cathy Creighton, director of \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Industrial and Labor Relations Buffalo Co-Lab and a former \uc2 NLRB\uc2  field attorney, said corporations have long used a number of tactics to prevent and dissuade their workers from organizing unions. These tactics have included firings, closing facilities, surveillance, offering increases to pay and benefits or withholding increases, refusing to bargain a first contract with units of workers who have unionized, and employing legal maneuvers to create delays and deplete the union\u8217\'20\'19s resources.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re trying to kill the movement. Corporate America is going full bore after the labor movement and that\u8217\'20\'19s what they\u8217\'20\'19re trying to do is to really annihilate them on every level that they can, including these legal maneuvers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Creighton. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a whole body of \uc2 NLRB\uc2  case law that it is a violation of federal labor law to sue as a means to prevent organizing and bargaining, so there will be \uc2 NLRB\uc2  charges brought in this regard if there haven\u8217\'20\'19t been already.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another increasingly popular tactic has been to argue that property rights supersede the rights of workers in workplaces, said David Muraskin, managing director for litigation at the legal advocacy group FarmStand.\par \par \uc2 Muraskin represented groups challenging a North Carolina state law that would have prevented whistleblowers from speaking with legislators, regulators or the media, a law that the North Carolina farm bureau federation defended in court. The \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/the-supreme-court-leaves-in-place-a-court-victory-for-peta-over-north-carolinas-undercover-gag-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined}}}\uc2  to hear an appeal of a lower court ruling that struck down four provisions of the law.\par \par \uc2 The North Carolina farm bureau did not return requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat the farm bureau and the state came in and said was, well, sure, there\u8217\'20\'19s a first amendment right to record and there\u8217\'20\'19s a first amendment right to speak. But all that is really trumped by the right to property. What\u8217\'20\'19s so shocking about that argument is there is no constitutional right to property,\u8221\'20\'1D said Muraskin. \u8220\'20\'1CI think this is a way in which the right has turned a weak argument into an anti-social justice tool across a variety of areas, and they\u8217\'20\'19re using it pretty aggressively.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/central-ny/politics/2023/10/03/farm-industry-asks-court-to-halt-enforcement-of-ny-collective-bargaining-rules"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://towardsjustice.org/litigation/pre-rulemaking-comments-on-overtime-rights-for-agricultural-workers-sb-87/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colorado}}}\uc2 , agricultural business groups are fighting against legislation that ensures farm workers have access to essential services such as legal representation or healthcare, with business groups arguing that their property rights supersede the law.\par \par \uc2 Colorado passed the Agricultural Workers\u8217\'20\'19 Rights bill in 2021, which would ensure farm workers can access essential service providers while on breaks. The Colorado Livestock Association and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.coloradofarmbureau.com/2022/09/19/colorado-farm-ranch-owners-file-suit-contesting-senate-bill-21-087/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a group of farm owners and ranchers}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://investigatemidwest.org/2023/09/21/a-new-network-of-attorneys-seeks-to-defend-abuses-of-industrial-agriculture-first-up-colorado/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed }}}\uc2  lawsuits to halt the law from being implemented, claiming the provision in the law violates employers\u8217\'20\'19 rights to exclude people from their property.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat the Colorado Livestock Association is doing in Colorado to try to weaponize the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s more business-friendly precedent is happening across the country,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kelsey Eberly, a senior attorney with FarmStand who is representing farm workers in an attempt to dismiss the lawsuit opposing the bill.\par \par \uc2 Jenifer Rodriguez, managing attorney of the Colorado Legal Services Migrant Farm Worker Division, argued opposition to the Colorado law is based on outdated precedents as US labor laws passed in the early 20th century excluded farm workers and domestic workers from basic protections that are afforded to other US workers.\par \par \uc2 She said laws placing the owners\u8217\'20\'19 interests and their property rights over the rights of farmworkers were \u8220\'20\'1Cbased on laws that were originally designed to exclude Black people and immigrants that apply to other working people\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Farm workers often work in isolated rural areas, and during harvest seasons do not have the time or means of transportation to access basic services, said Rodriguez. She also dismissed arguments against the law given property owners under the law are allowed to develop and enforce rules for visitors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery person regardless of where they come from, what kind of work they do, or where they live, should have access to fundamental services like healthcare, education and legal help,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rodriguez.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the Colorado Livestock Association said in an email on the business group\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to block the law, \u8220\'20\'1Csince the enactment of Senate Bill 21-087, there has been confusion and instances of individuals entering private property and businesses under the auspices of providing services to those employed on-premises. Colorado Livestock Association (CLA), along with others, has challenged through litigation the unlawful trespass by those posing as service providers for employees on private property as a part of these new labor laws. Additionally, CLA challenged the constitutionality of this provision in law. Given the nature of takings and the application of laws in this pretense, CLA believes compensation should be required by the enacting government entity. The real question is why Agribusinesses are not to be afforded the same reasonable expectation of private property as any other employment entity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240126ek1q001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
