\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 New York Daily News and Forbes staffers walk out amid US media job cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 Martin Pengelly \par \uc2 1,060 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 January 2024\par 15:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Staff at New York Daily News walk out in protest of \u8216\'20\'18chronic cuts\u8217\'20\'19, while Forbes staff protest lack of progress in bargaining talks\par \par \uc2 Staff at the New York Daily News and Forbes walked out on Thursday, adding to growing unease in the US newspaper industry in a week when the \uc2 Los Angeles Times{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2024/jan/23/los-angeles-times-layoffs-journalists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fired}}}\uc2  more than 100 journalists.\par \par \uc2 Mike Gartland, a city hall reporter and Daily News union steward, targeted owner \uc2 Alden Global Capital\uc2  with a reference to one of the paper\u8217\'20\'19s most famous headlines, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theguardian.newspapers.com/article/daily-news-ford-to-city-drop-dead/127089505/?locale=en-US"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ford to City: Drop Dead}}}\uc2 , a 1975 response to President \uc2 Gerald Ford\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s refusal to offer New York a financial bailout.\par \par \uc2 Gartland {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/michaelgartland/status/1750511393034346801"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CAlden to News: Drop Dead. Defend the Daily News!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Once home to legendary reporters including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/aug/18/pete-hamill-obituary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pete Hamill}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pulitzer.org/winners/jimmy-breslin"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jimmy Breslin}}}\uc2 , the paper is a shadow of its former self.\par \par \uc2 The Daily News \u8220\'20\'1Conce boasted the \u8216\'20\'18largest circulation in America\u8217\'20\'19 and was 4,000 employees strong,\u8221\'20\'1D the union said. But \u8220\'20\'1Cby the year 2000, circulation dropped by nearly half and the publication underwent several rounds of devastating job cuts. Since the spring of 2022, 27 people have left and the [News] Guild staff now totals 54 people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Citing changes to overtime policy and \u8220\'20\'1Cchronic cuts\u8221\'20\'1D as fuel for the first Daily News walkout since 1991 \u8211\'20\'13 the year the paper was bought by the British press baron Robert Maxwell \u8211\'20\'13 staffers planned a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nyguild.org/post/new-york-needs-the-daily-news-unionized-journalists-walk-off-the-job-in-one-day-protest-over-hedge-fund-owners-slashing-of-resou"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 24-hour}}}\uc2  operation. Protesters rallied {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SheridanWriter/status/1750555865902358720"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outside}}}\uc2  the Broadway building where the paper uses a co-working space, having closed its Manhattan newsroom in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Alden, a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/05/21/998730863/vulture-fund-alden-global-known-for-slashing-newsrooms-buys-tribune-papers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vulture}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D fund known for slashing newspaper budgets in search of profits, bought the company that owned the Daily News the following year.\par \par \uc2 Today, the New York Daily News Union is walking off the job for 24 hours. ??????\par \par \uc2 We are fed up with \uc2 Alden Global Capital\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s constant cuts and apparent commitment to shrinking the paper.\par \par \uc2 Here are some reasons we\u8217\'20\'19re taking a stand: ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/defendtheNYDN?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #defendtheNYDN}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Nv0heE4j6i"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Nv0heE4j6i}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 Michael Gartland (@michaelgartland) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/michaelgartland/status/1750511393034346801?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 25, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlden wants to act as if we are not being chiseled,\u8221\'20\'1D Gartland said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not going to engage in that intellectual dishonesty. In reality, we\u8217\'20\'19re being crushed for cash. As a result, staff is diminished, which means our ability to cover the city is diminished. We believe this is bad for New York.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ellen Moynihan, a metropolitan desk reporter, said: \u8220\'20\'1CEveryone I work with cares so much about this paper and this city, but Alden only cares about extracting money instead of investing in us. Reporting and producing a newspaper is not a job that fits neatly into an eight-hour box, and if we are not allowed to report the news as it happens, stories go untold.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Alden did not immediately comment.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Forbes\uc2 , a financial news outlet based across the Hudson River in Jersey City but owned by an investment company from Hong Kong, employees went on strike for the first time to protest lack of progress in bargaining talks, two years since forming a union.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/andreamurphy/archive/?sh=272e46d35961"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Andrea Murphy}}}\uc2 , a reporter and the Forbes union chair, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-01-25/forbes-staff-begin-3-day-strike-citing-union-busting-contract-terms-like-rto?sref=fqqmZ8gi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told Bloomberg}}}\uc2  that managers were \u8220\'20\'1Cdragging their feet and slow-walking this whole process. We\u8217\'20\'19re hoping [the strike] shows them that we are serious.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The walkout was planned to last three days. \uc2 Forbes\uc2  said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cworking diligently\u8221\'20\'1D with the union but \u8220\'20\'1Cdisappointed by the decision to stage a walkout\u8221\'20\'1D, though it respected the right to do so.\par \par \uc2 As an election year gathers pace, with \uc2 Donald Trump{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2024/jan/25/second-trump-presidency-dangers-democracy?utm_term=Autofeed&CMP=edit_2221&utm_medium=Social&utm_source=Twitter#Echobox=1706184476"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poised}}}\uc2  to win the Republican presidential nomination again, national and local US newspapers are experiencing increasing staff unrest and major cutbacks.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Sports Illustrated employees received an email saying many of them would be laid off in the coming days. On Tuesday, staffers at the \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2  were told the company was laying off at least 115 people \u8211\'20\'13 roughly a quarter of its staff.\par \par \uc2 In December, the \uc2 Washington Post{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2023/12/19/washington-post-cut-jobs-voluntary-buyouts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  it had met a target of 240 voluntary job cuts \u8211\'20\'13 notwithstanding its ownership by \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , one of the richest men in the world. A month earlier, in November, Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast announced it would be laying off a whopping 5% of its work force \u8211\'20\'13 equal to roughly 270 employees.\par \par \uc2 This week, more than 400 staffers at Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast, which is the parent company of Vanity Fair, Vogue, GQ and more, took part in a 24-hour work stoppage after the company revised the plan, according to CNN, and laid off 94 unionized members \u8211\'20\'13 20% of the Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast Union. However, Cond\u233\'00\'E9 Nast\u8217\'20\'19s director of corporate communications says the organization \u8220\'20\'1Cdidn\u8217\'20\'19t revise its plan \u8211\'20\'13 the 94 union members were a part of the originally announced 5% layoff\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, staff at the Baltimore Sun were left \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global/commentisfree/2024/jan/18/local-newspapers-media-chain-ownership-baltimore-sun-sinclair"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aghast}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D when it was bought by \uc2 David D Smith\uc2  of Sinclair, a conservative media company with a track record of enforcing editorial control.\par \par \uc2 Margaret Sullivan, a former senior editor at the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  and the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/culture/archive/2020/07/ghosting-news-margaret-sullivans-alarm-bell/614011/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ghosting the News}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2022/oct/16/newsroom-confidential-review-margaret-sullivan-new-york-times-washington-post"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Newsroom Confidential}}}\uc2  and now a Guardian columnist, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global/commentisfree/2024/jan/18/local-newspapers-media-chain-ownership-baltimore-sun-sinclair"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote}}}\uc2  : \u8220\'20\'1CAlden, reviled in the industry for strip-mining newspapers all over the country, was the Sun\u8217\'20\'19s most recent owner. The once-robust staff has shrunk to well under 100.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut it turns out that Alden has something going for it: its executives don\u8217\'20\'19t care about the content. That\u8217\'20\'19s terrible, on one hand, because they ruthlessly slash the number of journalists in order to maximise profit. But they generally don\u8217\'20\'19t impose their politics.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, speaking to the Guardian from the Daily News rally in Manhattan, Gartland said more than 95% of union members had turned out. After pausing to explain the strike to a group of passing kids, he summed up the role the Daily News has long played in New York life.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor a lot of people here, this is like our dream job. And to me, I have an emotional attachment to this paper. I grew up with this paper. This has traditionally been the paper of working people in New York City. The thing that\u8217\'20\'19s so good about this job is we get paid to talk to interesting people and we get paid to serve our readers, who need information to make decisions about their lives.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240125ek1p002sa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
