\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 LA Times fires 115 journalists in \u8216\'20\'18HR Zoom webinar\u8217\'20\'19 following union protests\b0\par\par\uc2 Lois Beckett \par \uc2 1,723 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2024\par 17:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Young journalists of color \u8216\'20\'18disproportionately affected\u8217\'20\'19, with many Black, Asian American and Latino staffers laid off, Times guild says\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2  said it was laying off 115 journalists \u8211\'20\'13 or more than 20% of its newsroom \u8211\'20\'13 the day after members of Congress warned in a letter that sweeping media layoffs could undermine democracy in a high-stakes election year.\par \par \uc2 The 142-year-old newspaper, which has one of the largest print circulations in the US, has also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2024-01-09/kevin-merida-steps-down-los-angeles-times"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost its executive editor}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2024-01-22/la-times-turmoil-sara-yasin-letter-congressional-democrats"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 managing editor}}}\uc2  in recent weeks. On Friday, the union representing the newsroom\u8217\'20\'19s journalists held an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/los-angeles-times-guild-walkout-layoffs-41f073cc8511f1c8c3c3a78d656b29df"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unprecedented day-long walkout}}}\uc2 , urging Dr \uc2 Patrick Soon-Shiong\uc2 , the paper\u8217\'20\'19s billionaire owner, to reconsider a planned staff reduction in response to the paper\u8217\'20\'19s struggling finances.\par \par \uc2 Young journalists of color were \u8220\'20\'1Cdisproportionately affected\u8221\'20\'1D by the layoffs, the Los Angeles Times Guild said in a statement, with many Black, Asian American, and Latino staffers losing their jobs, despite the Soon-Shiong family\u8217\'20\'19s public commitment in 2020 to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/opinion/story/2020-09-27/los-angeles-times-reckoning-on-race"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 diversity in the paper\u8217\'20\'19s staff}}}\uc2 , which it said \u8220\'20\'1Chas never truly reflected the region\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Matt Pearce, the president of Media Guild of the West, which represents the Times\u8217\'20\'19 unionized journalists, wrote on X that 94 union members had been notified on Tuesday that they were being laid off, about a quarter of the union\u8217\'20\'19s membership, but a smaller number than the union had originally anticipated. \u8220\'20\'1CWe believe our decision to go on strike saved scores of newsroom jobs today,\u8221\'20\'1D the Los Angeles Times Guild said in a statement, citing an email from the company\u8217\'20\'19s president and COO.\par \par \uc2 Statement from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/latguild?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @latguild}}}\uc2  on today's mass layoffs at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/latimes?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @latimes}}}\uc2  : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/uRPWcMJqpf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/uRPWcMJqpf}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 L.A. Times Guild ?? (@latguild) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/latguild/status/1749917120333541473?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 23, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 The LA Times layoffs appear to have affected a wide swathe of the paper\u8217\'20\'19s reporters, editors, and managers. These included journalists and editors working on more digitally focused initiatives for younger audiences, such as the paper\u8217\'20\'19s new \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/about/pressreleases/story/2022-06-01/this-is-not-an-error-404-by-la-times"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 meme team}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D that had worked on TikTok content, prominent culture writers and those leading its national political coverage. Among the journalists laid off on Tuesday were the paper\u8217\'20\'19s Pulitzer prize-winning DC bureau chief, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Kimbriell/status/1749847329086058608"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kimbriell Kelly}}}\uc2 , and its deputy DC bureau chief, Nick Baumann, who received their layoff notices on Tuesday, the day of the New Hampshire presidential primary election.\par \par \uc2 My team at De Los was gutted. I can't find the words to express how much I feel for them and the rest of my {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/latimes?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @latimes}}}\uc2  colleagues. For many, working here was THE dream job; others, uprooted their lives. For all of us, it's a privilege to tell stories that reflect our communities\u8212\'20\'14 Jessica Perez (@JessicaPerezLA2) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JessicaPerezLA2/status/1749901364581581257?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 23, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Among the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JessicaPerezLA2/status/1749901364581581257"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  journalists laid off}}}\uc2  was one of the paper\u8217\'20\'19s only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/angiejaime/status/1749854087074730460"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Indigenous editors}}}\uc2 , and many of those working on the paper\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/about/story/2023-06-01/de-los-everything-latinidad"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newly launched De Los section}}}\uc2 , designed by and for Latinos, who make up roughly half of Los Angeles county\u8217\'20\'19s population, and who have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/opinion/story/2020-09-27/los-angeles-times-reckoning-on-race"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 historically underrepresented}}}\uc2  among the paper\u8217\'20\'19s staff.\par \par \uc2 From what I can gather, it is sad to see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/delosLAtimes?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @delosLAtimes}}}\uc2  not even get the chance to grow. I strongly believe that the future of journalism is young and Latino. Immediate returns won't happen now, but they will years from now. The runway for experimentation no longer exists.\u8212\'20\'14 Julio Ricardo Varela (@julito77) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/julito77/status/1749902727071596847?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 23, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 The scale and the impact of the layoffs sent ripples throughout Los Angeles media circles: \u8220\'20\'1CMy whole timeline is literally journalists of color getting let go,\u8221\'20\'1D one {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Tomas_Mier/status/1749857885671379098"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rolling Stone journalist}}}\uc2  wrote on X.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve been laid off from my job as a columnist for the LA Times today, with 100 of my best colleagues. It\u8217\'20\'19s a dark day. I was the only Latina columnist for the opinion desk. Trump is looming & I\u8217\'20\'19m author of Hatemonger. If you're hiring, I\u8217\'20\'19m at jeangue619@gmail.com\u8212\'20\'14 Jean Guerrero (@jeanguerre) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jeanguerre/status/1749860647339479424?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 23, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 LA Times journalists who lost their jobs, and those who retained them, continued to publicly criticize what they described as a chaotic and poorly managed process.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe LA Times laid us off in an HR zoom webinar with chat disabled, no q&a, no chance to ask questions,\u8221\'20\'1D one former news editor wrote on X, a characterization the union confirmed in its statement.\par \par \uc2 Voluntary buyouts, like the ones the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  did last year, \u8220\'20\'1Ccould have helped prevent this, but that\u8217\'20\'19s not the path the company chose\u8221\'20\'1D, the union said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CVoluntary buyouts \u8230\'20\'26 could\u8217\'20\'19ve incentivized more senior staff members (who are disproportionately white) to step up and take the place of younger staff members (who are disproportionately POCs and more likely to get laid off). During our summer layoffs, buyouts went a long way toward reducing the racial impact of the cuts,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brian Contreras, the chair of the LA Times Guild unit council.\par \par \uc2 Both a spokesperson for the paper\u8217\'20\'19s management and the union said that a subsequent buyout process required by the paper\u8217\'20\'19s union contract could help offset the cuts over the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the Times did not have additional comment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe economic reality of our organization is extremely challenging. Despite our owner\u8217\'20\'19s willingness to continue to invest, we need to take immediate steps to improve our cash position,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Chris Argentieri\uc2 , the paper\u8217\'20\'19s president and COO, wrote in an email to employees on Tuesday, announcing \u8220\'20\'1Ca significant reduction\u8221\'20\'1D in staff.\par \par \uc2 The union\u8217\'20\'19s buyout plan was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot financially feasible\u8221\'20\'1D, Argentieri wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is important to emphasize that these changes are not a reflection of the hard work and dedication shown by any of our colleagues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The journalists\u8217\'20\'19 union blamed \u8220\'20\'1Cyears of middling strategy, the absence of a publisher, and no clear direction\u8221\'20\'1D for the layoffs, and wrote: \u8220\'20\'1CIt is still unclear who is in charge of our newsroom more than a week after our executive editor resigned.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While saying that they \u8220\'20\'1Cremain grateful for the Soon-Shiong family\u8217\'20\'19s investment in the newspaper\u8221\'20\'1D, the journalists also wrote: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s clear that those entrusted to steward his family\u8217\'20\'19s largesse have failed him \u8211\'20\'13 not the rank-and-file staff members with no say in editorial priorities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ll let people make their own individual announcements, but yes the LA Times Washington bureau was decimated. They haven\u8217\'20\'19t been filling jobs for two years now and that reduced number was cut even more today. There are five reporters left covering DC {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/wMb5Gxjk3S"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/wMb5Gxjk3S}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 Sarah D. Wire (@sarahdwire) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sarahdwire/status/1749879876553761146?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 23, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 The latest round of layoffs comes after more than 70 positions \u8211\'20\'13 about 13% of the newsroom \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/los-angeles-times-newspaper-layoffs-58495c97f70c577a5f61adc692d82ca4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were slashed last June}}}\uc2 , the Associated Press reported.\par \par \uc2 Soon-Shiong, a biotech billionaire, acquired the LA Times and other local papers in 2018, and had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2018/jul/21/los-angeles-times-new-owner-patrick-soon-shiong-interview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pledged to make his newspaper}}}\uc2  one of the \u8220\'20\'1Cbastions of democracy\u8221\'20\'1D and to fight fake news, which he called the \u8220\'20\'1Ccancer of our time\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Soon-Shiong {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2024-01-23/latimes-layoffs-115-newsroom-soon-shiong"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the LA Times}}}\uc2  that the paper was losing $30 to $40m a year and that it was not making enough progress in growing its readership and advertising revenue in order to become financially stable. \uc2 Forbes\uc2  estimates Soon-Shiong\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/profile/patrick-soon-shiong/?sh=6da61bcb1fee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 net worth at $5.4bn}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ten Democratic members of Congress who represent California had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mattdpearce/status/1749448807660831149/photo/1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 written Soon-Shiong a letter}}}\uc2  on Monday, saying that they were \u8220\'20\'1Cconcerned about reports of potential layoffs facing the LA Times newsroom and the effect this will have on all Angelenos, the availability of essential news and the strength of our democracy at large\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Responding to these comments on , Soon-Shiong said that US lawmakers should be doing more to support the struggling media industry, pointing to legislation in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/feb/16/google-and-facebook-the-landmark-australian-law-that-will-make-them-pay-for-news-content"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/canada-keep-pressure-facebook-pay-news-trudeau-says-2023-12-15/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Canada}}}\uc2  that forces companies like \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  to pay for news content shared on their platforms, as tech giants have captured wide swathes of the advertising market that used to sustain news publications.\par \par \uc2 Well, unfortunately I'm among those who were laid off by LAT today, among many other brilliant and dedicated colleagues both inside and outside our union. If you're looking for a tech reporter with experience on the AI or social media beats, I'm at briancontreras42 'at' gmail\u8212\'20\'14 Brian Contreras (@_B_Contreras_) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/_B_Contreras_/status/1749869242495762668?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 23, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19d like to put the question to [lawmakers]: what can they do to help preserve a free and robust press, one that is instrumental in upholding our democracy?\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The LA Times layoffs follow months of firings at media outlets across the US. A report released in early December 2023 found that the US news industry had lost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.poynter.org/commentary/2023/media-industry-cuts-top-20000-in-2023-report-finds/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2,681 jobs through November}}}\uc2 , a higher number than in previous years.\par \par \uc2 In comments on Monday and Tuesday, Soon-Shiong said that over the past five years since he had acquired the LA Times, he had invested \u8220\'20\'1Chundreds of millions of dollars \u8211\'20\'13 approaching $1 billion\u8221\'20\'1D in sustaining it.\par \par \uc2 During the tumultuous years of the Covid-19 pandemic, the paper had previously avoided significant layoffs despite \u8220\'20\'1Closses that surpassed $100m in operational and capital expenses\u8221\'20\'1D, Soon-Shiong told the LA Times\u8217\'20\'19 media reporter.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Soon-Shiong pushed back on descriptions of what his own newspaper had characterized as \u8220\'20\'1CLA Times Turmoil\u8221\'20\'1D in a headline.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are not in turmoil. We have a real plan,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur owner has publicly said he has a plan for moving forward but has not shared it with any of us,\u8221\'20\'1D the journalists\u8217\'20\'19 union said in its statement on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In advance of major presidential election year, several billionaire-owned US news publications have made significant staff layoffs. The union for journalists at Time Magazine, which is owned by the tech billionaire \uc2 Marc Benioff\uc2 , said that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/timemagunion/status/1749881396384907295"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 15% of their journalists had been laid off}}}\uc2 . The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , owned by the Amazon founder, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , announced last year that it needed to lay off 240 employees, leading to a round of buyouts that saw high-profile journalists and editors depart the paper. Other major news outlets, from National Public Radio to Vice News to Sports Illustrated, have seen substantial layoffs and staff reductions.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 24 January 2023 to correct the date the \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2  journalists were laid off.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240123ek1n003bj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
