\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Constantly monitored\u8217\'20\'19: the pushback against AI surveillance at work\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,231 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 January 2024\par 12:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Academics decry algorithmic monitoring of workers and call for stronger standards \u8211\'20\'13 but US unions have been slow to act\par \par \uc2 From algorithms firing staff without human intervention to software keeping tabs on bathroom breaks, technologies including artificial intelligence are already upsetting workers and unsettling workplaces.\par \par \uc2 At call centers, AI systems record and grade how workers handle calls, often giving failing grades for not sticking to the script. Some corporate software spies on workers to see whether they ever write the word \u8220\'20\'1Cunion\u8221\'20\'1D in their emails.\par \par \uc2 As technologies grow ever more sophisticated in monitoring, surveilling and speeding up workers, many workplace experts say US businesses, labor unions and government are not doing nearly enough to protect workers from tech\u8217\'20\'19s downsides.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers are being constantly monitored, and AI-based monitoring tools can make mistakes that can translate into unfair pay cuts or firings,\u8221\'20\'1D said Virginia Doellgast, a professor of employment relations at Cornell. \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers often don\u8217\'20\'19t know what monitoring tools are being used, what data the tools are collecting or how that data is used to evaluate their performance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Europe, unlike in the US and Canada, many unions have been pushing for years for protections against some of the more intrusive ways that AI tools track and manage workers. \u8220\'20\'1CThis issue has yet to be put at the center of the radar for unions in North America,\u8221\'20\'1D said Valerio De Stefano, a labor law professor at \uc2 York University in Toronto\uc2 , who has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/10242589231157656"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 written extensively}}}\uc2  on AI\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/10242589221141055"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  use in the workplace}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CUnions in Europe are more aware of the uses of technologies from the surveillance standpoint. This is not something that unions in North America have focused on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At some German companies, labor experts say, workers have won protections that could become models for US and Canadian workers. At \uc2 Deutsche Telekom\uc2 , Germany\u8217\'20\'19s largest telecommunications company, workers have won a prohibition against algorithms firing workers without any human involvement as well as a ban on using data collected by digital monitoring to discipline or dismiss individual workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn Europe, workers have stronger rights to obtain information and participate in decision-making,\u8221\'20\'1D Doellgast said. \u8220\'20\'1CIn the US, where there is a union present, workers have some information rights about AI and hopefully a voice in how it\u8217\'20\'19s used. Where unions aren\u8217\'20\'19t present, workers have no information rights, and all they see is the effects of the technologies on them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mindful of the downsides of AI and algorithmic management, US labor unions are starting to push harder for protections. For instance, at some call centers, the \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2  union has won not only requirements that managers notify workers whenever recording their calls, but also guarantees that management will only record calls for training purposes to help improve employee performance \u8211\'20\'13 and not for evaluating or disciplining workers.\par \par \uc2 Dan Reynolds, the Communications Workers\u8217\'20\'19 assistant research director, said the union has long been concerned about how new technologies affect jobs. \u8220\'20\'1CAI is a new technology often used to speed up the work, deskill the work, make workplaces more stressful and make jobs more demanding,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1COur concern about AI isn\u8217\'20\'19t just its effect on the number of jobs, but how it will affect the quality of jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur goal is not to stop new technologies,\u8221\'20\'1D Reynolds continued, \u8220\'20\'1Cbut to make sure the gains of these new technologies are broadly and equitably shared.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Germany has laws requiring companies to notify their works councils about AI and other new technologies that they\u8217\'20\'19re planning to adopt. Most German companies have work councils, which are worker-management committees that discuss everything from vacation schedules to the pace of work and the effects of AI.\par \par \uc2 To prevent AI-based performance data from being used against individual workers, the work councils at \uc2 Deutsche Telekom\uc2  got the company to agree that performance data can be gathered only for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ecommons.cornell.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/a0ac9f50-5a22-4b3d-a9d9-2cc06824e31d/content"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 groups of at least five employees}}}\uc2 . The company has also agreed not to use AI to gather certain personal information about employees, such as their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ecommons.cornell.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/a0ac9f50-5a22-4b3d-a9d9-2cc06824e31d/content"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 political opinions or sexual orientations}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Doellgast and De Stefano \u8211\'20\'13 who edited {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/toc/trsa/29/1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a recent academic journal}}}\uc2  about AI and work \u8211\'20\'13 say worker input about new technologies often reduces their invasiveness and other downsides for workers, while making the introduction of technologies smoother and more productive. Moreover, when workers have some say about new technologies, that often reduces employee resistance to those technologies.\par \par \uc2 De Stefano pointed to some problems with using AI to hire and discipline workers. \u8220\'20\'1CThese machines are, in many cases, unreliable,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey have certain discriminatory output, especially in hiring. These machines are basically benchmarked around a standard worker \u8211\'20\'13 normally white, prime-age, male workers. Anyone who doesn\u8217\'20\'19t correspond to that benchmark risks being misjudged by these algorithms.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the main US labor federation, has created a technology institute to develop expertise and policies on AI and other technologies. That institute is planning training sessions to educate union leaders and strategists about new technologies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn sectors where performance monitoring and algorithmic management are present, you can have a lot of negative impact,\u8221\'20\'1D said Amanda Ballantyne, director of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s technology institute. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you dive into a sector like \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , many workers wear wearables that track every movement their body makes, everywhere they go, how fast they complete tasks, how long they\u8217\'20\'19re off task, how long they take in the bathroom. It\u8217\'20\'19s [scientific management] on steroids.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ballantyne said the communications workers union, the hotel workers union, Sag-Aftra and the Writers Guild have helped lead the way on new technologies. After their recent 148-day strike, the Writers Guild {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/hollywood-ai-strike-wga-artificial-intelligence-39ab72582c3a15f77510c9c30a45ffc8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won protections}}}\uc2  that require studios to disclose whether any material given to writers was developed with the help of AI.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2  says AI and analytics {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://americaninnovators.com/advocacy/letter-to-ostp-on-automated-worker-surveillance-rfi/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 can have substantial benefits}}}\uc2  for workers and productivity, providing insights into worker performance and allowing for targeted coaching and training to improve performance.\par \par \uc2 AI surveillance, the chamber says, can also help prevent workplace violence by, for instance, monitoring abnormal behavior in the workplace. While many workers criticize the use of \u8220\'20\'1Cwearable\u8221\'20\'1D monitors, the chamber says \u8220\'20\'1Csmart sensors and wearable devices can help\u8221\'20\'1D protect them, by detecting potential accidents, ergonomic risks, toxic chemicals and imminent heat stress.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile there are clear benefits\u8221\'20\'1D to AI, said Michael Richards, policy director of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://americaninnovators.com/advocacy/letter-to-ostp-on-automated-worker-surveillance-rfi/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chamber\u8217\'20\'19s technology engagement center}}}\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cwe understand there are legitimate concerns surrounding the use of the technology.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Employers \u8220\'20\'1Crecognize that engaging in an inclusive dialogue about using new technologies is critical to fostering a culture of trust with employees\u8221\'20\'1D, he added.\par \par \uc2 Annette Bernhardt\uc2 , director of the technology and work program at the UC Berkeley Labor Center, pointed to the home-care sector as an example where new technologies make workers\u8217\'20\'19 lives more stressful. Many home-care aides must meticulously follow instructions that apps send them, while reporting back each and every task they complete.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need strong labor standards around the use of these technologies,\u8221\'20\'1D Bernhardt said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to support unions as they bargain around these technologies. Most important, we need to assure that workers have a seat at the table on these technologies from the outset, not just when they\u8217\'20\'19re being implemented.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bernhardt added: \u8220\'20\'1CWhen workers are at the table, it means better technology adoption for them and better results for employers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240107ek17000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
