\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US economy adds more jobs than expected; eurozone inflation rises to 2.9% \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Julia Kollewe \par \uc2 6,773 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 January 2024\par 15:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US unemployment rate remains at 3.7% against expectations of an uptick; however jobs increases in November and October are revised lower\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has opened flat to slightly higher after stronger-than-expected US jobs data. The Dow Jones was little changed while the Nasdaq edged 0.2% higher and the S&P 500 rose 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 Over here, the FTSE 100 index is 0.4% lower while the German and French markets are down 0.3% and 0.6% respectively.\par \par \uc2 The US workforce added 216,000 jobs last month, more than expected by economists, capping another robust year of growth in the face of higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers, weighing when to start cutting borrowing costs, are closely monitoring the strength of the labor market as they try to guide the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy to a so-called \u8220\'20\'1Csoft landing\u8221\'20\'1D, where price growth normalizes and recession is avoided.\par \par \uc2 American employers had been expected by economists to add about 164,000 jobs in December. Recruitment across the public, healthcare, social assistance and construction sectors helped drive growth as 2023 drew to a close.\par \par \uc2 However, the increases in US jobs in October and November were revised lower by a combined 71,000.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/05/us-economy-inflation-jobs-report-december"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US adds 216,000 jobs in December as stronger than expected rise caps robust year}}}\par \par \uc2 Inflation across the eurozone rose in December after an increase in energy costs, reversing six months of consecutive falls and easing the pressure on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/european-central-bank"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 European Central Bank}}}\uc2  (\uc2 ECB\uc2 ) to cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Figures from the \uc2 EU\uc2  statistical agency {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/documents/2995521/18261481/2-05012024-AP-EN.pdf/a80f5906-6834-0dae-2eae-44ac9a59e946"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Eurostat}}}\uc2  showed consumer prices across the 20-country bloc rose at an annual rate of 2.9% last month, up from 2.4% in November. Economists polled by Reuters had forecast a slightly higher reading of 3% for December.\par \par \uc2 The increase in the headline rate comes after the end of government support for utility costs, alongside a smaller annual decline in energy prices in December than in November connected to last year\u8217\'20\'19s one-off subsidy in Germany.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/05/eurozone-inflation-rises-after-increase-in-energy-costs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Eurozone inflation rises to 2.9% after increase in energy costs}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/05/not-that-difficult-buy-home-uk-natwest-chair-howard-davies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Not that difficult to buy a home in UK, claims NatWest chair}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/05/uk-house-prices-rise-for-third-month-in-row-but-are-forecast-to-drop-this-year"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK house prices rise again but Halifax forecasts up to 4% drop in 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/05/global-finances-what-does-2024-hold-in-store-for-major-economies-and-markets"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Global finances: what does 2024 hold in store for major economies and markets?}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/jan/05/sick-man-of-europe-what-is-happening-to-germany-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Why is Germany\u8217\'20\'19s economy struggling \u8211\'20\'13 and can the government fix it?}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2024/jan/05/tube-strikes-major-disruption-expected-on-london-underground-throughout-next-week"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tube strikes: major disruption expected on London Underground throughout next week}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/jan/05/electric-car-sales-in-uk-flatline-prompting-calls-for-vat-cut"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Electric car sales in UK flatline, prompting calls for VAT cut }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/05/endeavour-mining-sacks-ceo-over-serious-misconduct-allegations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gold-mining company sacks CEO over \u8216\'20\'18serious misconduct\u8217\'20\'19 allegations}}}\par \par \uc2 Thank you for reading the blog. Have a great weekend! We\u8217\'20\'19ll be back next week. \u8211\'20\'13 JK\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Davies: 'did not intend to underplay serious challenges' for first-time buyers\par \par \uc2 NatWest Group\uc2  chairman Sir \uc2 Howard Davies\uc2  has released a sort of mea culpa, a statement to clarify his earlier remarks that it\u8217\'20\'19s not \u8220\'20\'1Cthat difficult\u8221\'20\'1D to buy a home and people just have to save for it.\par \par \uc2 He said he \u8220\'20\'1Cdid not intend to underplay the serious challenges\u8221\'20\'1D people face buying homes. The interview on radio 4\u8217\'20\'19s Today programme this morning provoked outrage, including from campaign group Generation Rent. Critics said his claims showed he was out of touch with the reality faced by many people trying to buy a home.\par \par \uc2 Clarifying his remarks, Davies said:\par \par \uc2 Given recent rate movements by lenders there are some early green shoots in mortgage pricing and while funding remains strong, my comment was meant to reflect that in this context access to mortgages is less difficult than it has been.\par \par \uc2 I fully realise it did not come across in that way for listeners and as I said on the programme, I do recognise how difficult it is for people buying a home and I did not intend to underplay the serious challenges they face.\par \par \uc2 People have to save much more than they did in the past and that is tough for first-time buyers.\par \par \uc2 The role for banks in today\u8217\'20\'19s environment is to lend responsibly and support customers to build a savings habit and move towards home ownership.\par \par \uc2 Torsten Bell\uc2 , who runs the \uc2 Resolution Foundation\uc2  think tank, said the time required to save for a typical first-time buyer deposit has spiralled in recent years.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The dollar has given up the gains made since the US jobs numbers were released, as people realised they are not as strong as it first seemed.\par \par \uc2 The greenback is now flat against the pound and the euro, after jumping 0.5% and 0.6% respectively when the data came out just under an hour ago.\par \par \uc2 While employers hired 216,000 people in December, more than the 170,000 expected by Wall Street, the November and October increases in jobs were revised down by a total 71,000. The unemployment rate remained at 3.7%.\par \par \uc2 Axel Merk\uc2 , founder of \uc2 Merk Investments\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 enltrMuch of early market reaction to non-farm payroll now muted - in part as market may be digesting that the total labor force dropped by 676,000 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/wAwzRdMuTX"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/wAwzRdMuTX}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Axel Merk\uc2  (@AxelMerk) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AxelMerk/status/1743272743926415584?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sophie Lund-Yates\uc2 , lead equity analyst at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 There are a few vital signs officials can monitor when trying to diagnose the health of the economy, and the labour market is a crucial one. Developments show that the US labour market isn\u8217\'20\'19t losing steam, ultimately suggesting that economic activity will need a heavier hand to slow it down. That could see interest rate cuts across the pond pushed further out than hoped \u8211\'20\'13 bad news for the market, which until recently was pricing in heavy cuts this year.\par \par \uc2 The data has short-term implications, but the bigger question looks to the future. The resilience shown by the US labour market has been markedly stronger than predicted, which makes bringing things in line without triggering a rock-hard landing becomes a much more delicate task.\par \par \uc2 2023 overall was a bumper year for the labour market, and the overall temperature is still too hot to be fully comfortable. There\u8217\'20\'19s every chance the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will demand a run of softer macro readings before hitting the rewind button on rates. It shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be forgotten that the rate of labour growth is slowing, which is a step in the right direction, but there\u8217\'20\'19s work to be done.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Paul Ashworth\uc2 , chief North America economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 The slightly bigger-than-expected 216,000 gain in non-farm payrolls in December coupled with a second consecutive 0.4% m/m gain in average hourly earnings means that this labour market report will trigger a further paring back of expectations for a March rate cut. At this stage, however, all that really matters for the Fed is the CPI and PPI data, due next week, which we expect to be more supportive of early action from the Fed.\par \par \uc2 The 216,000 gain last month, was not quite as good as it looks at first glance. Gains in the preceding two months were revised down by a cumulative 71,000. The increase in December was once again concentrated in only a few non-cyclical sectors, with government employment rising by 52,000 and health & social assistance employment up by 59,000. To the extent that it is still a leading indicator of broader employment trends, the 33,000 decline in temporary jobs is also a concern.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate remained unchanged at 3.7%, but only because a 676,000 drop back in the size of the labour force almost kept pace with a 683,000 decline in the household survey measure of employment. Those declines more than offset the big gains in November, meaning that both are now below the October levels. With the unemployment rate still muted, December brought a second consecutive 0.4% m/m gain in average hourly earnings, which was enough to push the annual rate back up to 4.1%, from 4.0%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The dollar jumped after the stronger-than-expected increase in US jobs in December, as this suggests the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  won\u8217\'20\'19t be in a rush to interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index hit 103.10, a fresh two-week high, and is now at 102.75, up 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 The greenback strengthened as much as 0.5% against sterling and 0.6% against the euro. It is now trading 0.2% higher against the pound at $1.2650 and against the euro at $1.0917.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Some 52,000 new jobs were in government while 164,000 came from the private sector.\par \par \uc2 Here is our full story on the figures:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/05/us-economy-inflation-jobs-report-december"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US added 216,000 jobs in December, capping robust year in face of high interest rates}}}\par \par \uc2 However, Peter Schiff, chief economist and global strategist at Euro Pacific Asset Management, tweeted:\par \par \uc2 enltrOnce again the media is focusing on Dec. non-farm payrolls beating estimates by 46K. However, the downward revisions to both Oct. and Nov. subtracted 71K from prior estimates. So the report is not a beat, it's another miss. Dec.'s number is likely to be revised lower next month!\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Peter Schiff (@PeterSchiff) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PeterSchiff/status/1743267817825112164?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US economy adds more jobs than expected\par \par \uc2 NEWSFLASH: The US economy added 216,000 jobs last month, more than expected, although the November and October increases were revised lower.\par \par \uc2 Economists had expected an increase between 150,000 and 170,000. November\u8217\'20\'19s rise was revised to 173,000 from 199,000 while the October figure was also revised lower, to 105,000 from 150,000, according to the \uc2 US Labor Department\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate stayed at 3.7%, while analysts had expected an uptick to 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, shares in London-listed Revolution Bars have plunged nearly 20% after the company said it would shut eight of its worst-performing bars to reduce future site losses.\par \par \uc2 The company\u8217\'20\'19s bars and pubs trade under the Revolution, Revoluci\u243\'00\'F3n de Cuba and Peach Pubs brands.\par \par \uc2 The closed bars are: Revolution Bars in Beaconsfield, Derby, Reading, St Peters Liverpool and Wilmslow, Revoluci\u243\'00\'F3n de Cubas in Sheffield and Southampton and the Playhouse in Newcastle-Under-Lyme. Negotiations for five of the eight bars have already begun for them to be transferred to other operators or their leases rescinded.\par \par \uc2 The closures come despite a 9% rise in like-for-like sales in the four weeks from 4 to 31 December, the group\u8217\'20\'19s best festive period since 2019. But it is worried about the current macro-economic conditions. Like-for-like sales for the first half, including New Year\u8217\'20\'19s Eve, were on an improving trend but remained negative, with a decline of 2.8%.\par \par \uc2 Rob Pitcher\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 Revolution Bars Group\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 We have had the best festive trading period for four years with all of our brands recording positive like for like sales and Revoluci\u243\'00\'F3n de Cuba being the standout performer.\par \par \uc2 However, our younger customers are still feeling the disproportionate effect of the cost-of-living crisis and the national living wage will increase materially in April 2024. Therefore, we have taken the difficult yet ultimately beneficial step for the Group to close several bars which are unprofitable.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Less than 10 minutes to go until non-farm payrolls, a key US jobs report, for December. We are expecting an 150,000 increase in jobs after November\u8217\'20\'19s 199,000.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is rising ahead of the data, heading for its steepest weekly rise since May, as traders are scaling back expectations of early US interest rate cuts this year.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is up 0.2% against a basket of currencies, after touching a fresh three-week high. The index is up 1.3% this week so far, its strongest performance since the week to 15 May.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The chair of \uc2 NatWest Group\uc2 , Sir \uc2 Howard Davies\uc2 , has been criticised as being out of touch, after he said it was not \u8220\'20\'1Cthat difficult\u8221\'20\'1D buy a house at the moment.\par \par \uc2 The campaign group Generation Rent said Davies\u8217\'20\'19s comments were \u8220\'20\'1Castounding to hear from a senior banker\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Here is our full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/05/not-that-difficult-buy-home-uk-natwest-chair-howard-davies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Not that difficult to buy a home in UK, claims NatWest chair}}}\par \par \uc2 Torsten Bell\uc2 , chief executive of the \uc2 Resolution Foundation\uc2 , a think tank, tweeted:\par \par \uc2 enltrWhat's changed of course is how long you have to save for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/aVqg0cLCd3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/aVqg0cLCd3}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/UTeiNAdw2i"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/UTeiNAdw2i}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Torsten Bell\uc2  (@TorstenBell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TorstenBell/status/1743233252091969930?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 If your home has been flooded, here\u8217\'20\'19s the \uc2 ABI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s six flood recovery steps:\par \par \uc2 Step 1 - Contact your insurer as soon as possible. They will advise you on arranging emergency accommodation if necessary (or any temporary alternative trading premises if you are a business with business interruption cover), the information they will need from you to support your claim, and how to go ahead with the immediate clean-up and repair process.\par \par \uc2 Step 2 - Assessing the damage and finding temporary accommodation. A loss adjuster will be appointed to assess the claim. An initial assessment of the damage will be done, the repair process explained and the options for alternative accommodation outlined. You should expect to hear from your loss adjuster after contacting your insurer and they will get in touch if they need to arrange a visit to your home.\par \par \uc2 Step 3 - Cleaning and stripping out. Your loss adjuster will organise the cleaning and stripping out of your home. This work should start within four weeks of discussing it with your loss adjuster.\par \par \uc2 Step 4 - Disinfecting and drying your home. Your insurer or loss adjuster will appoint a drying company to disinfect and dry out your home. This can take from a few weeks to several months. Your loss adjuster will give you a timetable and keep you informed.\par \par \uc2 Step 5 - Repair and reconstruction. Repair work should begin shortly after you get your drying certificate from the drying company. Your loss adjuster will appoint a builder to do the repair and reconstruction work and will keep you updated on expected timeframes.\par \par \uc2 Step 6 - Moving back into your home. Your insurer or loss adjuster will discuss with you when you can return home. Depending on how badly damaged your home is, this can be between a few weeks and a year or more after the flood.\par \par \uc2 Some insurers will provide \u8220\'20\'1CBuild Back Better\u8221\'20\'1D which offers homeowners the chance to install Property Flood Resilience measures up to the value of \u163\'00\'A310,000 when repairing their properties after a flood. Speak to your insurer about whether you are eligible.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ABI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s guide is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.abi.org.uk/globalassets/files/publications/public/flooding/abi-responding-to-floods-guide---national-flood-forum.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Here is some insurance advice for homeowners and businesses who have been affected by flooding and storm Henk, from the \uc2 Association of British Insurers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Damage caused by floods and storm is covered by most standard home insurance and commercial business policies, and comprehensive motor insurance.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/live/2024/jan/05/uk-weather-storm-henk-rain-flooding-england-latest-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cold weather alert issued after homes flooded and transport network hit across England and Wales \u8211\'20\'13 live}}}\par \par \uc2 Louise Clark, general insurance policy adviser at the industry body, said:\par \par \uc2 Insurers expect bad weather to strike at any time and events such as this are exactly what your insurance is for. Their priority right now is to help any affected customers recover as quickly as possible. If you have been affected by flooding, contact your insurer as soon as you can and they will be able to offer help and advice.\par \par \uc2 If you have suffered storm damage:\par \par \uc2 \u183\'00\'B7 Contact your insurer as soon as possible. Most will have 24-hour emergency helplines to ensure you get advice on what to do and arrange repairs as quickly as is possible.\par \par \uc2 \u183\'00\'B7 If necessary, arrange temporary emergency repairs to stop any damage getting worse, but speak to your insurer first. If you have to arrange emergency repairs yourself, tell your insurer and keep any receipts, as this will form part of your claim.\par \par \uc2 \u183\'00\'B7 Do not be in a rush to throw away damaged items, unless they are a danger to health, as these may be able to be repaired or restored. Your insurer will advise.\par \par \uc2 If your home is uninhabitable while repairs are being carried out your insurer will arrange for, and pay the cost of, any alternative temporary accommodation you may need in line with your policy.\par \par \uc2 Commercial polices will cover damage to premises and stock. Business interruption cover (which may be included or purchased separately) will cover additional trading costs, such as hiring temporary alternative trading premises if necessary.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.abi.org.uk/products-and-issues/topics-and-issues/flooding/storms/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Here}}}\uc2  is the guidance in full.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 enltrOverall in the UK Construction PMI, construction new work fell in December & it has been falling since August, which points to further falls in construction activity in 2024 H1 (especially in house building) but... (11/n) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ukconstruction?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ukconstruction}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/xNuqdQXsZf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/xNuqdQXsZf}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Noble Francis (@NobleFrancis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NobleFrancis/status/1743211274261918115?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 UK's construction sector decline eases\par \par \uc2 The downturn in the UK\u8217\'20\'19s construction sector eased last month to the slowest rate of decline since September, according to a survey.\par \par \uc2 A sustained slump in house building was the main factor holding back construction output, which firms linked to elevated interest rates and subdued confidence among clients.\par \par \uc2 The headline purchasing managers\u8217\'20\'19 index, which tracks changes in industry activity, rose to 46.8 in December from 45.5 in November. Any reading below 50 indicates contraction.\par \par \uc2 Improving supply conditions meant delivery times for construction items shortened for the tenth month in a row. Price discounting among suppliers contributed to a moderate fall in average cost burdens across the construction sector at the end of 2023.\par \par \uc2 Tim Moore\uc2 , economics director at S&P Global Market Intelligence, which compiles the survey said:\par \par \uc2 Construction companies experienced another fall in business activity at the end of 2023 as weak order books meant a lack of new work to replace completed projects. House building was the worst-performing area of construction activity, but even in this segment there were signs that the downturn has started to ease. \u8220\'20\'1C\par \par \uc2 Elevated borrowing costs and a subsequent slump in market confidence were the main factors leading to falling sales volumes across the construction sector in the second half of 2023. Survey respondents also continued to cite worries about the broader UK economic outlook, especially in relation to prospects for commercial construction.\par \par \uc2 However, expectations of falling interest rates during the months ahead appear to have supported confidence levels among construction companies. December data indicated that 41% of construction firms predict a rise in business activity over the course of 2024, while only 17% forecast a decline. This contrasted with negative sentiment overall at the same time a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Industry expert Noble Francis tweeted:\par \par \uc2 enltrUK construction activity fell in December 2023 according to the latest S&P Global UK Construction PMI data (46.8) but it fell a slower rate than in November (45.5) where 50=no monthly change (& below 50 represents a decline in activity). (1/n) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ukconstruction?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ukconstruction}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ukhousing?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ukhousing}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/e05nWmG8Vb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/e05nWmG8Vb}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Noble Francis (@NobleFrancis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NobleFrancis/status/1743204375546233117?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 The December uptick in eurozone inflation will prove temporary, says \uc2 Nicola Nobile\uc2 , chief Italy economist at Oxford Economics.\par \par \uc2 As anticipated by yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s national numbers, eurozone inflation rate increased in December. But this was mainly driven by the energy component. German energy inflation, up to 4% was the main culprit as energy inflation was impacted by a strong base effect from the one-off gas price break in December 2022.\par \par \uc2 Apart from energy, all the other components continued their disinflationary trend. Although some of the components, such as food remain elevated and subject to some volatility, the disinflation dynamic is still quite clear. Food inflation (which also includes alcohol and tobacco) was at 6.1% in December, down from around 10% in the summer. Moreover, the second monthly increase in unprocessed foods inflation was very likely driven by unfavourable weather conditions and hence should soon readjust downwards.\par \par \uc2 Core inflation dropped to 3.4%, from 3.6% in November, driven by a decrease in non-energy industrial goods inflation. Services inflation remained at 4% over the year, but also in this area the monthly dynamic offers some optimism. The 0.7% m/m increase in services inflation is a touch lower than the 2010-2019 average December monthly increase\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Overall, apart from the monthly volatility, this was another encouraging inflation report and further confirms that a quick disinflationary process is underway. But today\u8217\'20\'19s print will not offer any major insights on the \uc2 ECB\uc2  easing cycle. We expect that the January inflation print will be much more informative, as large price adjustments typically happen at the start of the year, with this year\u8217\'20\'19s January print also impacted by the end of the energy-related fiscal measures in some countries. We will follow up on this specifically in forthcoming research.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 Bert Colijn\uc2 , senior eurozone economist at ING, said:\par \par \uc2 The increase in headline inflation was mainly driven by energy base effects in Germany. The core inflation rate dropped from 3.6 to 3.4%, indicating that the underlying trend in inflation remains relatively benign for the moment. Food inflation also continued to trend down rapidly.\par \par \uc2 Inflationary pressures made way for disinflation over the course of 2023 as demand weakened and supply shocks faded. This has brought inflation down more than expected at the start of last year. Currently though, base effects from easing supply shocks are moderating and some new inflationary concerns are surfacing. Think of the increased supply chain concerns related to the Red Sea. Besides that, \uc2 German government\uc2  measures are also adding to inflation for 2024. The \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s mantra has always been that the last leg is the hardest. Is this what we are currently witnessing?\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t overestimate the inflationary pressures for now though. Demand remains weak, which is a very important disinflationary driver right now. Also, inventories are high, making current supply chain concerns much less inflationary than the ones from 2021. And even though energy price shocks related to the Middle East are a clear risk to the outlook, oil prices are currently still below US$80 per barrel. So overall, the outlook for inflation continues to be quite benign and we expect eurozone inflation to be around 2% again by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 And what does this mean for interest rates?\par \par \uc2 Current inflationary developments therefore seem to support our view that recent market expectations of a first-quarter hike are premature, but don\u8217\'20\'19t think that we\u8217\'20\'19re back to \u8216\'20\'18higher for longer\u8217\'20\'19 either. We expect the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to start cautiously cutting rates from June onwards, with 75bp in total for 2024.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 NatWest\uc2  boss: Not 'that difficult' for people to buy a house\par \par \uc2 Sir \uc2 Howard Davies\uc2 , chair of \uc2 NatWest Group\uc2 , has caused a bit of a stir with his comments this morning that it\u8217\'20\'19s not \u8220\'20\'1Cthat difficult\u8221\'20\'1D for people to get on the housing ladder.\par \par \uc2 He said on BBC radio 4\u8217\'20\'19s Today programme:\par \par \uc2 I don\u8217\'20\'19t think it\u8217\'20\'19s that difficult at the moment [to buy a house]. You have to save, and that\u8217\'20\'19s the way it always used to be\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 But what we saw in the financial crisis was the risk of having people being able to borrow 100% In order to get onto the property ladder and then suffering severe falls in the equity value of their houses and having to leave and having a bad credit record etc. So there were dangers in very, very easy access to mortgage credit.\par \par \uc2 So I totally recognise that there are people who find it very difficult to start the process. They will have to save more. But that is, I think, inherent in the change in the financial system, as a result of the mistakes that were made in the last global financial crisis and we have to accept we\u8217\'20\'19re still living with that.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.43am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 Electric car sales in UK flatline, prompting calls for VAT cut\par \par \uc2 The number of new cars registered in the UK has jumped by nearly 18% but electric vehicle demand is flatlining, prompting the industry to call for a VAT cut to stimulate sales.\par \par \uc2 Annual figures released by the \uc2 Society of Motor Manufacturers and Traders\uc2  (SMMT) on Friday show 1.9m new cars were registered last year, well up on the previous year\u8217\'20\'19s figure of 1.6m and the highest level since the 2.3m registrations of 2019.\par \par \uc2 The increase is a boost for the automotive industry after the pandemic led to supply chain problems and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/sep/30/computer-chip-shortage-stalls-uk-car-industry-production"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shortage of vital computer chips}}}\uc2  that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/aug/26/lowest-levels-of-car-production-for-any-july-since-1956-uk-industry-reports"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slowed production}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Across the year, 315,000 new battery electric vehicles were sold. That was 50,000 more than 2022, but the number being bought as a share of total registrations failed to grow as expected. They represented just 16.5% of the total, slightly down on last year\u8217\'20\'19s 16.6%.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/jan/05/electric-car-sales-in-uk-flatline-prompting-calls-for-vat-cut"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Electric car sales in UK flatline, prompting calls for VAT cut }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 Eurozone inflation rises to 2.9%\par \par \uc2 Inflation in the eurozone has risen again and could go higher in the coming months, which makes early interest rate cuts from the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  less likely.\par \par \uc2 Inflation across the 20-nation bloc rose to 2.9% in December from November\u8217\'20\'19s two-year low of 2.4% because of a reduction of government subsidies on gas, electricity and food.\par \par \uc2 The data are in line with the \uc2 ECB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s expectation that inflation bottomed out in November. The central bank is projecting that price growth will range between 2.5% and 3% through 2024, well above its 2% target, before slowing again next year.\par \par \uc2 However, underlying inflation \u8211\'20\'13 price growth excluding food and energy which tend to be volatile \u8211\'20\'13 eased to 3.4% from 3.6%.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ECB\uc2 , which next meets on 25 January to discuss monetary policy, pushed back against investor expectations of imminent rate cuts last month. It wants to see wage pressures cool first to ensure infation is on track to fall back to its target.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 Neil Wilson\uc2 , chief market analyst at Finalto Financial Services, said:\par \par \uc2 European stock markets slipped in early trading, pushing the main indices firmly into the red in the first week of trading in 2024 as the data paints a picture of weakening economic activity and higher inflation.\par \par \uc2 Weak \uc2 PMI\uc2  data for the euro area was confirmed, whilst German consumer inflation surged higher as energy subsidies faded. German\u8217\'20\'19s inflation hit 3.8% in December, up from 2.3% in Nov. Final inflation data for the Eurozone as a whole is due at 10am, expected up to 3.0% from 2.4%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile FOMC minutes earlier this week suggested the Fed is not quite so close to cutting rates; the US 10-year Treasury yield broke above 4%, helping the dollar hit its highest since the middle of December and keeping the pressure on risk assets.\par \par \uc2 Shares in London, Frankfurt and Paris all retreated on Friday morning, with the US jobs report in focus. The Nasdaq notched its fifth straight daily decline on Thursday, whilst the S&P 500 dropped for a fourth session in a row with \uc2 Apple\uc2  suffering again on another downgrade. For the week, the FTSE 100 is off about three-quarters of a percent, held up relative to peers with oil firmer, whilst the Dax is down 1.4% and CAC is 2% lower with luxury taking a bit of a bashing on China fears.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 Stocks fall; crude oil prices rise over 1%\par \par \uc2 On the financial markets, stocks are falling while crude oil prices have risen more than 1% amid tensions in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures are up 87 cents at $78.46 a barrel while US light crude is 97 cents ahead at $73.16 a barrel. US secretary of state \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is heading to the Middle East for a week of diplomacy to try and prevent the Israel-Gaza conflict from widening. You can read more here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2024/jan/05/middle-east-crisis-live-updates-antony-blinken-israel-palestine-gaza-hamas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Middle East crisis live: Israel to continue war with \u8216\'20\'18new combat approach\u8217\'20\'19 until Hamas dismantled, defence minister says}}}\par \par \uc2 Attacks by Iran-backed Houthi rebels from Yemen on commercial ships in the Red Sea have also triggered supply concerns.\par \par \uc2 On the stock markets , the FTSE 100 index has fallen nearly 67 points, or 0.9%, to 7,656, as optimism about interest rate cuts fades.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s Dax is down 105 points, or 0.6%, at 16,513 while France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC has lost 66 points, a 0.9% drop to 7,384. Italy\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE MiB has shed 184 points, or 0.6%, to 30,221.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 NatWest\uc2  chair: Likely we will see slow reduction in interest rates\par \par \uc2 Sir \uc2 Howard Davies\uc2 , who chairs \uc2 NatWest\uc2 , has warned that we we could see a \u8220\'20\'1Crather slow reduction in interest rates\u8221\'20\'1D this year because \u8220\'20\'1Cwage expectations are quite high\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Speaking on radio 4\u8217\'20\'19s Today programme, he explained why lenders have already started to cut mortgage rates in a fierce price war. As financial markets are now expecting a series of rate cuts from the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  this year,\par \par \uc2 therefore you can, as a bank, fix your interest rate at a slightly lower level than you could even a couple of months ago. And it\u8217\'20\'19s that fixing of the rates in the market that determines what we can offer to customers. So the market expectations of rates are falling, therefore, we can pass that on to people who want a new mortgage.\par \par \uc2 He said because the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  was criticised for being slow to raise interest rates when inflation shot up mainly due to higher energy and food prices, policymakers will be careful when they reduce borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 Even at the last meeting in December, three of the nine members of the [rate-setting] committee still voted for a further increase in rates. So they\u8217\'20\'19re quite a long way away at the moment from a majority in favour of a reduction in rates. And there is a risk of that, having been burned once by reacting too slowly. They are now going to be rather cautious in coming down.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s likely that we will see a rather slow reduction in rates during the year. They will of course be influenced by what is going on in retail prices, not rising anything like as rapidly as they were, but still wage expectations are quite high and that if you read the recent speeches from the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 , thats what\u8217\'20\'19s worrying them the most.\par \par \uc2 Howard Davies\uc2 , chairman of \uc2 Natwest Group\uc2  at his office in Bishopgate. Photograph: Suki Dhanda/The Observer\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a lookahead to what 2024 might bring in the housing market:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/04/britains-housing-market-may-be-past-peak-pain-but-what-will-2024-bring"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s housing market may be \u8216\'20\'18past peak pain\u8217\'20\'19 but what will 2024 bring?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 EY\uc2  Item Club forecasting group said the 1.1% month-on-month rise in the Halifax measure of house prices in December capped off a year when values proved much more resilient than forecasters had expected.\par \par \uc2 An average of the Halifax and Nationwide measures was down 1.5% in the fourth quarter on a year earlier, in contrast to consensus predictions last January of a fall of 6.5%.\par \par \uc2 Martin Beck\uc2 , chief economic adviser to the group, said:\par \par \uc2 Two factors have led to a modest correction. Firstly, unemployment has remained low. Secondly, the rise in mortgage interest rates has been much more protracted than in the past, reflecting a shift in the mortgage stock from variable to fixed rate. Both factors have kept forced sales down and limited supply.\par \par \uc2 The closing months of 2023 saw a recovery in demand for properties, albeit from a low level. Mortgage approvals in December rose to a six-month high. This was probably aided by falling mortgage rates, as investors have priced in a substantial series of rate cuts by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 The EY Item Club thinks this recovery should continue as mortgage rates continue to drift down and lower inflation makes for a likely more predictable macroeconomic outlook. The fact that the ratio of house prices to average earnings is down by over a tenth since the 2022 peak, reflecting a fall in prices alongside strong growth in wages, should also support demand.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.03am GMT\par \par \uc2 Halifax house prices historical data Photograph: Halifax\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 This can cause a lot of stress and sleepless nights. My colleague Jedidajah Otte has talked to homeowners who fear a sharp rise in mortgage payments as they come off fixed-rate deals this year.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2024/jan/04/uk-homeowners-fear-impact-2024-mortgage-timebomb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Sleepless nights\u8217\'20\'19: UK homeowners fear 2024 mortgage timebomb}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 However, mortgage costs are still much higher than they have been in recent years after the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  raised interest rates to 5.25% to fight stubbornly high inflation. (Financial markets expect it to cut rates to below 4% by the end of the year.)\par \par \uc2 As a result, homeowners are facing a \u163\'00\'A319bn increase in mortgage costs as millions more fixed-rate deals expire and borrowers are forced to renegotiate their home loans after the toughest round of interest rate increases in decades, writes our economics correspondent Richard Partington.\par \par \uc2 Despite an escalating price war between lenders cutting the cost of remortgaging in recent days, economists at the US investment bank \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  said many UK households would still experience a dramatic leap in repayments compared with the deals they were leaving behind.\par \par \uc2 In what has been described as a Tory mortgage timebomb by \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s critics, just over 1.5m households are expected to reach the end of cheaper deals in 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 with an increase in annual housing costs of about \u163\'00\'A31,800 for the typical family, according to the \uc2 Resolution Foundation\uc2  thinktank.\par \par \uc2 As fixed-rate deals expire and households absorb the biggest hit to their finances in the postwar age, with inflation and tax rises taking their toll on spending power, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2024/jan/04/uk-homeowners-fear-impact-2024-mortgage-timebomb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 borrowers are turning to a range of measures}}}\uc2  to cope with the increased costs, from renting out rooms in their homes to drawing down pensions early and even postponing having children.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2024/jan/04/homeowners-face-19bn-rise-in-mortgage-costs-as-fixed-rate-deals-expire"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK homeowners face \u163\'00\'A319bn rise in mortgage costs as fixed-rate deals expire}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 Anthony Codling, housing analyst at RBC Capital markets, said:\par \par \uc2 The demise of the UK housing market is somewhat over reported. The Halifax reported today that house prices rose by 1.7% in 2023, an increase of \u163\'00\'A34,800. Most, including us, thought house prices would fall during 2023, and most think they will fall in 2024, but not us.\par \par \uc2 With rising wages, falling inflation, falling mortgage rates, and increasing talk of election related housing stimulus packages we expect house prices to rise in 2024. Our pessimism was misplaced in 2023, and we don\u8217\'20\'19t want to make the same mistake twice.\par \par \uc2 Halifax house price index Photograph: Halifax\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: UK house prices rise for third month amid property shortage\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 House prices in the UK rose for the third consecutive month in December, reflecting a shortage of properties on the market, according to mortgage lender Halifax. It added that with mortgage rates easing, confidence among buyers may improve in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 The cost of an average home rose by 1.1% to \u163\'00\'A3287,105, just over \u163\'00\'A33,000 more than in November and the highest level since March, Halifax said. This comes after monthly gains of 0.6% and 1.2% in November and October.\par \par \uc2 Compared with December 2022, values were up 1.7%, the first annual growth in eight months, following a 0.8% drop in November.\par \par \uc2 Kim Kinnaird, director of Halifax Mortgages, said:\par \par \uc2 The housing market beat expectations in 2023 and grew by 1.7% on an annual basis. The average property price is now \u163\'00\'A34,800 higher than it was in December 2022. Whilst it\u8217\'20\'19s encouraging that we saw growth in the last three months of the year, this was preceded with property price falls for six consecutive months between April and September.\par \par \uc2 The growth we have seen is likely being driven by a shortage of properties on the market, rather than the strength of buyer demand. That said, with mortgage rates continuing to ease, we may see an increase in confidence from buyers over the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Across all the UK regions, Northern Ireland recorded the strongest house price growth in 2023, as properties increased in value by 4.1% to \u163\'00\'A3192,153. Scotland saw property prices increase by 2.6% to \u163\'00\'A3205,170. At the other end of the scale, the south east fell most sharply, houses there now average \u163\'00\'A3376,804, a drop of \u163\'00\'A317,755 or 4.5%.\par \par \uc2 Halifax expects prices to fall by between 2% and 4% this year as many still struggle to afford the sharply higher mortgage costs compared with recent years following a series of \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  rate hikes. The question is when will the central bank start cutting rates? Financial markets are betting that the first reduction will come by May.\par \par \uc2 Kinnaird explained:\par \par \uc2 As we move through 2024, the UK property market will continue to reflect the wider economic uncertainty and buyers and sellers are likely to be naturally cautious when considering making a move. While wage growth is now above inflation, helping to ease cost of living pressures for some and improving housing affordability, interest rates are likely to remain elevated for as long as inflation remains markedly above the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s target.\par \par \uc2 Halifax house price index Photograph: Halifax\par \par \uc2 Mark Harris\uc2 , chief executive of mortgage broker \uc2 SPF Private Clients\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 The housing market saw a remarkably strong finish to the year, as buyer and seller confidence was boosted by three consecutive interest rate holds and the growing belief that the next move in rates will be downwards.\par \par \uc2 Increased competitiveness among lenders leads to lower mortgage rates and we find ourselves in the midst of a price war. With \uc2 HSBC\uc2  launching the headline-grabbing 3.94% five-year fix and reductions from Halifax, \uc2 NatWest\uc2 , \uc2 TSB\uc2  and other lenders, the gloves really are off.\par \par \uc2 With 2023 being a disappointing year in terms of amount of business done, lenders are keen to get this year off to a cracking start. Increased competition, rates aside, may also lead to lenders broadening criteria to attract business with longer mortgage terms or greater flexibility to allow certain variable incomes. Although those remortgaging this year will still see an increase in their payments, the pain will not be as bad as it could have been.\par \par \uc2 Later today we will get the US non-farm payrolls report for December, which is expected to show that the economy added 150,00 jobs following November\u8217\'20\'19s 199,000 increase.\par \par \uc2 The Agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am GMT: S&P Global/CIPS Construction PMI for December\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am GMT: Eurozone inflation for December (forecast: 2.9%)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm GMT: US Non-farm payrolls jobs report for December (forecast: 150,000)\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.08am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240105ek15000p4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
