\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Broken\u8217\'20\'19 US labor laws could hamper union wins for workers, experts warn\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,486 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 December 2023\par 12:40 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Strikes by autoworkers, actors and writers brought wins in 2023, but analysts worry labor laws could undo progress\par \par \uc2 Labor experts see more wins ahead in 2024 for US unions after a year of attention-grabbing strikes and surging public support but worry gains may be stymied by the US\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cbroken\u8221\'20\'1D labor laws.\par \par \uc2 Strikes by autoworkers, writers, actors and nurses and a threatened strike by UPS workers all led to significant wins in 2023. \u8220\'20\'1CThe big challenge for labor in 2024 will be to take that momentum and turn it into new organizing and getting first contracts where workers have organized,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ken Jacobs, the co-director of the UC Berkeley Labor Center. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a real challenge because labor law in the US is broken.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Among the big tests that labor faces is whether the \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW)\uc2  will succeed in using the impressive contracts it won with Detroit\u8217\'20\'19s automakers to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/dec/11/uaw-tesla-elon-musk-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 organize Toyota, Tesla}}}\uc2  and other non-union auto plants, especially in the anti-union south. Another challenge is whether the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/new-unions-won-historic-elections-but-first-contracts-harder_n_656a26fbe4b028b0f3d0196a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Starbucks, Amazon, Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s, Apple, Chipotle and REI}}}\uc2  workers who have unionized over the past two years will finally get first contracts that deliver improved wages and benefits.\par \par \uc2 During 2023, there were several major contract disputes, including ones involving 340,000 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/25/ups-teamsters-strike-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Teamsters at UPS}}}\uc2 , 150,000 screen and television actors, 140,000 autoworkers and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2023/10/13/1205788228/kaiser-permanente-strike-contract-deal-reached"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 85,000 Kaiser Permanente workers}}}\uc2 . In each of those negotiations, unions came away boasting of record contracts, although only after the actors, autoworkers and Kaiser workers went on strike. \u8220\'20\'1C2023 has been huge for labor, both the extraordinary increase in large strikes beyond and the success of workers through those strikes,\u8221\'20\'1D Jacobs said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a really a turnaround from where we had been.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The bargaining schedule for 2024 doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have as many major battles. It includes 200,000 postal workers whose contract expires in September \u8211\'20\'13 it is illegal for them to strikes. Other expiring contracts cover 45,000 dockworkers at ports from Maine to Texas, 30,000 \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/iatse-locals-contract-captain-system-2024-negotiations-1235623124/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 8,000 film crew members}}}\uc2  in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStrike activity might not reach the same level next year but it\u8217\'20\'19s still an opportune time to go on strike,\u8221\'20\'1D said Johnnie Kallas, director of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://striketracker.ilr.cornell.edu/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the ILR Labor Action Tracker}}}\uc2 , which keeps a tally of strikes across the US. Many labor experts say it\u8217\'20\'19s a favorable time to go on strike because the labor market is tight, public approval for unions is at its highest level in decades, and there\u8217\'20\'19s a vigorously pro-union president in the White House. Moreover, many unions feel emboldened because of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tcf.org/content/commentary/labors-great-reset/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unions\u8217\'20\'19 recent record gains}}}\uc2 , like the raises of more than 25% over four years that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  won from \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , with starting pay rising 68%.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis year has certainly shown the effectiveness of withholding labor as a tool,\u8221\'20\'1D said Saba Waheed, the director of the UCLA Labor Center. \u8220\'20\'1CThe number of people willing to go out into the streets has increased so much, and wage increases are bigger than we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen in a long time. Every time there is a successful strike or negotiation, they\u8217\'20\'19re helping set up negotiations for other workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  hopes its record contracts with Detroit\u8217\'20\'19s automakers will set up organizing victories at auto and battery plants across the south. It has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/business/autos-transportation/uaw-launches-bid-organize-tesla-entire-non-union-auto-sector-2023-11-29/#:~:text=The%20Detroit%2Dbased%20UAW%20said,KS)%2C%20Rivian%20(RIVN."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced plans to seek to unionize}}}\uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , Mercedes and \uc2 BMW\uc2 , and its effort to unionize the \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  plant in Chattanooga, Tennessee, is racing ahead of the others, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/dec/07/uaw-volkswagen-chattanooga-tennessee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 with more than 1,000 VW workers}}}\uc2  signing pro-union cards. But some auto executives, most notably \uc2 Tesla\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , have served notice that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  is unwelcome. \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.freep.com/story/money/cars/2023/11/30/elon-musk-tesla-unionsu-aw-general-motors/71754350007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 I disagree with the idea of unions}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Musk said recently. \u8220\'20\'1CI just don\u8217\'20\'19t like anything which creates a lords and peasants sort of thing. I think the unions naturally try to create negativity in a company.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Joseph McCartin, a labor historian at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , voiced relative optimism for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re in a better position to make a move on these companies than they have ever been,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey not only have a great contract to show what they\u8217\'20\'19ve accomplished, but they have the will to wage the campaign in a way that the union has not for a long time. It\u8217\'20\'19s bound to be a really important campaign. There\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a furious struggle.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Will Jones, a labor historian at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 , added: \u8220\'20\'1CA lot is resting on the \uc2 UAW\uc2 . It could make a big difference if they have some big victories in organizing these plants. It could have a ripple effect.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Like the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  have launched an ambitious unionization drive in targeting the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/nov/29/amazon-union-busting-kentucky-air-hub"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fiercely anti-union Amazon}}}\uc2 . The labor movement is also looking for many more unionization wins at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.insidehighered.com/news/faculty-issues/labor-unionization/2023/09/06/grad-worker-unionization-booming-even-down-south#:~:text=%E2%80%9COver%20the%20past%20decade%2C%20however,contingent%20or%20tenure%2Dtrack.%E2%80%9D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 universities}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2023/05/18/changing-institutional-culture-from-the-inside-out-why-more-and-more-us-museum-workers-are-forming-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 museums}}}\uc2 , where many white-collar workers have embraced unions to obtain better pay and a stronger voice at work.\par \par \uc2 On the strike front, labor leaders are threatening a highly innovative walkout by up to 30,000 workers in the Twin Cities in March. Unions representing 4,500 janitors, 1,000 airport workers, 2,500 security guards and thousands of teachers and support staff have lined up expiration dates so they can {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://workdaymagazine.org/what-could-we-win-together-labor-in-minnesota-gears-up-for-a-major-escalation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 all go on strike at the same time}}}\uc2 . Their demands focus on housing affordability, a cleaner environment, better jobs and improving schools. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re trying to do is a contribution to the evolving excitement about labor,\u8221\'20\'1D said Greg Nammacher, the president of a service employees local representing Twin Cities janitors and security guards.\par \par \uc2 A big question for labor in 2024 is whether \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers, who first unionized two years ago in Buffalo and now have unionized more than 360 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , will finally get a first contract after their union and the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  have repeatedly accused the company of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nrn.com/news/labor-board-claims-starbucks-refusing-negotiate-144-unionized-cafes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 failing to bargain in good faith}}}\uc2 . Union officials complain that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has refused to negotiate for more than six months and has not put forward any contract proposals in over a year. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  says it\u8217\'20\'19s intent on reaching a contract.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Luce, a labor studies professor at \uc2 City University of New York\uc2 , said that to pressure \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to reach a contract, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe need a major disruption of corporate power\u8221\'20\'1D. There {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/sep/13/starbucks-boycott-union-busting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are growing calls for a Starbucks boycott}}}\uc2  ; students at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehoya.com/students-petition-for-georgetown-to-expel-starbucks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Georgetown}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dailybruin.com/2023/12/05/usac-passes-referendum-calling-on-university-to-end-contracts-with-starbucks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UCLA}}}\uc2  are urging their universities to kick out \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many labor leaders see another important challenge for 2024: to help ensure that Joe Biden defeats \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the likely Republican nominee for president. McCartin said the 2024 election is reminiscent of 1948. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you think about \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 he was not doing well in the polls, he was struggling, his party was divided,\u8221\'20\'1D with rival candidates Strom Thurmond and \uc2 Henry Wallace\uc2 , McCartin said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen Truman won, he said that labor did it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McCartin said Biden is also facing a \u8220\'20\'1Csituation of how to hold together: Biden has a youth problem. People under 35 are not enthused about him. In my view, labor is well-positioned to be an engine for the re-election of Biden. But it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to prognosticate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jones of the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CLabor has to play a big role. I think it\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a make or break. Biden has talked a lot about turning out working-class voters, particularly white men. He\u8217\'20\'19s been fairly successful at that. There\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of talk of him losing support among Black and Latino voters. So the degree that Biden can turn out white, working-class voters is really critical. That\u8217\'20\'19s something the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and other unions can really help with.\u8221\'20\'1D Jones said unions could make a pivotal difference in industrial states such as Michigan and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Arguably the biggest challenge labor faces is whether unions can finally begin to reverse the decline in union membership and in the percentage of workers in unions. Just 10% of workers are in unions, down from more than 20% during the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 Many worker advocates say it\u8217\'20\'19s great that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has undertaken a bold, large-scale organizing drive that aims to unionize more than 100,000 workers. But these advocates say what is badly needed is for other unions, helped by the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , also to launch big, ambitious organizing drives.\par \par \uc2 Reversing the decline in union membership \u8220\'20\'1Cis the big test\u8221\'20\'1D, Jacobs said, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 UAW\uc2  demonstrated what a union can do when its members are fully engaged and taking on the boss. Can unions turn that into new organizing and expanding and increasing union density? In the context of our very broken labor law, none of this is easy. But I\u8217\'20\'19m the most optimistic I\u8217\'20\'19ve been since I began doing this work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231227ejcr001e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
