\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18If this was about money, we\u8217\'20\'19d still be teaching\u8217\'20\'19: inside the longest adjunct strike in US history\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,059 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2023\par 16:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Academics at Columbia College in Chicago are in a fierce dispute over cuts to courses and poor working conditions\par \par \uc2 The longest strike of adjuncts in US labor history is still ongoing, with academics at Columbia College in Chicago remaining in a fierce dispute over cuts to college courses and a host of complaints over poor working conditions.\par \par \uc2 The fierce dispute began when Columbia College leadership suddenly announced plans to implement significant cuts to courses and course sections, and consolidating classes which have ballooned class sizes, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blockclubchicago.org/2023/11/29/28-days-in-frustrations-mount-as-columbia-college-adjunct-strike-continues/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 citing}}}\uc2  a $20m budget shortfall.\par \par \uc2 The strike has thrown a spotlight on how higher education in the US has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.insidehighered.com/blogs/higher-ed-gamma/adjunctification-gen-ed-0#:~:text=We%20now%20know%20that%20reliance,grim%20realities%20of%20adjunct%20life."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increasingly relied }}}\uc2  on adjunct faculty, professors who often work with little to no job security and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.insidehighered.com/news/2020/04/20/new-report-says-many-adjuncts-make-less-3500-course-and-25000-year"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 low pay}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Diana Vallera, who has taught at the school for 15 years as an adjunct professor in photography and currently serves as president of the Columbia College faculty union, said the union immediately began pushing for more information while still trying to bargain over a new union contract for the school\u8217\'20\'19s adjunct faculty.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t make unilateral changes to mandatory subjects to bargaining. The employer just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t care. They kept referring to management rights. They don\u8217\'20\'19t want to work with the union, but there is one, and they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be accountable to anyone,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Vallera said 53 course sections were cut from the fall 2023 semester and 317 for the spring 2024 semester.\par \par \uc2 The union filed an unfair labor practice charge against Columbia College with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , one of seven they have filed since August 2023, along with voting to start striking on 30 October as contract bargaining has seen little movement toward an agreement.\par \par \uc2 The strike is now the longest by adjuncts in US history, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 The union has criticized the cuts while executives at Columbia College continued to receive large bonuses to their salaries despite the school\u8217\'20\'19s financial deficit woes as reasons behind the cuts.\par \par \uc2 Dr Kwang Wu-Kim, president and CEO of Columbia College, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.colum.edu/pdf/irs-form-990.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 received a bonus}}}\uc2  of $300,000 in 2021, on top of a salary of over $799,000, and over $600,000 in additional compensation were given to 14 other executive positions, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.colum.edu/pdf/irs-form-990.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2021 tax documents}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kim said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://vimeo.com/883363568/f3c195df96?share=copy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 video message}}}\uc2  in November that \u8220\'20\'1Cbasically the college is financially sound\u8221\'20\'1D, when downplaying concerns about the college\u8217\'20\'19s finances amid the cuts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe administrators who are in that room making decisions, all their salaries were increasing over the pandemic, it was disgusting,\u8221\'20\'1D added Vallera. \u8220\'20\'1CThe only people harmed by these eliminations to save money due to years of mismanagement and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.colum.edu/strike-information"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $50m building}}}\uc2 , the only people harmed are the most on the margins. They have repeatedly said they can\u8217\'20\'19t stand working with unions. This is union animus heightened. We\u8217\'20\'19re in bargaining and they still felt they could do whatever they want.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since August 2023, the union has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://drive.google.com/drive/u/1/folders/1KTdWcjJaJT1arnJ4E2hJ78TDQBbN8HPC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed}}}\uc2  seven unfair labor practice charges against Columbia College with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ), all of which are still awaiting adjudication.\par \par \uc2 The allegations against the college include refusal to furnish information required for bargaining, making unilateral changes to issues subject to collective bargaining, threatening to or transferring work out of the bargaining unit, soliciting workers to resign from the union and utilizing intellectual property of striking faculty.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we took on the changes, two-thirds of our membership would be gone, because they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have classes any more if we accepted what they originally offered us,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tim McCain, an adjunct theatre professor and working professional at Columbia College, who saw one of his theater classes increase from 20 to 85 students.\par \par \uc2 He said because of significant increases to the size of his classes, he had had to adapt the way he teaches because there are too many students to have a more personalized class with each student.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf this was about money, we\u8217\'20\'19d still be teaching. They would just be negotiating a contract. But this is so much more,\u8221\'20\'1D added McCain.\par \par \uc2 The strike has received {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cfacresources.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 support }}}\uc2  from several local elected officials and students who have refused to cross picket lines and even engaged in walkouts of their own as Columbia College has sought to have adjunct faculty courses filled in by other adjuncts crossing the picket line or by full-time faculty members.\par \par \uc2 Many students have criticized Columbia College\u8217\'20\'19s decision to cut courses and the impact it\u8217\'20\'19s having on their education.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s always the people they swear to protect that end up getting the brunt of it. It\u8217\'20\'19s an expensive college. A lot of people are struggling to afford to be here,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sarah Khairy Nev\u225\'00\'E1rez, a sophomore at Columbia College who said the cuts had impaired students\u8217\'20\'19 abilities to fulfill their graduation requirements on time.\par \par \uc2 Bria Hall, another sophomore at Columbia College, said the cuts were negatively affecting the ability for students to have one-on-one time and relationships with professionals, which was marketed to her in a mailer that ultimately led her to choose to attend Columbia.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C They don\u8217\'20\'19t see us as growing individuals or professionals, they see us as dollar signs. It\u8217\'20\'19s heartbreaking and it\u8217\'20\'19s not fair,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hall. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re going to take our money, at least give us the education we deserve, it makes no sense to me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Columbia College did not comment on the executive bonuses, but noted the union and administration have both agreed to federal mediation and characterized the cuts in classes and consolidations as an adjustment to enrollment and financial difficulties.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt the heart of the work stoppage is the fact that the college is asserting its management right to make decisions to address a financial shortfall. The union has refused to end the strike because it insists on a guarantee of employment for nearly all members of this contingent group of faculty and has sought veto power over class sizes and course offerings,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson for Columbia College said in an email.\par \par \uc2 They added: \u8220\'20\'1CThe union\u8217\'20\'19s demands would jeopardize the college\u8217\'20\'19s long-term sustainability by dictating which measures the college can and cannot use to restore its fiscal health.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231210ejca001jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
