\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US economy adds 199,000 jobs as unemployment rate falls slightly\b0\par\par\uc2 Callum Jones in New York \par \uc2 424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2023\par 14:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jobless rate down to 3.7% as world\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy grapples with interest rates and American policymakers plot next moves\par \par \uc2 The US workforce added 199,000 jobs last month, a robust reading as the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy continues to grapple with higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Employment growth has been fading this year after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  launched an aggressive campaign to pull back inflation from its highest levels in a generation. Official data has bolstered hopes that the central bank will manage to guide the US economy to a so-called \u8220\'20\'1Csoft landing\u8221\'20\'1D, where price growth normalises and recession is avoided.\par \par \uc2 Economists had expected non-farm payrolls to increase by about 180,000 jobs in November, up from a reading of 150,000 in October. Friday\u8217\'20\'19s report still amounts to a deceleration from earlier in the year, and is short of the average reading for 2023.\par \par \uc2 The headline unemployment rate stood at 3.7%, according to data released by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , down from 3.9% the previous month.\par \par \uc2 It comes as policymakers plot the next steps of their campaign, with inflation slipping back towards the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s 2% target. Some investors have expressed hope that the central bank will start cutting rates as soon as next year.\par \par \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , chairman of the Fed, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.federalreserve.gov/mediacenter/files/FOMCpresconf20231101.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stressed}}}\uc2  last month that it was \u8220\'20\'1Ccommitted to achieving a stance of monetary policy that is sufficiently restrictive to bring inflation sustainably down to 2% over time, and to keeping policy restrictive until we are confident that inflation is on a path to that objective\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Vanden Houten\uc2 , lead economist at Oxford Economics, said November\u8217\'20\'19s jobs data was boosted by the end of two large strikes, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/30/uaw-contract-gm-strike-end-update"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 United Auto Workers }}}\uc2  six-week strike and the months-long {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/oct/01/hollywood-writers-strike-end-back-work"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 actions by US writers and actors.}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPayroll gains were inflated by returning strikers in November, but the underlying pace of job growth has slowed in recent months,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s encouraging for the Fed, which has likely ruled out further rate increases. However, the details in the rest of the report were robust enough in our view to keep rate cuts off the table for several more months.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ian Shepherdson\uc2 , chief economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe trend in the unemployment rate is still upwards, consistent with people\u8217\'20\'19s reported nervousness about the labor market, and we have to expect a rebound to new highs over the next few months.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231208ejc800237\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
