\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Cuba health and education hollowed out as staff join emigration exodus\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed Augustin in Havana \par \uc2 1,054 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2023\par 11:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US \u8216\'20\'18maximum pressure\u8217\'20\'19 sanctions have slashed wages for state workers leading to a brain drain hitting the Revolution\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18pillars\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Every morning before dawn, hundreds of people line up outside the US embassy. At midday this particular day, Acelia De La Osa, a 71-year-old retired physics teacher with frizzy hair, bounds out of the sleek modernist building and hugs her daughter. They both laugh with tears in their eyes: Acelia\u8217\'20\'19s got a visa and will soon be in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Her daughter, Ana Delia, the only one of her siblings left on the island, also hopes to get out once her visa comes through. An intensive care nurse, she has seen her salary\u8217\'20\'19s purchasing power \u8211\'20\'13 now the equivalent of just $18 a month \u8211\'20\'13 cut in half over the last three years by inflation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHealth and education are the two toughest sectors in this country,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey pay you little and you work loads.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As well as her tiny wage, she cited the piecemeal education her children now receive as another reason for wanting to leave. With a mass exodus of teachers from schools, there\u8217\'20\'19s nobody to teach her 13-year-old twins maths.\par \par \uc2 State wages in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/cuba"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cuba}}}\uc2  have plummeted in recent years as \u8220\'20\'1Cmaximum pressure\u8221\'20\'1D US sanctions have created massive shortages and stoked runaway inflation. Meanwhile, squeezed public sector workers have more options than before: emigration out of the country is smashing records, while a resurgent private sector hoovers up much of the talent that remains.\par \par \uc2 The upshot is that the state is suffering an unprecedented brain drain: health and education, long seen as the \u8220\'20\'1Cpillars\u8221\'20\'1D of the Revolution, are fraying as qualified personnel empty out.\par \par \uc2 At a school for autistic children in the capital\u8217\'20\'19s Vedado neighborhood, director Elsa Escalona, 72, has lost 13 teachers over the last two years. Four have emigrated to the US \u8211\'20\'13 two with undergraduate degrees, two with master\u8217\'20\'19s degrees. She\u8217\'20\'19s had to increase class sizes from the recommended five to nine students per class.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou feel impotent because there\u8217\'20\'19s no way to convince people to stay,\u8221\'20\'1D said Escalona, who just about gets by on her and her husband\u8217\'20\'19s state salaries. \u8220\'20\'1CSome teachers have cried because they haven\u8217\'20\'19t wanted to leave, but the economic situation is overwhelming \u8211\'20\'13 they feel they need to go.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cuba invests more in health and education than any other Latin American country, and Cubans have for decades benefited from results in these sectors comparable to developed countries. But official figures show the number of doctors, nurses and dentists is falling sharply. In October, the ministry of education announced it was 17,278 teachers short.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, the government allowed Cubans to incorporate businesses and import goods. Since then, the private sector, long reined in by the Communist party, has roared back and now employs more than a third of the island\u8217\'20\'19s workforce, according to economists. Small and medium-sized businesses, which pay many times more than the state and can employ up to 100 people, are guzzling up many of the best and brightest.\par \par \uc2 In addition, a new layer of US sanctions, implemented by Trump and largely kept in place by Biden, have succeeded in their stated aim of starving the Cuban economy of hard currency. Economists say the new measures cost the island\u8217\'20\'19s economy billions of dollars a year. Caked on to a botched currency unification three years ago, the economic damage wrought by the pandemic and a highly dysfunctional planned economy, they have created a perfect storm.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe conditions under which the government is operating are impossible,\u8221\'20\'1D said Emily Morris, a development economist at \uc2 University College London\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThese new sanctions have caused a severe decline in living standards. Given that one of the effects of sanctions is to obstruct the government\u8217\'20\'19s access to international finance, there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing else they can do because they have no foreign exchange cushion.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The government of Ra\u250\'00\'FAl Castro eliminated the need for an exit visa to leave the island in 2013, putting an end to half a century of drastic limitations on travel. Up until then, Cubans had to jump through bizarre bureaucratic hoops to legally migrate: inspectors were often sent to people\u8217\'20\'19s homes to tally up knives, forks and spoons and would only issue papers if cutlery tallied with the number on record.\par \par \uc2 And over the past two years, more than 400,000 Cubans \u8211\'20\'13 about 4% of the island\u8217\'20\'19s population \u8211\'20\'13 have emigrated to the US. To address the crush of migrants on the southern border, the Biden administration this January unveiled a \u8220\'20\'1Chumanitarian parole programme\u8221\'20\'1D through which Cubans, Venezuelans, Nicaraguans and Haitians with a sponsor can apply from their countries to live and work temporarily in the US.\par \par \uc2 More than 50,000 Cubans have already arrived through the programme.\par \par \uc2 Once they\u8217\'20\'19re in the US, Cubans get a \u8220\'20\'1Cjump start\u8221\'20\'1D, according to Susan Eckstein, professor of sociology at \uc2 Boston University\uc2 . Of the four nationalities eligible for the new parole programme, she said, Cubans are the only ones for which it serves as a path to lawful permanent residence. \u8220\'20\'1CCubans also automatically qualify for welfare,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 The relative ease with which Cubans can make it into the US and the unique entitlements they have once they get in, analysts say, are a testament to the power of the Cuban American lobby, which enjoys special influence as it\u8217\'20\'19s rooted in the politically important state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 In a primary school in Havana\u8217\'20\'19s Marianao neighbourhood, teacher Fernando Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez \u8211\'20\'13 who asked to use a pseudonym for fear of reprisals \u8211\'20\'13 is feeling despondent. Free school meals for all students are still holding up, but all seven of the school\u8217\'20\'19s computers are broken and there\u8217\'20\'19s a shortage of pencils, erasers, textbooks \u8211\'20\'13 and teachers.\par \par \uc2 Since term started just three months ago, three of his colleagues have already left.\par \par \uc2 Ideally, he said, he would like to keep working as a teacher: he sees his job as important for future generations and loves how his students become his friends as they grow up.\par \par \uc2 But his monthly salary now only stretches to buy a few dozen eggs on the black market.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy dream,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cis to emigrate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231206ejc6001rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
