\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Family still wait for return of migrant worker\u8217\'20\'19s body after unexplained death on UK-US military base\b0\par\par\uc2 Katie McQue and Mark Townsend \par \uc2 1,439 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 November 2023\par 12:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Relatives allege American contractor on island of Diego Garcia failed to get proper medical help for 33-year-old Saddam Ali\par \par \uc2 A major US defence contractor has failed to repatriate the body of a migrant worker who died under unexplained circumstances on the British-owned island of Diego Garcia more than a month ago, the Observer has learned.\par \par \uc2 Saddam Ali died after a short illness on 18 October, on the secretive joint UK-US military base on the remote British overseas territory in the Indian Ocean. His family allege that \uc2 KBR\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 Ali\u8217\'20\'19s employer \u8211\'20\'13 failed to get the 33-year-old Indian sufficient medical assistance, keeping him on the base, which does not have a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://diegogarcia.tricare.mil/Health-Services/Urgent-Emergency-Care"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hospital-grade}}}\uc2  facility.\par \par \uc2 More than 1,000 migrant workers are contracted to work for \uc2 KBR\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a key Pentagon contractor that helped build Guant\u225\'00\'E1namo Bay in Cuba \u8211\'20\'13 on the military base, which the UK has leased to the US since the 1960s.\par \par \uc2 Strategically located, the atoll of Diego Garcia is crucial for US interests, lying within range of east Africa, the Middle East and Asia. However, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2023/sep/25/tamil-refugees-diego-garcia-win-fight-against-forcible-return-sri-lanka"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 treatment of migrant workers}}}\uc2  on its vast military base has become a source of increasing concern.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was \uc2 KBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s responsibility to evacuate Saddam for better treatment. They should have taken prompt action to evacuate him,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Junaidul Hasan, his cousin. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are not providing any information about why they did not.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a statement to the Observer, \uc2 KBR\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CSaddam reported ill to us Monday, 16 October and we transferred him to the naval doctors and called a medevac [medical evacuation] for him that same day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, Hasan and three \uc2 KBR\uc2  workers claim Ali\u8217\'20\'19s body remains on Diego Garcia, part of the Chagos Islands archipelago. \u8220\'20\'1CThey informed us they would arrange [a medevac] but it was never arranged,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hasan.\par \par \uc2 Ali, who had no underlying health conditions, became sick with a fever and died of a heart attack, \uc2 KBR\uc2  told the family. They have not been provided with any further details about what caused his death. Last night the defence contractor said Ali \u8220\'20\'1Cpassed before it [the medevac] could arrive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Questions over the potential mishandling of Ali\u8217\'20\'19s medical care comes amid allegations that \uc2 KBR\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cmistreating\u8221\'20\'1D its migrant workforce on the base. Ordered by a senior \uc2 Royal Navy\uc2  officer, the inquiry was launched after UK officials suspected \uc2 KBR\uc2  of recruiting undocumented workers for the military base.\par \par \uc2 Last November, \uc2 KBR\uc2  flew three Filipino men, aged 36, 40 and 49, to the tiny British territory from Dubai, where they were believed to be working illegally on tourist visas, according to two \uc2 KBR\uc2  employees.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, a spokesperson for the \uc2 Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office\uc2  (FCDO) said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 UK government\uc2  and the British Indian Ocean Territory [BIOT] administration take any allegations of forced labour or human trafficking extremely seriously.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The FCDO spokesperson said its investigation concluded the men entered Diego Garcia legally but requested the Observer to withhold the names of the UK officers involved in the investigation. The three Filipinos are still working on the base, it is understood.\par \par \uc2 KBR\uc2 , a former subsidiary of controversial oil services giant \uc2 Halliburton\uc2 , which was run for five years by \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  before he became US vice-president, refuted allegations that it recruited undocumented workers and denied the FCDO investigation took place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn late 2022, three employees who were properly documented arrived on the island without having taken their paperwork with them,\u8221\'20\'1D said the spokesperson. \u8220\'20\'1CThe BIOT asked us to look into this, specifically understanding at the time that the three employees may have simply forgotten their paperwork.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, Dr Maruja Asis, director of the Scalabrini Migration Center, in Manila, Philippines, said: \u8220\'20\'1CFor these workers being hired in Dubai and then moving on to Diego Garcia, it is very possible that they may be rendered very vulnerable. The protections that the Philippine government can extend to them can be very limited.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 KBR\uc2 , also known as \uc2 Kellogg Brown & Root\uc2 , is the main contractor on the military base, located in the Chagos Islands. The Chagos Islanders were forcefully removed to make way for the Diego Garcia base and have spent the past five decades in exile in Mauritius, Seychelles and the UK.\par \par \uc2 KBR\uc2  has held the military base\u8217\'20\'19s main operating support services contract since 2017, providing services including facilities management and IT. The Observer has interviewed 14 current and former \uc2 KBR\uc2  employees on Diego Garcia and reviewed documents such as payslips, work contracts, photographs, \uc2 KBR\uc2  presentation slides and communications.\par \par \uc2 All the interviewees requested anonymity. They allege a culture of fear on the base, with managers telling staff there is a zero-tolerance policy for workers who speak out.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 KBR\uc2  is always making us scared about this, and we need work to provide for our families,\u8221\'20\'1D said Maggie*. \u8220\'20\'1CThey always say they can terminate our jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 KBR\uc2  recruited the workers after experiencing short staffing issues when the Philippines government blocked Filipinos from travelling to Diego Garcia to work for \uc2 KBR\uc2  unless they could show they would be earning more than the US minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 In October 2021, the Philippine government demanded that companies on Diego Garcia pay their Filipino workers the US federal minimum wage of $7.25 (\u163\'00\'A35.98) an hour. Other contractors on the base complied with this request, but \uc2 KBR\uc2  did not, triggering tensions with its employees and the government.\par \par \uc2 Regular warnings are issued by the Philippines government against unlicensed recruiters hiring its citizens, stating they risk falling victim to human trafficking schemes and \u8220\'20\'1Cunfair labour conditions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 KBR\uc2  is trying to avoid hiring Filipinos from the Philippines because they\u8217\'20\'19d have to pay the minimum wage. If these workers don\u8217\'20\'19t have the proper documentation, that\u8217\'20\'19s human trafficking,\u8221\'20\'1D said John*, a former \uc2 KBR\uc2  employee who resigned earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Because the Philippine government is blocking workers from travelling to Diego Garcia, workers can\u8217\'20\'19t travel home for vacations and say they are being forced to choose between their job and seeing their children.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re tired and exhausted,\u8221\'20\'1D said Maggie, a mother who has not seen her young children for more than four years because of the pay dispute. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat \uc2 KBR\uc2  is doing to us is inhuman.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To remedy the staffing issues, the \uc2 British government\uc2  recently approved hiring limited numbers of Indian and Kenyan workers. However, three Indian workers claim they have been misled over the salaries they would be paid, alleging they were hired in mid-2022 on an $800 a month package, which was then cut to around $500 a month when their annual contracts were renewed this year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe HR manager said if you want to work, the salary is $500; if you don\u8217\'20\'19t want to work, go back. Many people resigned,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rohan*, an Indian worker in his 20s.\par \par \uc2 A small group of Mauritian employees also claim their compensation packages were recently slashed by almost half, with one worker alleging that \uc2 KBR\uc2  stated it was deducting a $300 per month meal allowance from them.\par \par \uc2 This meal allowance was written into job offer letters as part of the salary compensation package offered to workers, according to documents seen by the Observer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have since invested in culturally appropriate food for non-Filipino employees. Accordingly, when these employees\u8217\'20\'19 contracts expire, we explain that new contracts would provide free meals instead of the food stipends if they want to continue working on the island,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 KBR\uc2  spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 KBR\uc2  pays its employees on Diego Garcia lawful compensation and provides good and safe working conditions. Our Filipino employees choose to work for us because of this,\u8221\'20\'1D the spokesperson added.\par \par \uc2 Workers claim the British and US forces on the base are aware of the pay situation with \uc2 KBR\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Asis added: \u8220\'20\'1CThe UK and US governments should take action. The frontline face of these military bases are British and American, but behind that is an army of workers coming from developing countries who are doing a lot of work, but whose responsibility are they?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 KBR\uc2  has held meetings with its Filipino employees over the situation with one meeting slide showing that the company claims the Philippine government is \u8220\'20\'1Cusing a misplaced concept that Diego Garcia is a US territory\u8221\'20\'1D, and that \u8220\'20\'1Cpaying the US minimum wage would represent a 235% salary increase\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 KBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson told the Observer that the total compensation given to the Filipino workforce exceeds the Philippine government\u8217\'20\'19s mandated hourly rate \u8220\'20\'1Cwhen housing, food, medical coverage, and other benefits are included\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * Names have been changed to protect their identities.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231122ejbj003at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
