\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Pound, shares and bonds rally as US inflation slows to 3.2% \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 7,730 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2023\par 15:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US core inflation has dropped to a two-year low, cheering investors, while more UK firms fall into insolvency last monthUS inflation cools to 3.2% as fuel prices drop and rise in housing costs slows\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Afternoon summary\par \par \uc2 Time to recap\par \par \uc2 Government bond yields have dropped sharply, and stocks have surged on Wall Street, after US inflation fell more than expected.\par \par \uc2 The annual US Consumer Prices Index dropped to 3.2% in October, down from 3.7% in September, a bigger drop than expected.\par \par \uc2 Prices were flat on a monthly basis, as inflationary pressures eased.\par \par \uc2 enltrLooking at major categories of CPI - they are all trending lower. Core services running at 3.2%. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4Mv19iJY1m"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/4Mv19iJY1m}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kathy Jones (@KathyJones) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KathyJones/status/1724422175950565842?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 The data bolstered hopes that the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  will not raise interest rates again, and will start cutting next year.\par \par \uc2 Bond prices jumped, pushing down the yield (or interest rate) on US 10-year Treasuries by aroudn 20 basis points to 4.4%, down from 4.6%.\par \par \uc2 enltr??BREAKING:\par \par \uc2 *U.S. TREASURY YIELDS PLUNGE AFTER WEAK CPI REPORT ADDS TO PEAK RATE BETS\par \par \uc2 ???????? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/RGT9pY4cK8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/RGT9pY4cK8}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Investing.com (@Investingcom) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Investingcom/status/1724440042888687992?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 The dollar also weakened, driving up the pound by 1.8 cents to $1.246, a two-month high.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has rallied strongly, with the S&P 500 index up 1.8% in morning trading.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/14/us-inflation-fuel-housing-cpi-fed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US inflation cools to 3.2% as fuel prices drop and rise in housing costs slows}}}\par \par \uc2 In other news\u8230\'20\'26\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/nov/14/uk-real-wages-inflation-vacancies-fall-insolvencies-us-inflation-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-65533e4d8f08bbf5dbce182d#block-65533e4d8f08bbf5dbce182d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Business insolvencies in England and Wales rose 18% year-on-year in October}}}\uc2 , as firms struggled under the weight of higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 UK real pay has grown at its fastest rate in two years, according to new data which suggests Britain\u8217\'20\'19s labour market has so far emerged largely unscathed from the slowdown in the economy.\par \par \uc2 The latest {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/bulletins/uklabourmarket/november2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 health check}}}\uc2  from the Official for National Statistics found the jobless rate broadly unchanged, a small fall in employment and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/nov/14/uk-real-wages-inflation-vacancies-fall-insolvencies-us-inflation-business-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strongest rise in pay}}}\uc2  adjusted for inflation in two years, as the pace of prices rises comes down.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2023/11/pay-growth/giv-13425d1Sr1LuDKEsd/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\par \par \uc2 The ONS said the UK\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment rate in the three months to September had remained steady at 4.2% while the employment rate over the same period had dropped by 0.1 points to 75.7%.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/14/uk-jobs-market-unemployment-rate-real-wages-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK jobs market dodges hit from economic slowdown}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/14/jeremy-hunt-staff-shortages-autumn-statement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jeremy Hunt needs to act on staff shortages in autumn statement}}}\par \par \uc2 The UK boss of \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  has told MPs that widespread cases of sexual harassment and bullying at its fast food shops are \u8220\'20\'1Ctruly horrific\u8221\'20\'1D and that the company is doing everything it can to eradicate them.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/14/mcdonalds-harassment-allegations-uk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s harassment and bullying cases \u8216\'20\'18truly horrific\u8217\'20\'19, says UK boss}}}\par \par \uc2 The world\u8217\'20\'19s biggest offshore wind developer, Denmark\u8217\'20\'19s \u216\'00\'D8rsted, has lost two of its most senior executives after it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/nov/01/rsted-cancels-two-us-offshore-windfarm-projects-at-33bn-cost"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abandoned a pair of windfarm projects off the US coast}}}\uc2  at a cost of more than \u163\'00\'A33bn.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/14/wind-developer-orsted-bosses-exit-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wind developer \u216\'00\'D8rsted bosses exit after \u163\'00\'A33bn-plus failure}}}\par \par \uc2 One of Britain\u8217\'20\'19s biggest property developers is shifting its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk/london"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 London}}}\uc2  portfolio from the City of London to the West End as tenants want to be in vibrant locations with easy access to shopping and leisure activities.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/14/landsec-city-of-london-west-end-bluewater-trinity-leeds"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Landsec shifts focus from City of London to West End as tenants seek \u8216\'20\'18vibrancy\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Ministers have come under further pressure to expand the financial support for Britons struggling with the cost of living crisis, after a committee of MPs found some had \u8220\'20\'1Cslipped through the safety net\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/14/cost-of-living-support-mps-committee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ministers urged to expand cost of living support as MPs find Britons \u8216\'20\'18slipping through net\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  cutting 500 jobs in Kent\par \par \uc2 Back in the UK, around 500 jobs are to go at pharmaceuticals giant \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Kent site.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.sky.com/story/500-jobs-to-go-as-pfizer-discontinues-some-kent-operations-13008242"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sky News reports}}}\uc2  that the roles are to be cut as \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is discontinuing its pharmaceutical sciences small molecule (PSSM) operations at Sandwich in Kent, the company confirmed to Sky News.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s part of a company-wide cost-cutting program, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Affected staff were notified on Tuesday morning and consultation with these employees is under way.\par \par \uc2 Update: A \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  spokesperson says:\par \par \uc2 As previously announced, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  has launched an enterprise-wide cost realignment program. Various areas of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s global enterprise are making changes to operate more efficiently and effectively. These changes will be implemented on a rolling basis and will differ area to area.\par \par \uc2 One of the consequences of this program is a plan to consolidate our Pharmaceutical Sciences Small Molecule (PSSM) capabilities, aligned around our portfolio priorities. Therefore, the company has made the difficult decision to propose a discontinuation of our PSSM operations at Sandwich, Kent, impacting approximately 500 roles. Under the proposals, other functions at our Sandwich site will continue with a different size.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Full story: US inflation cools to 3.2%\par \par \uc2 The rate of US consumer price rises cooled in October amid lower gasoline prices and slowing increases in housing costs.\par \par \uc2 The annual rate of inflation measured by the Consumer Price Index (CPI) \u8211\'20\'13 which tracks the cost of a basket of goods and services \u8211\'20\'13 slowed to 3.2% last month, lower than the 3.7% reading in September and the coolest rate since July.\par \par \uc2 In another encouraging sign, over the month prices remained unchanged after rising by 0.4% in September. Core prices, a measure that excludes the volatile food and energy sectors, were up 4%, also a bit slower than in September.\par \par \uc2 US inflation has fallen from a 40-year high of 9.1% in June 2022. Despite the trend, the Biden administration has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/15/biden-economy-bidenomics-poll-republicans-democrats-independents"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polled badly}}}\uc2  on its handling of the economy.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Biden celebrated the news.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday we saw more progress bringing down inflation while maintaining one of the strongest job markets in history,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m working to get results for the American people and it\u8217\'20\'19s happening \u8211\'20\'13 and I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to let up for one second.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The energy index decreased 4.5% for the 12 months ending October. Prices for used cars, which shot up after the pandemic, and airline tickets also fell. Housing costs have also been a big driver of US inflation. The shelter index increased 0.3% in October, down from 0.6% the previous month.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/14/us-inflation-fuel-housing-cpi-fed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/14/us-inflation-fuel-housing-cpi-fed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US inflation cools to 3.2% as fuel prices drop and rise in housing costs slows}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Pound hits two-month high\par \par \uc2 Sterling is continuing to strengthen against the US dollar.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s now up by 1.7 cents, or 1.4%, to $1.2450, a two-month high.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wall Street opens higher\par \par \uc2 Stocks are rallying hard on Wall Street at the start of trading, as investors celebrate the drop in US inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average has gained 1%, or 349 points, to 34,687 points.\par \par \uc2 Technology stocks are rallying strongly, pushing the Nasdaq Composite index up by 2%.\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24NVDA&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $NVDA}}}\uc2  +1.8% after the open. On track for 10th straight day of gains. That would be longest stream of gains going back to Dec 2016. Trading at a record high.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ed Ludlow (@EdLudlow) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/EdLudlow/status/1724436263015461314?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Traders appear confident that US interest rates are at their peak, and expecting rate cuts next year.\par \par \uc2 Ronald Temple, chief market strategist at \uc2 Lazard\uc2 , explains:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe immaculate disinflation continues.\par \par \uc2 Over the last 5 months, core CPI has annualized at a rate of 2.8% as subsiding shelter inflation and falling core goods prices reduce pressure on consumers.\par \par \uc2 Absent any exogenous shocks, the Fed is increasingly likely to be in a position to cut rates in the second quarter of 2024.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The chief economist of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  has warned that UK wages are growing too fast to be consistent with its 2% inflation target.\par \par \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  said this morning\u8217\'20\'19s labour market data showed a weakening of pay growth, but cautioned:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe did have this morning the latest official data on pay growth in the UK with pay growing at 7.7%. And that\u8217\'20\'19s a little bit off where it was.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Speaking at a discussion about central banks at the Bristol Festival of Economics 2023, Pill added:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut actually over the summer pay growth has remained very strong and we certainly wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t see pay growth of that rate as consistent with achieving the 2% inflation target on an ongoing basis.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The next UK inflation report, due at 7am tomorrow, is expected to show a sharp fall in the annual CPI index, from 6.7% to around 4.8%.\par \par \uc2 Pill said the \uc2 BoE\uc2  was watching for a fall to \u8220\'20\'1Caround 5%\u8221\'20\'1D, but added:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNonetheless, 5% is still much too high.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Halifax, First Direct and HSBC UK cutting mortgage rates\par \par \uc2 Back in the UK, several major lenders have announced new mortgage rate cuts, widening the choice for borrowers searching for deals under the 5% mark.\par \par \uc2 First Direct\uc2  announced rate cuts of up to 0.40 percentage points from Tuesday, PA Mediia reports.\par \par \uc2 Halifax also announced cuts to its mortgage rates by up to 0.46 percentage points from Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 This includes cutting a five-year fix for borrowers with a 10% deposit by 0.24 percentage points to reach 4.97%.\par \par \uc2 The lending giant will also be reducing a five-year fix for borrowers with a 40% deposit by 0.20 percentage points, to 4.53%. Both Halifax deals have a \u163\'00\'A3999 fee.\par \par \uc2 HSBC UK is also expected to make widespread reductions to its mortgage rates on Wednesday. The details have not yet been announced.\par \par \uc2 First Direct\uc2  said it is the most substantial round of mortgage rate drops it has made since February this year.\par \par \uc2 It has also launched two new mortgages for borrowers with a 5% deposit.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The underlying trend is that US inflation returning to near normal, reports economics professor \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  of the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrInflation's receding, baby.\par \par \uc2 Headline inflation was literally zero -- on average, prices were the same in October as September.\par \par \uc2 Over the year, headline inflation is 3.2%, beating expectations of 3.3%.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  (@JustinWolfers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JustinWolfers/status/1724426447975063853?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe decline in headline inflation is partly due to falling gas prices.\par \par \uc2 Want to know where inflation is going?\par \par \uc2 You're better off focusing on core inflation (ex food and energy), which was 0.2% last month, and 4.0% over the past year. Again, better than the markets expected.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  (@JustinWolfers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JustinWolfers/status/1724426929120428084?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrPoint is, the press reports on inflation *over the past year*, and core inflation over the past year is 4.1%.\par \par \uc2 But that includes a lot of stale data from many months ago.\par \par \uc2 Focus on the most recent few months, and you'll see core inflation is running at around a 3% annual rate.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  (@JustinWolfers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JustinWolfers/status/1724428260262412654?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe past five core inflation readings have been remarkably good:\par \par \uc2 June: +0.2%\par \par \uc2 July: +0.2%\par \par \uc2 August: +0.3%\par \par \uc2 Sept: +0.3%\par \par \uc2 Oct: +0.2%\par \par \uc2 If monthly inflation is 0.2% half the time, and 0.3% the other half, that yields an annualized inflation rate of three percent.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  (@JustinWolfers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JustinWolfers/status/1724427868770332900?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrInflation is such a closely watched indicator that last month's mild uptick led to a lotta hand-wringing.\par \par \uc2 This month better number easily undoes that.\par \par \uc2 Reality: Data are noisy\par \par \uc2 The underlying trend is inflation returning to near normal.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Justin Wolfers\uc2  (@JustinWolfers) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JustinWolfers/status/1724429044500803613?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.05pm GMT\par \par \uc2 enltrBREAKING! US Headline {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/inflation?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #inflation}}}\uc2  dropped to 3.2% in October, lower than expected.\par \par \uc2 Core {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/CPI?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #CPI}}}\uc2  dropped to 4.0%, also lower than expected. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/jTWSyozbBR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/jTWSyozbBR}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 jeroen blokland (@jsblokland) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/jsblokland/status/1724419621619367939?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s US inflation data is a further signal that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s work on interest rates is probably done, says Lindsay James, investment strategist at \uc2 Quilter Investors\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 James says:\par \par \uc2 Although core inflation is currently declining only slowly, there are increasing signs this will speed up in early 2024 amidst a softening economic backdrop, and so whilst central bankers seem keen to end all talk of future rate cuts, they are likely to be disappointed on this front. Managing the message around the path of interest rates may become trickier should the data appear to be more positive than expected.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe release highlighted the impact of falling energy prices, something that will be mirrored across other advanced economies, with the energy component down 4.5% year-on-year in October and down 2.5% month on month. However shelter inflation, which constitutes one of the largest components of US CPI, remained stubbornly high, rising 0.3% month on month.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the path back down to target is going to be a long and arduous one, and may just give the Fed enough cover to ignore the calls for rate cuts for now, no matter how noisy they get.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrCPI, the long view {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/wNxCxbewJU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/wNxCxbewJU}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Axel Merk\uc2  (@AxelMerk) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AxelMerk/status/1724424786242052532?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures have surged, with the Dow Jones industrial average set to jump over 1% when trading begins in under 40 minutes.\par \par \uc2 enltrUS Oct 23 CPI cooler than expected.\par \par \uc2 Oct 2023 CPI @ 3.2% lower than the 3.7 percent in September 2023.\par \par \uc2 Good news for global markets. Dow, NASDAQ, and S&P Futures are up. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/UgbSui2C0k"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/UgbSui2C0k}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Investors Cafe: NISM Certified Research Analyst (@ValueIn84502221) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ValueIn84502221/status/1724424542875975731?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Soft landing 'looks likely' as US inflation falls\par \par \uc2 US inflation continues \u8220\'20\'1Con a downward trend\u8221\'20\'1D, says Richard Garland, chief investment strategist at Omnis Investments, adding:\par \par \uc2 This should reaffirm the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s view that interest rates are restrictive enough to bring inflation back to target, albeit they will keep rate hikes on the table until a low 2% inflation handle heaves into view.\par \par \uc2 lthough a comforting inflation reading is still some distance away, the labour market is weakening and economic growth is set to slow as consumers reign in their spending; a soft landing for the economy still looks most likely, but downside surprises in economic growth could change the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s focus next year.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Richard Flynn, managing director at C harles Schwab UK, says today\u8217\'20\'19s drop in US inflation means the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  is less likely to raise interest rates higher.\par \par \uc2 Flynn explains:\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s figures show that the rate of inflation has fallen compared to last month, making the prospect of a soft landing ever more likely. The numbers revealed today indicate the Fed is inching closer to its 2% CPI target.\par \par \uc2 The drop in inflation suggests that recent monetary policy has been doing its job. This good news reinforces the likelihood that the Central Bankers will hold off from further rate hikes in this cycle, which is the direction they seem to be leaning in any case. With wage growth slowing and sectors such as manufacturing losing pace, there is a risk that further tightening could trigger a problem in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Higher rates in the US would also make it more expensive to borrow in US dollars, creating difficulties for emerging-market economies that do so. All in all, \u8220\'20\'1Chigher for longer\u8221\'20\'1D looks like a much more sensible move than \u8220\'20\'1Ceven higher\u8221\'20\'1D.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Government bond prices are also strengthening, on relief that US inflation fell more sharply than expected last month.\par \par \uc2 This has pushed down the yield (or interest rate) on US Treasuries, and also on European government bonds.\par \par \uc2 enltrBetter than expected US CPI inflation numbers with headline flat (month-on-month) and core up 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 This compares to the consensus forecasts of 0.1% and 0.3%, respectively.\par \par \uc2 The market reactions include a sharp fall in yields (currently 12 bps for the 10 year), a weaker dollar,\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Mohamed A. El-Erian\uc2  (@elerianm) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/elerianm/status/1724421557060067446?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 enltrUS CPI inflation came in at 3.2% YoY.\par \par \uc2 Still hovering just above the 2% target.\par \par \uc2 Rate CUTS expected during 2024. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/W2F3huFMrx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/W2F3huFMrx}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Josh (@CryptoWorldJosh) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CryptoWorldJosh/status/1724422070191177848?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Dollar slides as inflation falls\par \par \uc2 The dollar is tumbling, after US inflation fell more quickly than expected last month.\par \par \uc2 This has pushed the pound up by a whole cent, to $1.239, the highest in a week.\par \par \uc2 The euro has also gained against the dollar, up nearly a cent to $1.079.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US inflation falls to 3.2%\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: Inflation in the United States has dropped by more than expected, as price pressures continue to ease.\par \par \uc2 The US consumer prices index rose by 3.2% in the year to October, below the 3.3% expected, and down from the 3.7% recorded in September.\par \par \uc2 Core inflation, which strips out food and energy, fell to 4.0%, the lowest since September 2021.\par \par \uc2 Energy prices fell by 4.5% over the last year, while food inflation was 3.3%.\par \par \uc2 On a monthly basis, CPI was unchanged in October compared with September, a slowdown on the 0.4% rise recorded a month ago.\par \par \uc2 This may reassure America\u8217\'20\'19s central bankers that they are winning in their battle to bring down inflation, meaning that further interest rate increases may not be needed.\par \par \uc2 enltrUS October CPI: Further evidence of disinflation inside the October inflation report. Inflation flat m/m & 3.2% y/y. Core up 0.2% & 4% over that same time frame.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Joseph Brusuelas\uc2  (@joebrusuelas) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/joebrusuelas/status/1724419640154079453?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UK\u8217\'20\'19s Serious Fraud Office\uc2  has carried out a series of dawn raids and arrests in connection with the collapse of law firm group Axiom Ince.\par \par \uc2 The SFO arrested seven individuals and raided nine sites around Bedfordshire on Tuesday morning, the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  reports, as it announced a formal investigation into allegations of fraud at Axiom and \u163\'00\'A366mn of missing client money.\par \par \uc2 The SFO\u8217\'20\'19s action comes almost seven weeks after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/legal/legalindustry/uk-police-launch-investigation-into-law-firm-axiom-ince-2023-09-28/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 British police launched}}}\uc2  a criminal investigation into Axoim, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lawgazette.co.uk/news/axiom-ince-owner-admits-64m-is-gone/5117204.article"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  admitted in mid-September that \u163\'00\'A364m of client money has largely disappeared}}}\uc2 , seemingly used to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thebusinessdesk.com/yorkshire/news/2113682-former-law-firm-boss-admits-using-client-money-to-fund-takeovers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fund several takeovers.}}}\par \par \uc2 In August, three Axiom Ince directors were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lawgazette.co.uk/news/sra-suspends-directors-of-axiom-saviour-of-ince-and-plexus/5116974.article"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suspended}}}\uc2  by \uc2 the Solicitors Regulation Authority\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SFO director Nick Ephgrave, who began his role in September, has told the FT that the operation highlighted a new approach to investigations under his leadership.\par \par \uc2 Ephgrave says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe accepted the investigation on September 5 and a few weeks later we\u8217\'20\'19re going through doors, gathering evidence and making arrests, and that\u8217\'20\'19s the kind of approach I\u8217\'20\'19m encouraging here.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 We have \u8220\'20\'1Cgone from flash to bang quite quickly\u8221\'20\'1D, he added.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 In the currency markets, the rouble has hit its highest level against the US dollar since August.\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s currency strengthened to 90.6 roubles to the dollar, extending a recent rally since the start of October (when the rouble weakened through 100 to the dollar).\par \par \uc2 The rouble has benefited from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/27/russia-raises-interest-rate-to-15-after-steep-climb-in-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  recent interest rate increases}}}\uc2 , as \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  has tried to support the currency.\par \par \uc2 Moscow has also imposed additional currency control, restricting western companies that sell their Russian assets from taking the proceeds in dollars and euros, which also helped the rouble.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bus workers to strike, but BBC dispute resolved\par \par \uc2 Bus workers are to stage a series of strikes in a dispute over pay.\par \par \uc2 Members of the \uc2 Rail, Maritime and Transport union (RMT)\uc2  at Stagecoach East Midlands will walk out for 48 hours from November 27 and for the same period from December 4, 11 and 18 after voting in favour of industrial action.\par \par \uc2 RMT general secretary \uc2 Mick Lynch\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe huge endorsement for strike action by our \uc2 Stagecoach\uc2  members reflects the growing disgust among bus workers at being taken for granted while their pay continues to fall in real terms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CManagement need to come up with a reasonable offer that deals with the poverty pay and poor conditions in the industry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHowever, if \uc2 Stagecoach\uc2  bosses remain intransigent, our members are fully prepared to take further industrial action on and into the new year if necessary.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But peace has broken out at the BBC, where journalists have voted overwhelmingly to accept a deal on jobs and programming, ending a long-running dispute.\par \par \uc2 Members of the \uc2 National Union of Journalists\uc2  (\uc2 NUJ\uc2 ) in BBC Local backed the deal by 70% after previously taking industrial action.\par \par \uc2 Paul Siegert, \uc2 NUJ\uc2  broadcasting organiser, said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is an overwhelming result in our long-running dispute at BBC Local. We\u8217\'20\'19ve gained significant safeguards on jobs and income protection for \uc2 NUJ\uc2  members, along with new concessions on radio news bulletins and shared programming.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s Georgia Quenby, Partner at City law firm Fladgate, on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/nov/14/uk-real-wages-inflation-vacancies-fall-insolvencies-us-inflation-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-65533e4d8f08bbf5dbce182d#block-65533e4d8f08bbf5dbce182d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 today\u8217\'20\'19s insolvency data}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 The October 2023 insolvency statistics show a continuation of the post-pandemic trend of a significant increase in the numbers of CVLs, a doubling of the number of CVAs from 11 to 23 and a further increase in administrations from 125 to 146 as against September 2022.\par \par \uc2 For context, these 23 CVAs represent more than 4 times as many CVAs than October 2022. At 146, the number of company administrations is 36% higher than October 2022, but this is a slight slowing of the rate of increase in administrations bringing the curve much more into line with historic averages.\par \par \uc2 The CVLs are likely to reflect a continuing \u8220\'20\'1Cclear-out\u8221\'20\'1D of small businesses which have ceased trading across a whole range of sectors. But the increase in CVAs and administrations bringing the numbers more in line with 2019 and prior years is probably reflective of the continued availability of new money to support businesses which can be restructured and continue trading.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 The \u8216\'20\'18slow crush\u8217\'20\'19 of persistently high interest rates are pushing businesses of all kinds to the point of failure, say {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.stewartslaw.com/people/alex-jay/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alex}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.stewartslaw.com/people/alex-jay/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jay}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.stewartslaw.com/people/tim-symes/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tim}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.stewartslaw.com/people/tim-symes/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Symes}}}\uc2  , partners at law firm Stewarts.\par \par \uc2 enltrAs the latest statistics from The Insolvency Service reveal that registered company insolvencies in October 2023 were 18% higher than in the same month in 2022, partners Alex Jay and Tim Symes analyse the challenges facing the economy.\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/JYFIJIQPH3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/JYFIJIQPH3}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Stewarts (@StewartsLawLLP) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/StewartsLawLLP/status/1724385399533867293?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 Although business insolvencies are climbing, personal insolvencies dropped last month.\par \par \uc2 The Insolvency Service reports that there were 9,881 individual insolvencies across England and Wales in October.\par \par \uc2 That is 6% lower than in October 2022, although an increase on September when 7,280 personal insolvencies were filed.\par \par \uc2 A chart showing individual insolvencies Photograph: Insolvency Service\par \par \uc2 Joanne Wright, managing director at \uc2 Kroll\uc2 , says these personal insolvency statistics are \u8220\'20\'1Can ongoing conundrum\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Despite the spike in interest rates and the ongoing cost of living crisis, the overall numbers are declining which is not what anyone would expect to see.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe one understandable statistic is the sharp incline in debt relief orders. Borrowers with no assets and debt levels of less than \u163\'00\'A330,000 seem to be using this procedure instead of Individual Voluntary Arrangements (IVAs). This could be seen as a positive step as there have recently been some questions around how appropriate IVAs are for smaller borrowers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.22am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.51am GMT\par \par \uc2 Businesses are being \u8220\'20\'1Cbattered from all sides\u8221\'20\'1D, warns Nicky Fisher, President of R3, the UK\u8217\'20\'19s insolvency and restructuring trade body.\par \par \uc2 Following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/nov/14/uk-real-wages-inflation-vacancies-fall-insolvencies-us-inflation-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-65533e4d8f08bbf5dbce182d#block-65533e4d8f08bbf5dbce182d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the rise in insolvencies}}}\uc2 , Fisher explains:\par \par \uc2 Costs have increased, demands for wages are incoming and people are spending less as they look to save ahead of the winter and to make sure they have enough left to cover the basics.\par \par \uc2 If the Christmas trading period doesn\u8217\'20\'19t bring a wave of new income, we could see insolvencies continue to rise in the new year, and at the moment, it\u8217\'20\'19s impossible to predict whether this will be a badly needed boost or the final blow for struggling firms.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 enltrThe pressure on small businesses right now is off the scale, so the massive jump in insolvencies, up 18% in October, isn't surprising.\par \par \uc2 Next week's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/AutumnStatement?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #AutumnStatement}}}\uc2  must deliver for small businesses, the backbone of the economy. There is no time to waste. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/bD6KN6JTYg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/bD6KN6JTYg}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Candy Richards (@FSBEastAnglia) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FSBEastAnglia/status/1724372390736478288?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.45am GMT\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://app.newspage.media/mather-and-murray-financial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Samuel Mather-Holgate}}}\uc2 , independent financial advisor at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://app.newspage.media/mather-and-murray-financial"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mather and Murray Financial}}}\uc2  , blames the government and the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  for the rise in insolvencies:\par \par \uc2 The legacy of Sunak won\u8217\'20\'19t be halving inflation, it will be the pain and suffering he and Andrew Bailey have inflicted on British households.\par \par \uc2 The amount of people and businesses unable to cope with rising levels of debt and higher interest is yet to be seen, but these figures indicate how rapidly things are turning bad.\par \par \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  may be able to declare victory in his pledge to halve inflation this year tomorrow, when the latest inflation data is released.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 We have confirmation that the eurozone is halfway into recession.\par \par \uc2 GDP across the single currency region fell by 0.1% in July-September, new data released by statistics body \uc2 Eurostat\uc2  shows. That matches the first estimate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/oct/31/eurozone-economy-french-growth-slows-italy-portugal-inflation-bp-profits-business-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released at the end of October}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The data confirms that the German economy shrank by 0.1% in July-September, while France grew by 0.1% and Italy stagnated.\par \par \uc2 If the eurozone shrinks in the current quarter (October-December), then the eurozone would be in a technical recession.\par \par \uc2 enltrEuro area {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/GDP?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #GDP}}}\uc2  -0.1% in Q3 2023, +0.1% compared with Q3 2022: flash estimate from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Eurostat?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Eurostat}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/7YufkCo68N"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/7YufkCo68N}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/0i5uH21VT0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/0i5uH21VT0}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 EU_\uc2 Eurostat\uc2  (@EU_\uc2 Eurostat\uc2 ) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/EU_Eurostat/status/1724366572179882016?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/10/uk-economy-flatlines-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The UK economy, for contrast, didn\u8217\'20\'19t grow in the quarter}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 David Kelly, Head of Insolvency at \uc2 PwC\uc2 , says smaller companies are bearing the brunt of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/nov/14/uk-real-wages-inflation-vacancies-fall-insolvencies-us-inflation-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-65533e4d8f08bbf5dbce182d#block-65533e4d8f08bbf5dbce182d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rise in insolvencies in England and Wales}}}\uc2  last month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CContinuing supply chain pressures, energy costs, inflation and the high cost of debt mean that many corporate balance sheets remain fragile - particularly those of smaller owner-managed companies. These continue to bear most of the pain, with 98% of insolvencies in October being for companies with turnover of less than \u163\'00\'A31 million.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlthough there was a 58% increase in companies with more than \u163\'00\'A31 million turnover going into an insolvency process in October 2023 compared with October 2022, the numbers are still relatively low, with less than 50 companies filing for an insolvency process in this turnover range. Engineering, business services, and hospitality and leisure continue to be the sectors facing the most pressure, with 70% of insolvencies coming from these four sectors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLooking ahead, unfortunately we expect the number of insolvencies to remain high in the final months of this year, with no sector immune from the prevailing external challenges.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.00am GMT\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2023/nov/14/uk-real-wages-inflation-vacancies-fall-insolvencies-us-inflation-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-65533e4d8f08bbf5dbce182d#block-65533e4d8f08bbf5dbce182d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The jump in insolvencies last month}}}\uc2  is sparking more calls for \uc2 Jeremy Hunt\uc2  to help businesses, in next week\u8217\'20\'19s autumn statement\par \par \uc2 Nick O\u8217\'20\'19Reilly, director of restructuring and recovery at accountancy firm MHA, says the chancllor should introduce an overhaul to business rates and policies to stem spiralling insolvencies:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe government must act swiftly to stem the rising tide of insolvencies and stimulate growth. The current plight of hospitality and retail businesses demands urgent government intervention through a comprehensive overhaul of the business rate system.\par \par \uc2 The government must create a system that is based on turnover rather than property values. A minimum fixed rate that increases based on turnover would mean that retailers and local authorities would both be winners when a business does well and ensure they are not overburdened in downturns.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHMRC also desperately requires increased funding to streamline its financial debt recovery processes. The existing Time to Pay system is a postcode lottery as to whether a firm will secure an agreement to repay its debts or face a winding up petition. Businesses need a more cohesive approach from HMRC so they have greater certainty when it comes to managing finances.\par \par \uc2 To stimulate growth, a multifaceted approach is needed. This should include the reduction of red tape on exports, lowering interest rates and implementing a tax stimulus. Businesses yearn for policies that instil hope and foster a conducive environment for sustained growth. It is high time the government implemented measures that resonate with the needs of businesses and propel the economy forward.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 Insolvencies up 18% in October\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: the number of companies falling into insolvency in England and Wales has jumped again.\par \par \uc2 There were 2,315 registered company insolvencies in October, which is 18% higher than in October 2022 (when there were 1,954), a worrying sign for the UK economy as it enters the winter.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s also an 18% increase on a monthly basis, as there were 1,969 insolvencies in September.\par \par \uc2 UK companies have been hit by high interest rates, and weak consumer spending as households have been hit rising prices.\par \par \uc2 The increase in company insolvencies was driven mostly by Creditors\u8217\'20\'19 Voluntary Liquidations (CVLs) in which company directors opt to wind up an insolvent company which has no prospect of recovery.\par \par \uc2 There were 256 compulsory liquidations, 1,889 creditors\u8217\'20\'19 voluntary liquidations (CVLs), 146 administrations, 23 company voluntary arrangements (CVAs) and one receivership appointment, the Insolvency Service says.\par \par \uc2 Compulsory liquidations, CVLs, CVAs and administrations were all higher than in October 2022.\par \par \uc2 David Hudson, restructuring advisory partner at business advisory firm FRP, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInflated input costs and higher interest rates have pushed many firms over the edge in recent weeks. However, this data is the result of pressure built up in businesses over months and years, so the likelihood is that we haven\u8217\'20\'19t seen the true extent of this wave of insolvencies yet.\par \par \uc2 Hudson adds that management teams will need to keep a close eye on signs of distress in their supply chains.\par \par \uc2 Rising insolvencies increases the risk of a knock-on effect in supply chains that could, in the worst cases, pull them under too.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.21am GMT\par \par \uc2 IEA\uc2  raises oil growth forecasts\par \par \uc2 In the energy sector, the \uc2 International Energy Agency\uc2  (\uc2 IEA\uc2 ) has raised its oil demand growth forecasts for this year and next.\par \par \uc2 In its latest monthly report, the \uc2 IEA\uc2  says world oil demand continues to exceed expectations, although demand growth could slow next year.\par \par \uc2 It says:\par \par \uc2 In this Report, we have slightly revised up our 2023 growth forecast to 2.4 million barrels per day (mb/d), as US deliveries proved more resilient than indicated by preliminary data and Chinese oil demand in September set another all-time high above 17 mb/d, fuelled by a booming petrochemical sector.\par \par \uc2 Global oil demand is set to rise to a record annual high of 102.9 mb/d in 2024, the \uc2 IEA\uc2  estimates, even though there are signs of weaker growth in major economies.\par \par \uc2 Demand is expected to fall in the \uc2 OECD\uc2  next year.\par \par \uc2 On the production side, the \uc2 IEA\uc2  says world oil supply is \u8220\'20\'1Cfirmly on an upward trajectory\u8221\'20\'1D, adding:\par \par \uc2 World oil supply growth is also exceeding expectations. Fears that the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  would escalate into a wider regional conflict, disrupting oil supply flows, have yet to materialise.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 IEA\uc2  warns that the oil market could experience more volatility, saying:\par \par \uc2 With demand growth set to slow, the market could shift into surplus at the start of 2024.\par \par \uc2 For now, with demand still exceeding available supplies heading into the Northern Hemisphere winter, market balances will remain vulnerable to heightened economic and geopolitical risks \u8211\'20\'13 and further volatility ahead.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s little in today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report to encourage the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to change interest rates, higher or lower, argues \uc2 Martin Beck\uc2 , chief economic advisor to the EY ITEM Club.\par \par \uc2 Beck explains:\par \par \uc2 The latest labour market release signalled a stable UK jobs market. The jobless rate was unchanged from Q2 to Q3 and while pay growth slowed a little, it was still strong by past standards.\par \par \uc2 But judging what the data means for monetary policy isn\u8217\'20\'19t clear cut.\par \par \uc2 On the quantities side, today\u8217\'20\'19s release was again lacking the usual Labour Force Survey (LFS) data. And the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) has been sceptical about the story told by the official pay series, although the latest numbers are more in line with other measures. But overall, labour demand and pay growth appear to be cooling, albeit only slowly, consistent with interest rate cuts not being on the agenda for a while yet.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 NIESR, the economic research institute, predicts that wage growth will remain historically elevated in the final quarter of this year.\par \par \uc2 Paula Bejarano Carbo, associate economist at NIESR, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAverage weekly earnings, excluding bonuses, grew by 7.7 per cent in the third quarter of 2023 and by 7.9 per cent if we include bonuses \u8211\'20\'13 though the latter figure is affected by one-off civil service bonus payments.\par \par \uc2 While both figures remain high relative to their respective historical average growth rates of 3.2 and 3.1 per cent, when we adjust for inflation, today\u8217\'20\'19s figures drop to 1.3 and 1.4 per cent, highlighting the extent to which elevated inflation continues to harm households.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s experimental data suggest that the UK unemployment rate was largely unchanged on the quarter at 4.2 per cent, while vacancies fells by 58,000 in the three months to October; these data indicate that the labour market continues to loosen at a slow pace, leading us to expect that wage growth will remain historically elevated in Q4.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltr??OUT NOW?? Our latest monthly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Wage?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Wage}}}\uc2  Tracker suggest that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/LabourMarket?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #LabourMarket}}}\uc2  is continuing to loosen at a slow pace, and we therefore expect that wage growth will remain historically elevated also in the last quarter of the year ????\par \par \uc2 More here ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/vwKfigxlJY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/vwKfigxlJY}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/dLJHYqNIPm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/dLJHYqNIPm}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 National Institute of Economic and Social Research (@NIESRorg) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NIESRorg/status/1724399576012984435?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 Victoria Scholar, head of investment at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ii.co.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interactive investor}}}\uc2 , says there are signs of slack in today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs market figures:\par \par \uc2 While the headline unemployment rate remains unchanged, there are some signs of slack in the labour market with a fall in job vacancies across 16 out of 18 industry sectors, highlighting the cautiousness among businesses across the economy towards hiring.\par \par \uc2 Plus the UK employment rate decreased by 0.1 percentage points during the quarter to 75.7%.\par \par \uc2 However wage growth remains very strong, with real pay rising at the fastest rate in two years partly driven by the fall in inflation.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 Resolution: Outlook for jobs is murky\par \par \uc2 Nye Cominetti\uc2 , principal economist at the \uc2 Resolution Foundation\uc2 , has dug into today\u8217\'20\'19s data and repoorts that \u8220\'20\'1Cpay growth looks to be cooling rapidly in the private sector\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Cominetti adds:\par \par \uc2 A rapid cooling will reassure the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  that rising rates are having their desired affected, though for workers it could mean that the recent period of rising real wages is ending soon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CData limitations mean that the outlook for jobs murky. While vacancies keep falling, there\u8217\'20\'19s no sign yet of a big rise in unemployment that some people fear.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.09am GMT\par \par \uc2 Wage figure are 'good news' for \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  will be encouraged that pay growth slowed in the last quarter, reports \uc2 James Smith\uc2 , developed markets economist at ING.\par \par \uc2 It might encourage the \uc2 BoE\uc2  to conclude that it has now raised interest rates high enough to bring inflation down to target.\par \par \uc2 Smith says:\par \par \uc2 The latest UK wage figures are generally good news from the perspective of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 . Private sector regular pay growth, which strips out volatile bonus payments, inched lower to 7.8% from 8.1% on a year-on-year basis. This is one of the three key metrics the Bank has said it\u8217\'20\'19s looking at as a guide for policy.\par \par \uc2 Admittedly, that\u8217\'20\'19s still far too high and inconsistent with a 2% inflation target. But equally, the news is better if you look at pay in level terms. Private sector pay was unchanged in the most recent month, as the chart below shows.\par \par \uc2 That means if you compare the most recent three-month period to the three months prior, the rate of growth has slowed a lot. These figures do bounce around, but it looks like a signal that momentum is slowing.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.58am GMT\par \par \uc2 TUC General Secretary Paul Nowak says the UK economy is in the \u8220\'20\'1Crelegation zone\u8221\'20\'1D, adding;\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnemployment remains 225,000 higher than a year ago with vacancies continuing to decline.\par \par \uc2 Working people are far worse off than they were when the Conservatives took office.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf pay packets had been growing at pre-crisis levels, workers would be on average nearly \u163\'00\'A315,000 better off. And across parts of the economy, real wages are still shrinking.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Conservatives\u8217\'20\'19 economic mismanagement is costing people their jobs and livelihoods. It\u8217\'20\'19s time for change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 IoD\uc2  concerned by long-term sickness\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Institute of Directors\uc2  is concerned that the rise in long-term sickness is making it harder for firms to recruit staff.\par \par \uc2 Alexandra Hall-Chen, Principal Policy Advisor for Employment at the \uc2 IoD\uc2 , explains:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s data shows that while the number of vacancies has fallen slightly, the labour market remains very tight and businesses are still struggling to hire the people they need.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur own data shows that business leaders continue to cite skills and labour shortages as having a negative impact on their organisation, second only to more general concerns about the UK economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHistorically high levels of economic inactivity due to long-term sickness remain a cause for concern. The government should use next week\u8217\'20\'19s Autumn Statement to support businesses to invest more in occupational health provision by expanding the range of occupational health services covered by Benefit in Kind exemptions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/apr/27/britain-poor-record-health-costs-economy-43bn-year-long-term-sickness-earnings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s poor record on health costs economy \u163\'00\'A343bn a year, says report}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 Liz Kendall\uc2  MP, Labour\u8217\'20\'19s Shadow Work and Pensions Secretary, says today\u8217\'20\'19s labour market figures show \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Tories\u8217\'20\'19 failure on the economy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur employment rate still hasn\u8217\'20\'19t got back to pre pandemic levels, unlike every other G7 country, and a record number of people remain locked out of work due to long term sickness. This is terrible for individuals and their families, especially during a cost of living crisis, and for taxpayers too as the benefits bill continues to soar.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLabour\u8217\'20\'19s plan to get Britain working will tackle the root causes of economic inactivity by driving down \uc2 NHS\uc2  waiting lists, reforming social security, making work pay and supporting people into good jobs across every part of the country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 Public sector wages are growing at the fastest rate in at last 22 years, the ONS reports.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report says:\par \par \uc2 Annual average regular pay growth for the public sector was 7.3% in July to September 2023 and is the highest regular annual growth rate since comparable records began in 2001; for the private sector this was 7.8% and among the largest annual growth rates seen outside of the coronavirus (COVID-19) pandemic period.\par \par \uc2 Including bonuses, public sector pay rose by 8.6%.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 Jeremy Hunt\uc2  (still the chancellor after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2023/nov/13/reshuffle-that-brought-david-cameron-back-is-likely-to-be-last-before-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s reshuffle}}}\uc2  ) says he\u8217\'20\'19s heartened that real wages are growing.\par \par \uc2 He also drops a hint that next week\u8217\'20\'19s autumn statement will include new measures to target employment:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s heartening to see inflation falling and real wages growing, keeping more money in people\u8217\'20\'19s pockets.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBuilding on the labour market reforms in Spring, the Autumn Statement will set out my plans to get people back into work and deliver growth for the UK.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 Company payrolls keep rising\par \par \uc2 Company payrolls continued to grow last quarter.\par \par \uc2 The number of payrolled employees in the UK is estimated to have increased by 33,000 in October, to 30.2 million.\par \par \uc2 September\u8217\'20\'19s figures have been revised too \u8211\'20\'13 to show an increase of 32,000, not the decrease of 11,000 first reported.\par \par \uc2 enltrFrom HMRC\u8217\'20\'19s PAYE RTI data, the number of employees on the payroll rose by 33,000 in October 2023.\par \par \uc2 This was 398,000 up on the year and 1.19 million above its pre {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/COVID19?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #COVID19}}}\uc2  pandemic level.\par \par \uc2 ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Muy9Dna2A5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/Muy9Dna2A5}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/LUvIj3BzDg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/LUvIj3BzDg}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1724321298350850400?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State for Work and Pensions, \uc2 Mel Stride\uc2  MP says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are leaving no stone unturned to help people into work, with a record number of employees on payrolls - up nearly 400,000 in the last year alone with 3.9 million more people in work than in 2010.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith our ambitious welfare reforms, including the expansion of free childcare and extra support for people with health conditions, we are taking long term decisions that will grow the economy and change lives for the better.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Vacancies drop again, but real wage growth hits two-year high\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Vacancies at UK firms are continuing to fall as the economy struggles to grow, while real pay is rising at the fastest rate in two years.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s two key points from the latest survey of the UK labour market, just released by the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2 , which shows little change in unemployment, employment, or economic activity in the last three months.\par \par \uc2 The ONS reports that vacancies fell for the 16th time in a row, dropping by 58,000 in August-October compared with May-July, as companies cut back on hiring.\par \par \uc2 Vacancy levels fell in 16 of the 18 industry sectors, led by professional, scientific, and technical activities.\par \par \uc2 But workers are continuing to see their wage packets rise faster than inflation, thanks to strong pay growth and the drop in inflationary pressures this summer.\par \par \uc2 The ONS reports that pay growth slowed a little, with regular pay (excluding bonuses) growing by 7.7% in July to September 2023 (down from 7.9% last month).\par \par \uc2 Total pay rose by 7.9% (down from 8.2%), bolstered by one-off bonus payments to civil service staff this summer.\par \par \uc2 Adjusted for CPI infation, that means that total pay rose by 1.1% on the year and regular pay rose by 1.0% on the year, the fastest since autumn 2021.\par \par \uc2 enltrNew: slowdown in the labour market continues with position largely unchanged from last quarter - vacancies continue to fall (960,000), unemployment unchanged at 4.2% but pay up 7.9%, rising 1% in real terms v CPI, the fastest in two years\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Paul Kelso (@pkelso) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/pkelso/status/1724326875135127743?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrRegular pay (excluding bonuses) was 7.7% in July to September 2023, slightly down on the previous periods but still among the highest annual growth rates since comparable records began in 2001.\par \par \uc2 ?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/kNF3QWQo2X"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/kNF3QWQo2X}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/3QfzvmIL01"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/3QfzvmIL01}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1724321294055796746?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 The ONS has been having problems producing its usual labour market report, due to low response rates. So instead it has produced an alternative set of estimates, which show:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 the UK employment rate decreased by 0.1 percentage points on the quarter to 75.7%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 the UK unemployment rate was largely unchanged on the quarter at 4.2%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 the UK economic inactivity rate was largely unchanged on the quarter at 20.9%.\par \par \uc2 Darren Morgan, ONS director of economic statistics, said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur labour market figures show a largely unchanged picture, with the proportions of people who are employed, unemployed or who are neither working nor looking for a job all little changed on the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe number of job vacancies fell for the 16th straight month. Nevertheless, vacancies still remain well above their pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith inflation easing in the latest quarter, real pay is now growing at its fastest rate for two years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrCommenting on today\u8217\'20\'19s figures, ONS Director of Economic Statistics {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DarrenM44?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @DarrenM44}}}\uc2  said: (1/3)???? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/gNRrlJysCN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/gNRrlJysCN}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1724321281670054128?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrDarren went on to say: (2/3)???? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/tfzdWjHugp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/tfzdWjHugp}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1724321285956612230?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrHe concluded by saying: (3/3)???? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/EpPxrf3B7b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/EpPxrf3B7b}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1724321290041860184?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 14, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Reaction to follow\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 We also get the latest UK insolvency figures this morning, an update on eurozone growth, and then US inflation data this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am GMT: \uc2 IEA\uc2  oil market report\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am GMT: US monthly insolvency statistics for October\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am GMT: Eurozone GDP report (second estimate)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am GMT: ZEW index of eurozone economic sentiment\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm GMT: US inflation report for October\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231114ejbe000xg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
