\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 The actors\u8217\'20\'19 strike is all but over. What do we know about the deal?\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 1,270 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par 21:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 For 118 days, Hollywood actors were striking for a better deal and now there is a tentative end in sight\par \par \uc2 The longest work stoppage for film and television actors is all but over, as the Sag-Aftra union {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/nov/08/hollywood-actors-strike-ends-sag-aftra"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced a tentative deal}}}\uc2  with studios to end a months-long strike on Wednesday night. The deal, which will end the 118-day strike that ground Hollywood to a halt and caused considerable financial pain for the nearly 2 million Americans who work in jobs directly or indirectly related to production, was hailed by union leadership as well as the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  (\uc2 AMPTP\uc2 ) as a historic breakthrough, with notable agreements on minimum pay, streaming-based bonuses and consent and compensation guarantees for the use of AI likenesses.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the actors union, like the writers union a month before it, secured major concessions from studios \u8211\'20\'13 a \u8220\'20\'1Ccapitulation by Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s biggest companies\u8221\'20\'1D, as the \uc2 New York Times{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/11/08/business/media/actors-strike-deal.html?smid=nytcore-ios-share&referringSource=articleShare"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2 , and a stark reversal for studio heads who assumed the unions would be \u8220\'20\'1Crelatively compliant\u8221\'20\'1D in reaching a deal.\par \par \uc2 For both actors and writers, the negotiations represented a once in a blue moon opportunity to address what were seen as existential threats to their careers. Namely, the future of AI in Hollywood and the brutal economics of the floundering streaming economy, which could deliver massive audience hits \u8211\'20\'13 such as \uc2 Netflix\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Orange is the New Black or Suits, the old USA Network series that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2023/aug/17/suits-netflix-america-most-watched-summer-show"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 broke streaming records}}}\uc2  on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  this summer \u8211\'20\'13 with little compensation for creatives.\par \par \uc2 There are still i\u8217\'20\'19s to be dotted and t\u8217\'20\'19s to be crossed before Hollywood is fully up and running again \u8211\'20\'13 union leadership will vote to ratify the deal on Friday and it still needs likely approval by its 160,000-strong membership \u8211\'20\'13 but the future of a post-strike Hollywood is becoming clear. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what we know so far:\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s the deal with AI?\par \par \uc2 As it was for the writers, AI emerged as one of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jul/22/sag-aftra-wga-strike-artificial-intelligence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 toughest sticking points}}}\uc2  for actors, particularly after Duncan Crabtree-Ireland, \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief negotiator, said this summer that studios proposed a day\u8217\'20\'19s pay to use extras\u8217\'20\'19 digital likenesses in perpetuity, without consent and compensation. Disagreements over AI rights was the primary hurdle to the studios\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Clast, best and final offer\u8221\'20\'1D presented on Sunday to Sag-Aftra; the union pushed back on a clause that would have allowed studios to pay to use high-earning performers\u8217\'20\'19 likenesses after their death without consent by their estates or Sag-Aftra, according to union sources speaking to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/sag-aftra-ai-protections-for-high-earning-members-sticking-point-1235638247/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Hollywood Reporter}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Studios {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2023/biz/news/sagaftra-strike-ai-amptp-contract-1235783065/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ultimately capitulated}}}\uc2  on this demand, which seems to have been the tipping point in negotiations. It is still unclear exactly what AI protections the actors union secured, though Crabtree-Ireland, in an interview with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rollingstone.com/tv-movies/tv-movie-features/sag-aftra-chief-negotiator-breaks-down-strike-ending-deal-ai-protections-streaming-residuals-1234874112/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rolling Stone}}}\uc2 , confirmed that the deal requires studios to obtain consent from actors for specific use of their AI likenesses. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a really robust set of protections for performers that I think will not only protect them during the trial of this contract, but for many years thereafter,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. Fran Drescher, the union\u8217\'20\'19s president, told CNN: \u8220\'20\'1CNow [studios] have to ask for permission for everything.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The writers union {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/oct/01/hollywood-writers-strike-artificial-intelligence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 secured similarly groundbreaking protections}}}\uc2  with their contract in September, which allows writers to use AI with permission, but establishes guardrails to prevent AI from replacing human jobs or labor. Both contracts will shape {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/aug/21/ai-jobs-hollywood-writers-actors-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s already burgeoning relationship}}}\uc2  with generative AI for years to come.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s in the rest of the deal?\par \par \uc2 On compensation, the new contract, which is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2023/biz/news/sag-aftra-streaming-bonus-40-million-1235784773/#recipient_hashed=58e80303eb86ef6e1590496f3313a95182cb7e47e4431739bdb854b95b0768c7&recipient_salt=7e749829dd8fdacaf3affd4f51643a323740a6b6883a45b85c04133fdf95e512"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly worth $1bn over three years}}}\uc2 , secured \u8220\'20\'1Cabove pattern\u8221\'20\'1D minimum wage increases. The guild initially asked for an 11% increase in the first year; Crabtree-Ireland did not reveal the exact number but told Rolling Stone the union \u8220\'20\'1Cachieved a number that is significantly above\u8221\'20\'1D the standard 5%.\par \par \uc2 The union was unsuccessful, however, in securing a share of revenue from each streaming platform. Drescher had made this a top priority, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2023/biz/news/fran-drescher-interview-sag-strike-bob-iger-vaccines-1235672987/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arguing}}}\uc2  that it was essential to \u8220\'20\'1Cget into the pocket\u8221\'20\'1D of streaming revenue in order for actors to sustain a livelihood in the streaming era. The studios vowed the union\u8217\'20\'19s proposal \u8211\'20\'13 2% of revenue, later cut to 1% or about $500m a year \u8211\'20\'13 would not happen, and it did not.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the union settled for a streaming bonus arrangement modeled on terms secured by the writers guild. Under the new deal, actors on a \u8220\'20\'1Csuccessful\u8221\'20\'1D streaming show will receive 100% of their existing residual as a bonus. \u8220\'20\'1CSuccessful\u8221\'20\'1D means a show which attracts views equivalent to 20% of a company\u8217\'20\'19s subscriber base within 90 days. However, actors will only receive a portion of that money. The rest will go to a fund jointly administered by employers and the union for distribution to actors on a range of streaming shows, not just the most popular ones.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Chybrid\u8221\'20\'1D model is supposed to work similarly to the potential revenue-sharing proposal, though with much less money to distribute. Union leadership has expressed disappointment over the concession, but Crabtree-Ireland told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2023/biz/news/sag-aftra-streaming-bonus-40-million-1235784773/#recipient_hashed=58e80303eb86ef6e1590496f3313a95182cb7e47e4431739bdb854b95b0768c7&recipient_salt=7e749829dd8fdacaf3affd4f51643a323740a6b6883a45b85c04133fdf95e512"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Variety}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cthe net result will be a wider distribution of money to more members\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s next?\par \par \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2  leadership will first need to approve the deal in a meeting on Friday \u8211\'20\'13 all but guaranteed, given that the negotiating committee unanimously recommended its passage. More information about the contract will be available after that vote. Union members will then have several weeks to vote to ratify the deal, which again seems all but guaranteed given the strain and frustration after nearly four months out of work. Drescher and Crabtree-Ireland were under mounting pressure to reach a deal from agents, crew members and even union members, including such high-profile names as George Clooney and Ben Affleck.\par \par \uc2 What does this mean for viewers?\par \par \uc2 In the near future, not that much in terms of theatrical content, as studios have already pushed a number of completed big-name titles \u8211\'20\'13 Denis Villeneuve\u8217\'20\'19s Dune: Part Two, Luca Guadagnino\u8217\'20\'19s Challengers \u8211\'20\'13 to 2024. Production {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/nov/09/actors-strike-end-production-deadpool-wonka"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 should be able to resume}}}\uc2  quickly for a number of tentpole films whose release dates in 2024 and beyond were in flux, including the two-part Wicked film adaptation, Deadpool 3 and Venom 3, as well as fan-favorite TV shows such as Abbott Elementary, Yellowjackets and The White Lotus.\par \par \uc2 But the strike\u8217\'20\'19s end does mean your favorite celebrities will be back on the promotional trail and just in time for awards season. Good news for Bradley Cooper\u8217\'20\'19s Maestro, in which he both directs and stars as Leonard Bernstein, as well as the cast of \uc2 Oppenheimer\uc2  and Barbie, the summer blockbusters seeking awards clout. Late-night shows will be able to book actors to speak directly about their projects; Timoth\u233\'00\'E9e Chalamet, already scheduled to host Saturday Night Live this weekend through a network code agreement, will now be free to actually mention that he\u8217\'20\'19s starring in Wonka, which is still slated for a Christmas season theatrical release.\par \par \uc2 Red carpets and press junkets, down for four months with the exception of individual films given union waivers (Sofia Coppola\u8217\'20\'19s Priscilla, The Hunger Games: A Ballad of Songbirds & Snakes), will return for holiday season releases. And the dates for next year\u8217\'20\'19s major award shows, starting with the Golden Globes on 7 January 2024, will go on as planned. The 2023 Emmys, which were originally scheduled for mid-September but delayed by the joint strikes, will take place on 15 January 2024.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231109ejb9003h3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
