\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 WeWork\uc2 , once a $47bn firm, files for bankruptcy after accruing $2.9bn debt\b0\par\par\uc2 Blake Montgomery and Callum Jones in New York \par \uc2 663 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2023\par 17:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Company never recovered from 2019 ouster of founder \uc2 Adam Neumann\uc2  and remote work revolution\par \par \uc2 WeWork\uc2  filed for chapter 11 bankruptcy on Monday in New Jersey, according to a statement from the company.\par \par \uc2 The beleaguered company, once valued at $47bn on the private market, endured a 98% decline in its share price this year, leaving it with a market capitalization of less than $50m. In August, it raised \u8220\'20\'1Csubstantial doubt\u8221\'20\'1D that it could continue to operate as it grappled with $2.9bn in net long-term debt and more than $13bn in long-term leases. The company said in a statement it had entered into a restructuring support agreement and would deal with the debt by \u8220\'20\'1Caddressing our legacy leases and dramatically improving our balance sheet\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Company leadership also asserted that most WeWorks would remain open for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 WeWork\uc2  spaces remain open and operational and we will continue to provide our members with the exceptional experience they have come to expect,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 WeWork\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s statement reads. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 WeWork\uc2  is here to stay and we plan to remain in the vast majority of buildings as we move into the future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 WeWork\uc2  were suspended on Monday as Wall Street braced for the company to file for bankruptcy, following reports that it was planning to do so.\par \par \uc2 According to the company\u8217\'20\'19s statement, it has entered a restructuring support agreement with stakeholders to drastically reduce its existing funded debt. As part of the filing, \uc2 WeWork\uc2  will request the ability to reject the leases of certain, largely non-operational, locations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe remain committed to investing in our products, services and world-class team of employees to support our community,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 David Tolley\uc2 , CEO of \uc2 WeWork\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 WeWork\uc2  has a strong foundation, a dynamic business and a bright future.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The business never quite recovered from the ouster of its founder, \uc2 Adam Neumann\uc2 , who resigned in September 2019 amid a push to go public, and the remote work revolution of the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The attempt to list the company on the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  as \uc2 the We Company\uc2  in 2019 included the filing of a revealing prospectus with the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  that raised questions over its long-term viability, profitability and leadership. The company would not go public until 2021. Neumann received a $445m payout package on his exit.\par \par \uc2 Before the bankruptcy filing, Neumann issued a statement: \u8220\'20\'1CThe company\u8217\'20\'19s anticipated bankruptcy filing is disappointing. It has been challenging for me to watch from the sidelines since 2019 as \uc2 WeWork\uc2  has failed to take advantage of a product that is more relevant today than ever before. I believe that, with the right strategy and team, a reorganization will enable \uc2 WeWork\uc2  to emerge successfully.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Founded in 2010, the company\u8217\'20\'19s business involves taking long-term leases on office buildings and selling short-term memberships to offices geared towards co-working. The company was once valued at $47bn on an investment of $12.8bn, primarily from the Japanese multinational SoftBank. After the publication of its S-1 prospectus, however, analysts valued the company at $10bn.\par \par \uc2 WeWork\uc2  shuttered dozens of its co-working spaces in response to pandemic lockdowns, when remote working came to dominate co-working.\par \par \uc2 Its commercial real estate portfolio remains vast, however, with about 777 locations across 39 countries as of June. These housed 906,000 desks, according to the company. (Guardian US leases space from \uc2 WeWork\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 WeWork\uc2  raced to adapt to a post-Covid world, seeking to position itself as a specialist provider of flexible office space as businesses and their employees weighed how, and where, to work. It remained deep in the red, however, and lost $696m in the first half of this year.\par \par \uc2 Neumann, 44, has already started a new venture. Flow, which raised $350m from the Silicon Valley venture capital firm \uc2 Andreessen Horowitz\uc2  last year, is focused on residential real estate.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231107ejb7000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
