\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US jobs report weaker than expected; UK economy \u8216\'20\'18flirting with recession\u8217\'20\'19, economists say \u8211\'20\'13 business live\b0\par\par\uc2 Julia Kollewe \par \uc2 5,043 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2023\par 13:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US economy adds 150,000 jobs in October and previous months are revised lower, boosting investor expectations that the Fed won\u8217\'20\'19t hike interest rates again\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wall Street shares are expected to rise at the open, after the weaker than expected non-farm payrolls data, which showed slowing job growth and a surprise uptick in the unemployment rate to 3.9%. This boosted investor expectations that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  won\u8217\'20\'19t hike interest rates again.\par \par \uc2 You can find the \uc2 US Labor Department\uc2  figures {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/newsroom/economicdata/empsit_11032023.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jay Hatfield, chief executive at Infrastructure Capital Management, said:\par \par \uc2 It is consistent with the views of the market that the job market and the economy is decelerating and that\u8217\'20\'19s going to keep the Fed on hold and will cause central banks next year to cut rates.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more instant reaction. Daniel Casali, chief investment strategist at the wealth management group \uc2 Evelyn Partners\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 While there is increasing evidence that US job creation is slowing, it is unlikely to change the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s plan to keep interest rates \u8216\'20\'18higher for longer\u8217\'20\'19 to address inflation in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, overall employment is still expanding by around 2% per annum and that should provide support for consumption growth. Other measures of labour activity are also indicative of a relatively healthy labour market. For instance, weekly initial job claims are a timely signal for the unemployment trend. Given this was recently reported at an historically low level, this implies there are fewer workers seeking unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, the September reading for job openings (from the JOLTS survey) came in at 9.6 million, versus the pre-pandemic level of around 7m at the end of 2019. This shows that firms are still looking to hire. Indeed, the Manpower survey rebounded in the fourth quarter to show that a net 36% of firms were planning to hire in the 3 months, compared to 20% at the end of 2019.\par \par \uc2 Importantly, average hourly earnings from the payroll report are slowing and that should give the Fed some comfort that wage-driven inflation is coming under control. Though recent bumper wage deals agreed between the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 labour union and major auto manufacturers is a risk to the overall inflation outlook.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US factory jobs declined by 35,000 last month while government jobs increased by 51,000. There were also job gains in health care and social assistance.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Hunter\uc2 , deputy chief US economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 The muted 150,000 gain in non-farm payrolls in October is another sign that the economy\u8217\'20\'19s strength in the third quarter is likely to unwind in the fourth. With wage growth also continuing to slow, it is increasingly hard to imagine the Fed hiking interest rates any further.\par \par \uc2 Admittedly, recent strike action hit payrolls again last month, with 33,200 \uc2 UAW\uc2  (\uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ) workers walking out during the October survey period. That helps explain the 35,000 fall in manufacturing payrolls, which will be reversed in November with the autoworkers\u8217\'20\'19 strike now over.\par \par \uc2 That said, strikes can\u8217\'20\'19t explain the declines in employment in transportation & warehousing, information and finance. Furthermore, payrolls are still being supported by growth in the non-cyclical government and health care & social assistance sectors \u8211\'20\'13 excluding those, payrolls rose by just 22,000 last month.\par \par \uc2 Finally, after a blip in September, the persistent pattern of downward revisions to past months has returned \u8211\'20\'13 with August and September payroll gains revised down by a cumulative 101,000.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW union\uc2  struck deals with the car manufacturers Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  in the past week, ending the car worker strikes.\par \par \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  members walk the picket line at the Ford Michigan Assembly Plant in Wayne, Michigan, in September. Photograph: Paul Sancya/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US government\uc2  bonds rallied after the data, which showed employers hired fewer people than expected in October.\par \par \uc2 This pushed bond yields lower (yields move inversely to prices). The yield, or interest rate, on benchmark 10-year Treasuries fell 11 basis points to 4.55%, the lowest in three weeks. Two-year yields dropped 10 bps to 4.87%, the lowest since early September.\par \par \uc2 European bonds also rallied, as did sterling and the euro, rising 0.8% against the dollar to $1.2305 and $1.0714 respectively.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 enltrBREAKING: Non-farm payrolls come in at just 150K vs last week's reading of 336K ??\par \par \uc2 The forecast was for 178K\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate is 3.9%\par \par \uc2 This comes after strong JOLTS data, a weak ADP reading, and unemployment claims staying low {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/JWkCySfNj8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/JWkCySfNj8}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Markets & Mayhem (@Mayhem4Markets) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Mayhem4Markets/status/1720418424419938632?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US jobs report weaker than expected\par \par \uc2 The US economy added 150,000 jobs in October, fewer than expected, and previous months were revised lower, suggesting the economy is not quite as strong as thought.\par \par \uc2 Economists had expected 180,000 new jobs. In September, employers hired 297,00 more people, less than the 336,000 previously reported, and in August, there were 165,000 new private sector jobs, revised down from 227,000.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate ticked up to 3.9% from 3.8% in September.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which measures the greenback against a basket of other major currencies, fell to a 10-day low.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 UK regulator closes investigations into Facebook Marketplace and Amazon Marketplace\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s competition watchdog has closed its separate investigations into \uc2 Meta \u8217\'20\'19s Facebook\uc2  Marketplace service and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  marketplace, accepting commitments from both tech companies.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Competition and Markets Authority{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/cma-secures-improvements-in-ways-amazon-and-meta-treat-competitors-benefitting-customers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s commitments \u8220\'20\'1Chelp ensure that third-party Marketplace sellers can compete on a level-playing field and that UK customers get access to the best deals\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  welcomed the move.\par \par \uc2 We welcome the CMA\u8217\'20\'19s decision to close its investigation into Marketplace on the basis of the commitments offered by \uc2 Meta\uc2  to put in place systems and controls designed to confirm and validate that advertiser data from competitors is not used in Marketplace.\par \par \uc2 Ann Pope, senior director for antitrust enforcement at the CMA, said:\par \par \uc2 We have accepted \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s commitments as they help thousands of independent UK sellers to compete on a level playing field against \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s own retail arm. This should also mean customers get access to the best product offers.\par \par \uc2 The commitments secured from \uc2 Meta\uc2  mean the firm cannot exploit advertising customers\u8217\'20\'19 data to give itself an unfair advantage \u8211\'20\'13 and as such distort competition.\par \par \uc2 The Meta \uc2 Facebook\uc2  application Marketplace for buying and selling people's goods online on a smartphone screen. Photograph: Stephen Frost/Alamy\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 Economists say UK economy 'flirting with recession'\par \par \uc2 Tim Moore\uc2 , economics Director at S&P Global Market Intelligence, which compiles the UK services survey, said:\par \par \uc2 A shallow downturn in UK service sector activity persisted in October as businesses struggled to make headway against a backdrop of worsening domestic economic conditions and stretched household budgets.\par \par \uc2 Forward-looking survey indicators suggested that service providers will continue to skirt with recession. The degree of optimism towards the business outlook was the lowest in 2023 so far, despite relief that interest hikes have taken a pause this autumn.\par \par \uc2 Samuel Tombs\uc2 , chief UK economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics, said:\par \par \uc2 The composite \uc2 PMI\uc2  suggests that the economy is flirting with a mild recession, but we continue to think that one will be narrowly avoided.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 Unemployment rose slightly in the eurozone in September, according to the latest official figures.\par \par \uc2 The jobless rate rose to 6.5% from 6.4% in August, with just over 11 million people out of work including 2.2 million young people (under the age of 25), {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/documents/2995521/17791746/3-03112023-AP-EN.pdf/7de0add9-2fd1-b656-b90d-63aaa97f72ff?version=1.0&t=1698766018969"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the EU\u8217\'20\'19s statistics office Eurostat said}}}\uc2 . The youth unemployment rate ticked up to 14%. Across the EU, the overall unemployment rate was stable at 6%.\par \par \uc2 Youth unemployment in Europe Photograph: \uc2 Eurostat\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 Martin Beck\uc2 , chief economic advisor to the EY ITEM Club forecasting group, said:\par \par \uc2 Although October\u8217\'20\'19s final services \uc2 PMI\uc2  came in slightly higher than September\u8217\'20\'19s reading, it still signalled a modest contraction in private sector activity. The EY ITEM Club thinks GDP should still grow in Q4, helped by a fading drag from strike action in the public sector, but the pace of growth is likely to be marginal.\par \par \uc2 The Monetary Policy Committee will have had sight of October\u8217\'20\'19s survey in arriving at its November interest rate decision. The picture of weak activity, easing cost pressures and falling employment painted by the latest survey will likely have contributed to the committee\u8217\'20\'19s decision to keep Bank Rate unchanged again.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 Optimism at UK service firms sinks to lowest this year\par \par \uc2 A closely watched survey has shown a slight reduction in service sector output as lacklustre demand conditions continued in October, and optimism worsened to the lowest level so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Services firms experienced another reduction in business activity, although the downturn was only marginal and slightly less marked than in September, according to the final reading from the monthly S&P Global and \uc2 CIPS\uc2  purchasing managers\u8217\'20\'19 index survey.\par \par \uc2 Survey respondents typically cited cost of living pressures, high interest rates and weak consumer confidence as factors holding back customer demand. Job shedding continued in October, reflecting lower new orders and uncertainty about the business outlook. The degree of optimism among services companies regarding year ahead growth prospects was the lowest in 2023 so far.\par \par \uc2 At 49.5 in October, the headline business activity index was up slightly from 49.3 in September and above the earlier \u8216\'20\'18flash\u8217\'20\'19 reading of 49.2. However, it remained below the crucial 50.0 no-change threshold for the third month running. Lower volumes of service sector output in recent months contrast with moderate growth earlier this year (the index averaged 53.3 during the first half of 2023).\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 Banks must guard against 'larger and faster' bank runs, says \uc2 BOE\uc2 's Hauser\par \par \uc2 Regulators need to ensure that banks have adequate financial buffers as advances in technology increase the risk of bank runs, a senior \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  official has warned.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Hauser\uc2 , the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s executive director for markets, said challenges facing central banks included\par \par \uc2 how to ensure that banks\u8217\'20\'19 liquidity insurance remains appropriate as technology change increases the risk of larger and faster deposit runs, of the kind seen this spring in the US.\par \par \uc2 The sudden collapse of the Californian lender \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  in March caught regulators by surprise, as people rushed to get their money out using online banking services.\par \par \uc2 In a speech at a conference organised by King\u8217\'20\'19s College in London, Hauser said central banks need to assess where their balance sheets should settle, after expanding them massively over 15 years of emergency bond buying,\par \par \uc2 as monetary policy makers return inflation \u8211\'20\'13 which remains far too high \u8211\'20\'13 to target, through a combination of higher interest rates and unwinding Quantitative Easing (QE) and other \u8216\'20\'18unconventional\u8217\'20\'19 policy interventions.\par \par \uc2 A third challenge is:\par \par \uc2 how to ensure the stability of the financial system as a whole in the face of the growing incidence of systemic liquidity shocks, not just in banks but increasingly in non-bank market finance too.\par \par \uc2 You can read Hauser\u8217\'20\'19s full speech {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/speech/2023/november/andrew-hauser-keynote-speech-bank-of-england-watchers-conference"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  in the City of London. Photograph: Neil Hall/\uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 In a separate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fao.org/worldfoodsituation/csdb/en/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2 , the FAO stuck to its forecast for world cereal production of 2.8bn metric tonnes this year, up 0.9% from 2022.\par \par \uc2 Global wheat production in 2023 is forecast at 785.1m tonnes, 2.2% (18m tonnes) lower than last year\u8217\'20\'19s level.\par \par \uc2 Turning to 2024, winter wheat plantings are underway across the northern hemisphere and area growth is expected to be limited, reflecting softer crop prices this year.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, drought conditions have partially dissipated in key producing states, and with above-average rainfall forecast for the next months, weather conditions appear to be more favourable for early stages of the 2024 crop; plantings have progressed at an average pace as of October.\par \par \uc2 In the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , comparatively dry and warm conditions are favouring sowing of the winter wheat crop, with plantings already nearing completion in northern countries.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine, the continuing effects of the war, including constrained access to fields and low farm-gate prices, along with less-than-ideal weather conditions, are seen engendering a reduction in the wheat area.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 The FAO Meat Price Index fell 0.6%, the fourth monthly decline, leaving it 3.4% below its value a year ago.\par \par \uc2 In October, international pig meat prices fell for a third month, driven by persistently sluggish import demand, especially from some East Asian countries, while some leading suppliers had high exporting capabilities.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, world poultry meat prices rebounded slightly, as avian flu outbreaks continued to constrain supplies from several world leading suppliers and consumer demand stayed robust, because poultry is more affordable than other meat.\par \par \uc2 International beef and lamb prices also increased slightly, reflecting robust import demand from some leading importers, despite ample supplies of beef from Australia and Brazil and lamb from Oceania.\par \par \uc2 The FAO Sugar Price Index dropped 2.2% in October after two consecutive monthly increases, but remained 46.6% above the level a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 The decline was mainly driven by strong production in Brazil, despite the negative impact of rains on sugarcane crushing in the first half of October. The weakening of the Brazilian real against the US dollar and lower ethanol prices in Brazil also weighed on world sugar prices. However, persistent concerns over a tighter global supply outlook in the recently started 2023/24 season, together with shipment delays from Brazil, capped the declines in world sugar prices.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 Thinning maize supplies in Argentina placed upward pressure on world maize prices, but this was capped by higher seasonal supplies in the United States, where the harvest progressed, and strong export competition from Brazil, the FAO said.\par \par \uc2 As for vegetable oils, a slight fall in that price index mainly reflected lower world palm oil prices, more than offsetting higher prices of soy, sunflower and rapeseed oils.\par \par \uc2 The FAO Dairy Price Index rose 2.2% in October from September, following nine months of consecutive declines, but was still down 20.1% from its value a year ago. World milk powder prices increased the most, mainly driven by surges in import demand for both near- and longer-term supplies, especially from Northeast Asia.\par \par \uc2 Tight milk supplies in Western Europe and some uncertainty over the impact of the El Ni\u241\'00\'F1o weather conditions on the upcoming milk production in Oceania added further downward pressure on prices.\par \par \uc2 Global butter prices also rose, boosted by increased sales ahead of the winter holidays in Western Europe and higher import demand from Northeast Asia. By contrast, international cheese prices dropped slightly as the euro weakened against the dollar, and because of higher exports from Oceania.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 Global food prices continue to drop, but at a slower pace\par \par \uc2 Global food prices continue to drop, but at a slower pace, with prices for sugar, cereal, vegetable oil and meat down while dairy products went up in price.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UN\uc2  food price index fell 0.5% in October from September to 120.6 points, its lowest level since March 2021. It was 14.8 points, or 10.9%, below its level a year ago.\par \par \uc2 The slight drop in October reflects declines in the price indices for sugar, cereals, vegetable oils and meat, while the index for dairy products rebounded, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fao.org/worldfoodsituation/foodpricesindex/en/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) of the United Nations said}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wheat prices fell by 1.9% in October because of higher-than-expected supplies in the US and strong competition among exporters, and rice prices dropped 2%.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  says investors face 5.5% long-term borrowing costs\par \par \uc2 The world\u8217\'20\'19s largest asset manager expects US borrowing costs to hover around 5.5% for the next five years as the central bank and investors grapple with inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 Ten-year Treasury yields are at 4.67%, but \uc2 Jean Boivin\uc2 , head of the BlackRock Investment Institute and a former deputy governor of the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 , said markets were heading for higher long-term borrowing costs. These will come from ageing populations, geopolitics and costs associated with the energy transition to net zero, he said.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/afbf891a-dea7-4536-b253-8e0fe0fbf82a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 He told the Financial Times}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 We think 5.5% long-term 10-year yields in the US is the level that seems consistent with the macro backdrop in the next five years.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s also consistent with the compensation for risks that bond investors should require to invest in long-term bonds.\par \par \uc2 His comments come after a rally in government bonds on both sides of the Atlantic in recent days as investors become more convinced that central banks have reached the end of their interest rate raising cycles.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 Stocks, oil rise; rouble firms despite fresh US sanctions\par \par \uc2 On the markets, stocks and oil prices are heading higher today, notching up modest gains.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 index rose 16 points, or 0.2%, to 7,463 while Germany\u8217\'20\'19s Dax added 0.3%, France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC edged up 0.1%, and Italy\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE MiB advanced nearly 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 Richard Hunter, head of markets at interactive investor, said:\par \par \uc2 After a dismal October for markets, November has opened with a different narrative and a very different performance.\par \par \uc2 That the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  held rates this week was no surprise, but the accompanying comments from Chair Powell lit the fire under stocks, with a noticeable fall in Treasury yields providing further fuel. While leaving the door slightly ajar to further rate rises should inflation unexpectedly tick higher once more, sentiment has switched to the belief that the hiking cycle is now over.\par \par \uc2 Today, yields (interest rates) on \uc2 US government\uc2  bonds, known as Treasuries, edged higher again, with the two-year yield climbing above 5%, after tumbling the day before, on relief that the \uc2 US government\uc2  announced smaller-than-expected increases in Treasury supply.\par \par \uc2 In the oil market, Brent crude, the global benchmark, rose 0.5% to $87.30 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 The Russian rouble edged up 0.4% to 92 versus the dollar, as the market digested the impact of the latest round of US sanctions against Moscow, following its invasion of Ukraine in February last year. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decree on mandatory foreign currency sales for some exporters is still lending some support to the currency.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the US imposed new measures against Russia over its war in Ukraine, targeting its future energy capabilities, sanctions evasion, seven Russia-based banks and dozens of industrial businesses.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 : 'There will come a point where no job is needed' due to AI\par \par \uc2 The US tech billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has warned that technology could eventually replace all human jobs, as \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 , the UK prime minister, announced that the most advanced technology companies will allow governments to vet their artificial intelligence tools for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Musk, the founder of the electric carmaker \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and owner of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , now known as X, said:\par \par \uc2 We are seeing the most disruptive force in history here. There will come a point where no job is needed. You can have a job if you want a job \u8230\'20\'26 but the AI will be able to do everything.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/nov/02/top-tech-firms-to-let-governments-vet-ai-tools-sunak-says-at-safety-summit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tech firms to allow vetting of AI tools, as Musk warns all human job threatened}}}\par \par \uc2 Companies including \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Google DeepMind\uc2  and \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  have agreed to allow regulators to test their latest AI products before releasing them to the public, in a move that officials say will slow the race to develop systems that can compete with humans.\par \par \uc2 Sunak made the announcement yesterday after a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/nov/02/five-takeaways-uk-ai-safety-summit-bletchley-park-rishi-sunak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two-day summit at Bletchley Park}}}\uc2  at which a diverse group including the world\u8217\'20\'19s richest man, the vice-president of the US and a senior Chinese government official agreed that AI poses a grave risk to humanity.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters at the end of the summit, Sunak said:\par \par \uc2 I believe the achievements of this summit will tip the balance in favour of humanity.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister also announced international support for an expert body inspired by the \uc2 Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\uc2 , to be chaired by one of the \u8220\'20\'1Cgodfathers\u8221\'20\'1D of modern AI.\par \par \uc2 The moves were welcomed afterwards by Musk in a conversation between the pair in central London, during which Musk described what he sees as a dramatically different future for humanity.\par \par \uc2 Britain's Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  attends an in-conversation event with \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 's CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  in London on 2 November. Photograph: Reuters\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  found guilty of defrauding \uc2 FTX\uc2  customers out of billions\par \par \uc2 Over in the United States, \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2 , the founder of now-bankrupt crypto exchange \uc2 FTX\uc2 , was found guilty on all counts of defrauding his customers in Manhattan federal court.\par \par \uc2 The one-time mogul stood with his hands clasped facing the jury as he was found guilty on seven counts of wire fraud and conspiracy to launder money. He faces decades in prison at a sentencing hearing that US district Judge Lewis Kaplan set for 28 March 2024. The verdict, reached after just four hours of jury deliberation, brought an end to nearly a month of court proceedings that featured stunning testimony from his closest allies and the disgraced entrepreneur himself. He maintained his innocence until the end.\par \par \uc2 Mark Cohen, Bankman-Fried\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer, said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 We respect the jury\u8217\'20\'19s decision. But we are very disappointed with the result. Mr Bankman-Fried maintains his innocence and will continue to vigorously fight the charges against him.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/02/sam-bankman-guilty-ftx-alameda"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried found guilty of defrauding FTX customers out of billions}}}\par \par \uc2 FTX\uc2  founder \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  stands with his lawyers after the verdict is read in his fraud trial over the collapse of the bankrupt cryptocurrency exchange at federal court in New York City on Thursday, in this courtroom sketch. Photograph: Jane Rosenberg/Reuters\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 Maersk\uc2  to cut 10,000 jobs\par \par \uc2 The Danish shipping giant \uc2 A.P. Moller-Maersk\uc2  is to cut 10,000 jobs, after posting a steep drop in profits and revenue in the third quarter, as it battles with lower freight rates and lower demand for container shipping.\par \par \uc2 The Danish company now expects to revenues and operating profits to come in at the lower end of its forecast range.\par \par \uc2 Chief executive \uc2 Vincent Clerc\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 Our industry is facing a new normal with subdued demand, prices back in line with historical levels and inflationary pressure on our cost base.\par \par \uc2 Since the summer, we have seen overcapacity across most regions triggering price drops and no noticeable uptick in ship recycling or idling.\par \par \uc2 In August, \uc2 Maersk\uc2  warned of a steeper decline in global demand for shipping containers by sea this year, due to slow economic growth and de-stocking in the wake of the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Maersk\uc2  said it intends to cut its workforce from 110,000 in January to below 100,000, which will result in saving $600m next year. It declined to comment on the impact on UK jobs.\par \par \uc2 The world\u8217\'20\'19s first methanol-enabled container vessel called \u8220\'20\'1CLaura Maersk\u8221\'20\'1D of \uc2 A.P. Moller-Maersk\uc2  after its namegiving ceremony in Copenhagen. Photograph: Sergei Gapon/AFP/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 Qantas\uc2  chairman heckled by shareholders as they reject executive pay plans\par \par \uc2 There was chaos at the Australian airline \uc2 Qantas\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s annual general meeting, where shareholders shouted \u8220\'20\'1Cshame on you\u8221\'20\'1D at the board\u8217\'20\'19s chairman, \uc2 Richard Goyder\uc2 , as investors overwhelmingly rejected the embattled company\u8217\'20\'19s executive pay deal.\par \par \uc2 That result, which marked one of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s largest ever protest votes against executive pay, came after Goyder and the airline\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, \uc2 Vanessa Hudson\uc2 , apologised to investors for a year of sagas that had seen the company\u8217\'20\'19s share price plummet.\par \par \uc2 Qantas\uc2  has been grappling with several potentially costly issues, including a looming compensation bill for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/sep/13/qantas-loses-high-court-outsourced-jobs-twu-transport-workers-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 illegally outsourcing ground handling jobs}}}\uc2  and regulatory action over allegations it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/oct/30/qantas-accc-case-response-fees-cancelled-flights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sold tickets to thousands of already-cancelled flights}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/03/qantas-chairman-heckled-by-shareholders-at-agm-as-investors-reject-executive-pay-plans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Qantas chairman heckled by shareholders at AGM as investors reject executive pay plans}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 German exports disappoint as trade remains a drag on the economy\par \par \uc2 In Germany, exports fell more than expected in September amid weaker global demand, according to figures from the federal statistics office.\par \par \uc2 Exports fell 2.4% from the previous month, worse than the 1.1% decline forecast by economists, while imports were down 1.7%. Most of Germany\u8217\'20\'19s exports went to the US despite a 4% drop, and most imports came from China, down 0.9%. Exports to the UK rose 2.3% to \u8364\'20\'AC6.3bn, while imports from the UK rose by 5.2% to \u8364\'20\'AC3.2bn.\par \par \uc2 Germany is normally an exports powerhouse but exports have been a drag on the economy in four out of six quarters since the start of last year.\par \par \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2 , global head of macro at ING, said:\par \par \uc2 Things are still looking pretty downbeat for Germany\u8217\'20\'19s economy.\par \par \uc2 The cooling of global demand is currently worsening the structural problems and the weakening of the euro since the summer is still too small to have any significant impact on exports. Export order books remain weak. Last but not least, recently German technology groups warned that they were being hit by customs delays for exports to China. As a result, trade is no longer the strong resilient growth driver of the German economy that it used to be, but rather a drag.\par \par \uc2 Maybe the only upside of today\u8217\'20\'19s disappointing data is that things can hardly get worse. However, as positive signals remain absent, the base case for the German economy over the next months remains stagnation at best.\par \par \uc2 enltrGood Morning from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Germany?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Germany}}}\uc2 , where the export model is under pressure. Sep exports fell 2.4% MoM but because imports also fell by 1.7%, the trade surplus fell only slightly to \u8364\'20\'AC16.5bn from a revised \u8364\'20\'AC17.7bn in Aug. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/5mOuoYTOus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/5mOuoYTOus}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Holger Zschaepitz (@Schuldensuehner) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Schuldensuehner/status/1720339098420846941?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 Global stocks headed for strongest week in a year as investors bet on end of rate hikes\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets, and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Global stocks are on course for their biggest weekly gain in a year as investors are betting that there won\u8217\'20\'19t be any more interest rate hike in major economies, while bonds rallied.\par \par \uc2 World stocks, as measured by the MSCI ACWI, which captures large and mid cap stocks across 23 developed markets and 24 emerging markets, are up 4.3% so far this week, the biggest rise since November 2022.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the Dow Jones industrial average jumped 564 points, or 1.7% yesterday, its best day since May. Investors are hoping that there won\u8217\'20\'19t be any more interest rate hikes, although central banks are signalling that rates will stay high for an extended period \u8211\'20\'13 painful for people with mortgages.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/01/fed-us-interest-rates-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/01/fed-us-interest-rates-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US Federal Reserve}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/01/fed-us-interest-rates-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  left interest rates unchanged}}}\uc2  at a 22-year high on Wednesday as inflation continues to fade from its highest level in a generation \u8211\'20\'13 but its chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  cautioned that the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s campaign to bring down price growth had \u8220\'20\'1Ca long way to go,\u8221\'20\'1D leaving the door open for further hikes.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/02/bank-of-england-leaves-interest-rates-unchanged-inflation-slowdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/02/bank-of-england-leaves-interest-rates-unchanged-inflation-slowdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bank of England}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/02/bank-of-england-leaves-interest-rates-unchanged-inflation-slowdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  kept its main interest rate at 5.25%}}}\uc2  yesterday for the second meeting in a row, the highest level since the 2008 financial crisis. It warned the economy will be on the brink of recession in an election year and signalled rates will stay high for a while to tackle stubborn inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs day! We will get the latest US non-farm payrolls figures at lunchtime, which are expected to show that companies are still hiring strongly.\par \par \uc2 Ipek Ozkardeskaya, senior analyst at \uc2 Swissquote Bank\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  hinted that the rate hikes could be coming to an end because the recent surge in US long term [bond] yields helped them tighten the financial conditions without the need for another rate hike.\par \par \uc2 US growth is strong, and the jobs market remains healthy. The Fed thinks that solid labour-force participation and immigration explain the resilience of the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 Any strength in job additions or wages growth data could bring bond trades back to earth and remind them that if the US jobs market - and the economy - remains this strong, the Fed could turn hawkish again. But strong jobs data in a context of higher supply is not necessarily inflationary.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up:\par \par \uc2 We have the \uc2 UN\uc2  global food index later this morning, and several \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  speeches at the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s Watchers\u8217\'20\'19 conference organised by Kings College.\par \par \uc2 The Agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am GMT: UN Food index for October\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am GMT: \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 Andrew Hauser\uc2  keynote speech at conference\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am GMT: UK S&P Global/CIPS Services PMI final for October\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am GMT: Eurozone unemployment for September\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 12.15pm GMT: BOE chief economist \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  speaks\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 12.30pm GMT: US Non-farm payrolls for October (forecast: 180,000)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 2pm GMT: US ISM Services PMI for October\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.58am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231103ejb3001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
