\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Is the pendulum of power swinging towards US unions?\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Reich \par \uc2 805 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2023\par 10:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  have scored a major victory. And it could be a sign of more to come for workers \u8211\'20\'13 unless the Fed undermines them again\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  has scored a major victory. It\u8217\'20\'19s still awaiting a vote by union members, but it\u8217\'20\'19s a big deal \u8211\'20\'13 a 25% wage increase over the four and a half years of the contract, cost-of-living increases that will further ratchet up hourly pay, the right to strike over plant closures and a shorter time period for workers to reach top pay.\par \par \uc2 If the victory ripples across the auto industry and encourages wage increases in other sectors, it will also be a victory for the American middle class.\par \par \uc2 For 30 years \u8211\'20\'13 from 1946 to the late 1970s \u8211\'20\'13 the American middle class expanded. That was largely because unions won increases in wages and benefits that roughly tracked gains in overall productivity.\par \par \uc2 Non-union companies gave their workers similar raises because they knew they\u8217\'20\'19d be targets of union organizing if they didn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 It was America\u8217\'20\'19s postwar social contract.\par \par \uc2 But since the late 1970s, the wages of production workers have been nearly stagnant, adjusted for inflation. Most gains have gone to the top.\par \par \uc2 What happened?\par \par \uc2 For one thing, activist investors (called \u8220\'20\'1Ccorporate raiders\u8221\'20\'1D in the 1970s and 80s, and \u8220\'20\'1Cprivate equity managers\u8221\'20\'1D today) got the right to mount hostile takeovers of companies, and then demand fatter profits.\par \par \uc2 Since payrolls comprise about two-thirds of corporate costs, the raiders forced corporations to keep a lid on wages and benefits.\par \par \uc2 To do this, corporations had to bust unions \u8211\'20\'13 outsourcing jobs abroad, moving to anti-union (AKA \u8220\'20\'1Cright-to-work\u8221\'20\'1D) states and firing workers who tried to organize.\par \par \uc2 Ronald Reagan legitimized all this when in 1981 he fired more than 11,000 striking air traffic controllers represented by the Professional Air Traffic Controllers Organization, or Patco.\par \par \uc2 The result was a dramatic decline in the bargaining power of ordinary workers. And with it, a shrinkage of the American middle class.\par \par \uc2 In the 1950s, over a third of all private-sector workers were unionized. Today, unionized workers comprise just 6% of private-sector workers (10% of all workers belong to a union but many work in the public sector).\par \par \uc2 From 1946 through the early 1970s, unions staged hundreds of major strikes each year. After 1981, the number of major strikes dropped to a few dozen per year.\par \par \uc2 Is the pendulum now swinging back?\par \par \uc2 Confidence in big business is at its lowest point in decades while approval of labor unions is near its highest\par \par \uc2 So far this year, there have been 22 major strikes, 17 of them at corporations.\par \par \uc2 Contracts negotiated by the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , Hollywood writers, UPS workers, \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  healthcare workers and even university employees, among others, provide significant pay increases and more job security (writers even got some protections against AI).\par \par \uc2 Most Americans are solidly behind the workers. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/510281/unions-strengthening.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Polls}}}\uc2  show that the public supported autoworkers over the companies by large margins.\par \par \uc2 Confidence in big business is at its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/5248/big-business.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lowest}}}\uc2  point in decades while approval of labor unions is near its highest.\par \par \uc2 What accounts for this burst of labor activism, remarkable run of labor victories and public support of unions?\par \par \uc2 Partly, I think, it\u8217\'20\'19s the harsh inequalities exposed by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic dramatically revealed how much easier it is for rich Americans to survive than everyone else, and how dependent all of us are on average workers simply doing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Couple this with the rise in populist politics \u8211\'20\'13 starting with Bernie Sanders\u8217\'20\'19 surprisingly strong showing in 2016 while Donald Trump posed as the \u8220\'20\'1Cvoice\u8221\'20\'1D of workers \u8211\'20\'13 in a system looking increasingly rigged against average people.\par \par \uc2 In addition, union victories have animated a virtuous cycle \u8211\'20\'13 encouraging more workers to join unions and more unions to flex their muscles and demand wage hikes.\par \par \uc2 And then there\u8217\'20\'19s the tight post-pandemic labor market, in which consumers are spending like gangbusters, the economy is surging, and employers worry about getting and keeping the workers they need.\par \par \uc2 So, will the pendulum continue to swing toward unions?\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19d love to think so. But I frankly worry about the Fed chair, Jerome Powell, and his colleagues.\par \par \uc2 They continue to believe \u8211\'20\'13 wrongly \u8211\'20\'13 that inflation is being pushed by wage increases rather than by corporate profits.\par \par \uc2 If they succeed in slowing the economy to the point where workers lose whatever bargaining leverage they now have, it\u8217\'20\'19s far from clear that populist politics or more vivid inequalities or a string of labor victories will be enough to put organized labor on the path to where it was four decades ago.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 , a former US secretary of labor, is professor of public policy at the \uc2 University of California at Berkeley\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231101ejb1001h2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
