\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US unions winning big gains amid \u8216\'20\'18Great Reset\u8217\'20\'19 in worker power\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,197 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2023\par 19:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Increased militancy after pandemic sacrifices and tighter labor market contributed to impressive contract deals in the last year\par \par \uc2 Call it the Great Reset. Across the US, labor unions are winning surprisingly large contract settlements as workers have reset their expectations to demand considerably more than they did just a few years ago, and that has in turn pressured many corporations to reset \u8211\'20\'13 and increase \u8211\'20\'13 the pay packages they are giving in union contracts.\par \par \uc2 The result has been a wave of impressive \u8211\'20\'13 sometimes eye-popping \u8211\'20\'13 union contracts over the past year, far more generous than in recent decades. In August, 15,000 \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  pilots won pay increases of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/08/21/business/american-airlines-pilots-union.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 46% over four years  }}}\uc2 . In a huge labor confrontation last summer, 340,000 Teamster members at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jul/25/ups-teamsters-strike-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UPS won raises  }}}\uc2  of $7.50 an hour over five years, with drivers\u8217\'20\'19 pay climbing to $49 an hour and part-time workers receiving a pay increase of 48% on average.\par \par \uc2 After a three-day strike earlier this month, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/13/kaiser-permanente-unions-deal-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 85,000 Kaiser Permanente workers  }}}\uc2  won raises of 21%, as well as a $25 minimum wage for Kaiser\u8217\'20\'19s workers in California. In March, 30,000 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/education/los-angeles-schools-union-leaders-reach-deal-on-worker-pay-and-benefits-after-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Los Angeles school district workers  }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 bus drivers, cafeteria workers and teachers\u8217\'20\'19 aides \u8211\'20\'13 won a 30% wage hike over four years. In Oregon, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nwlaborpress.org/2023/08/providence-settles-with-nurses/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1,400 nurses at Providence Portland  }}}\uc2  hospital secured raises between 17% and 27% over two years.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders and rank-and-file workers hailed these contracts as great and historic, but Thomas Kochan, a longtime professor of industrial relations at MIT, put it another way: \u8220\'20\'1CAll this reflects a a reset in expectations and wage norms for workers and for employers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese successes,\u8221\'20\'1D Kochan continued, \u8220\'20\'1Care a reflection of the workforce\u8217\'20\'19s strong expectations and the workforce\u8217\'20\'19s demands to make up for lost ground due to inflation \u8211\'20\'13 and to signal that times have changed. The modest wage increases of the past will no longer be adequate to deal with our situation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They haven\u8217\'20\'19t experienced this level of pressure from unions or this amount of bargaining power on the side of workers\par \par \uc2 Thomas Kochan\par \par \uc2 Workers are feeling not only rising expectations, but also rising frustration and anger, seeing corporate profits and CEO pay soar, while their pay has often failed to keep up with inflation. The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  , before going on strike a month ago against GM, Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  , kept hammering the point that auto workers\u8217\'20\'19 hourly pay has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/blog/uaw-automakers-negotiations/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trailed inflation by 19%  }}}\uc2  since 2008, while CEO pay has jumped by 40%. The union\u8217\'20\'19s initial bargaining proposal was for a 46% raise, far more than the union won from Detroit\u8217\'20\'19s automakers in 2019 \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/25/business/gm-contract.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a four-year contract  }}}\uc2  with raises of 3% in the second and fourth years and a 4% lump sum (but no raise) in years one and three.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve talked with labor relations people at several companies,\u8221\'20\'1D Kochan said, \u8220\'20\'1Cand they have had to get their CEOs and CFOs [chief financial officers] to reset their expectations. They were used to getting modest wage settlements, but they haven\u8217\'20\'19t experienced this level of pressure from unions and they haven\u8217\'20\'19t experienced this amount of bargaining power on the side of workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Feeling angry and emboldened, workers have been flexing their muscles. There were 301 strikes in the first nine months of his year, up from 172 over the same period in 2021, according t0 ILR {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://striketracker.ilr.cornell.edu/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Labor Action Tracker  }}}\uc2 . This has ratcheted up pressure on employers, and this increased militancy has often translated into better contracts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve never seen a moment quite like this. I\u8217\'20\'19ve never seen this level of action and enthusiasm among workers, especially among young workers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lane Windham, associate director of the Kalmanovitz Initiative for Labor and the Working Poor at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2  . \u8220\'20\'1CPeople drew a line in terms of their expectations. For 40 years, workers have been putting up with low wages and bad jobs. But after coming through the pandemic, many people said: \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to do this any more. I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to put up with this any more.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Windham said many employers had been surprised by the level of labor militancy they are encountering. \u8220\'20\'1CEmployers are entering negotiations and beginning with their old calculations, but they\u8217\'20\'19re seeing this is not business as usual. They\u8217\'20\'19ve had to recalibrate what they\u8217\'20\'19re willing to give.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That has been the case with Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s studios, which didn\u8217\'20\'19t expect 165,000 television and movie actors and 15,000 Hollywood writers to go on strike at the same time. Before the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/sep/26/hollywood-writers-strike-ends-studio-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Writers Guild\u8217\'20\'19s members walked out  }}}\uc2 , Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s studios dug in against that union\u8217\'20\'19s demands, but after the writers were out nearly five months, the studios largely acceded to the union\u8217\'20\'19s wishes on pay, minimum staffing levels for writers and limiting the use of artificial intelligence in scripts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are three main factors driving the big contract wins,\u8221\'20\'1D said Eric Blanc, a labor studies professor at \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2  . \u8220\'20\'1COne is the tight labor market. Second is increased union momentum generally. Third is more effective and militant bargaining methods.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Blanc added: \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t underestimate how crucial the tight labor market has been and continues to be for giving workers leverage. In the past if you were on strike for an extended period and the labor market wasn\u8217\'20\'19t tight, employers could replace you easily. For years, unions were reluctant to go on strike because of that. But today\u8217\'20\'19s tight labor market is important for giving workers and unions confidence.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 People see that they can fight back and really get corporate America to cough up concessions to workers\par \par \uc2 Eric Blanc\par \par \uc2 The tight labor market has encouraged unions to think and act bigger and bolder. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re not thinking big gains, there\u8217\'20\'19s probably something wrong with what you\u8217\'20\'19re doing as a union,\u8221\'20\'1D Blanc said. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople see that they can fight back and really get corporate America to cough up concessions to workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even as workers at companies with long-established unions rack up big gains, workers at some prominent, newly unionized companies, most notably \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  , \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s, are struggling to reach their first contract to win improved pay and benefits. But some newly unionized workers have won excellent contracts: this month {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ueunion.org/ue-news/2023/mit-graduate-workers-first-ue-contract-sets-new-standards"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 3,800 graduate student workers at MIT  }}}\uc2  won a 12.6% increase in their stipend, a $10,000 needs-based childcare subsidy and an 84% dental subsidy. At the Whitney Museum in New York, the 180-member union won {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/03/06/arts/design/whitney-museum-union-contract.html#:~:text=She%20and%20three%20other%20employees,raise%2C%20to%20%2454%2C101%20from%20%2440%2C500."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 30% raise on average  }}}\uc2  in its first contract, with entry-level pay jumping to $54,100 from $40,500.\par \par \uc2 Professor Kent Wong, director of the UCLA Labor Center, said the fact that unions have their strongest public approval in more than 50 years \u8211\'20\'13 not to mention a US president who bills himself as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/02/joe-biden-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most pro-union president  }}}\uc2  ever \u8211\'20\'13 has created an atmosphere that helps unions do well in their contract fights. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re winning the public opinion war and they\u8217\'20\'19re building on other union victories,\u8221\'20\'1D Wong said. \u8220\'20\'1CVictories inspire other victories.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231024ejao001bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
