\par\par \uc2 Media\par \b \uc2 Netflix\uc2  says password crackdown working as it adds 8.8 million new users\b0\par\par\uc2 Dominic Rushe in New York \par \uc2 435 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2023\par 01:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Streaming company announces plans to increase prices as profits rise, even as Hollywood strikes threaten to affect programming\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  announced its global crackdown on password sharing was working and unveiled plans to increase prices as it announced its latest quarterly results on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The streaming media company added 8.8 million new subscribers over the last three months, far better than expected and up from 2.4 million in the same quarter last year. The increase came even as a strike by Hollywood actors and writers threatened to affect the rollout of new shows.\par \par \uc2 The price of a basic \uc2 Netflix\uc2  subscription in the US will rise by $2 to $11.99, while premium subscriptions will rise by $3 to $22.99. In the UK, a basic subscription will rise by \u163\'00\'A31 to \u163\'00\'A37.99, with premium memberships increasing by \u163\'00\'A32 to \u163\'00\'A317.99.\par \par \uc2 In Australia, prices will remain the same but the basic tier, currently priced at $10.99, will no longer be offered to new and rejoining subscribers from next week, in an effort to push them towards the more expensive \u8220\'20\'1Cstandard\u8221\'20\'1D tier, or the cheaper, ad-supported tier.\par \par \uc2 The basic tier will also be phased out for new subscribers in Germany, Spain, Japan, Mexico and Brazil next week. It has already been phased out in the US, UK, Italy and Canada.\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  began {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2023/jun/24/netflix-cracks-down-as-it-begins-password-sharing-purge-in-uk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cracking down}}}\uc2  on people using shared passwords to access its service this summer. The company said the \u8220\'20\'1Ccancel reaction\u8221\'20\'1D has been lower than expected.\par \par \uc2 The company also launched the ad-supported tier a year ago, which they said now accounts for 30% of new subscribers but added that it would take time to build the service \u8220\'20\'1Cfrom scratch\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe remain very optimistic about our long-run opportunity in this very big market,\u8221\'20\'1D the company said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood writers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/sep/26/hollywood-writers-strike-ends-studio-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed to end their strike}}}\uc2  after a five-month walk out triggered in large part by grievances over the lack of revenue sharing from \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and other streaming services. Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s actors are still negotiating and remain on strike.\par \par \uc2 The dispute led to delays and cancellations that have cut \uc2 Netflix\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s expenses for the year. The company expects to invest about $13bn in content this year, down from the $17bn it expected to spend earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  reported revenues of $8.54bn, in line with projections. Net profit rose 20% to $1.68bn in the third quarter, topping its forecast. \uc2 Netflix\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s share price rose over 11% in after-hours trading.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231018ejai002h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
