\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 UAW\uc2  threatens to escalate strike as negotiations with automakers stall\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 554 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2023\par 16:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The autoworker union president, Shawn Fain, says the \u8216\'20\'18stand-up\u8217\'20\'19 strike will intensify unless the big three car companies \u8216\'20\'18pony up\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  president Shawn Fain threatened to escalate the union\u8217\'20\'19s strike actions on Friday as talks with the US\u8217\'20\'19s biggest auto manufacturers appeared to have stalled.\par \par \uc2 The strike is in its fourth week and Fain has been updating members on progress and new strike actions each Friday. Fain said Ford, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2  have used that timetable to hold back negotiations until late in the week. From now on, strikes can be called at any time, he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey thought they figured out the so-called rules of the game, so we changed the rules, and now there\u8217\'20\'19s only one rule: pony up,\u8221\'20\'1D said Fain.\par \par \uc2 About 34,000 auto workers at Ford, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2  are now on strike as part of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cstand-up\u8221\'20\'1D strike strategy \u8211\'20\'13 which involves hitting individual auto plants rather than a full walkout. The strike began on 15 September after contracts expired for 150,000 autoworkers represented by the \uc2 UAW\uc2  at the big three US automakers.\par \par \uc2 About 8,700 autoworkers at Ford\u8217\'20\'19s truck plant in Louisville, Kentucky, its largest plant, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.autonews.com/manufacturing/uaw-strike-8700-ford-workers-kentucky-truck-plant-join-walkout"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 walked off}}}\uc2  the job on 11 October in a surprise move after Ford told the \uc2 UAW\uc2  it currently had no counteroffer on negotiations. The vehicles produced at the plant generate $25bn a year in revenue for Ford.\par \par \uc2 Ford {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mtruby/status/1712250333177467280"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criticized}}}\uc2  the strike move as \u8220\'20\'1Cgrossly irresponsible\u8221\'20\'1D and executives there have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.detroitnews.com/restricted/?return=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.detroitnews.com%2Fstory%2Fbusiness%2Fautos%2Fford%2F2023%2F10%2F12%2Fford-says-its-reached-limit-on-economic-offer-to-uaw-as-ktp-strike-fallout-begins%2F71156549007%2F#:~:text=Ford%20executives%20said%20Thursday%20they,plant%20came%20into%20clearer%20focus."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed }}}\uc2  the company has reached its limit on what it is willing to offer the union on economics. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve actually stretched ourselves to get to this point,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Kumar Galhotra\uc2 , president of the Ford Blue division, said on a conference call.\par \par \uc2 Fain dismissed the comments on Friday. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Kentucky truck plant we just shut down in Louisville generates $48,000 in revenue per minute. That\u8217\'20\'19s vastly more than the lowest-paid Ford worker makes in a year,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHow much further do they want US autoworkers to stretch? We are not going to keep begging for scraps. Meanwhile, [Ford CEO] \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  took in $21m last year. We need him to do two things right now: look in the mirror and look in Ford\u8217\'20\'19s bank account. Go get the big checkbook. The one Ford uses when it wants to spend millions on company executives or Wall Street giveaways.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has contended throughout negotiations that it is asking for a contract that reinstates the protections workers gave up to save the auto industry when the Great Recession threatened to destroy it and to make up for the lagging wages workers have received since then.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are some that are trying to say that I\u8217\'20\'19m raising members\u8217\'20\'19 expectations too high. They think it\u8217\'20\'19s dangerous to tell the working class they deserve more,\u8221\'20\'1D added Fain. \u8220\'20\'1CI want to be clear on this point. I didn\u8217\'20\'19t raise members\u8217\'20\'19 expectations. Our broken economy is what\u8217\'20\'19s raising our member\u8217\'20\'19s expectations and our members are right to be angry. Corporate America rebounded after the Great Recession and profits are soaring amid a seven-year high in 2022. Meanwhile, the working class has kept going backwards.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231013ejad002h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
