\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Burnt out and fed up\u8217\'20\'19: 75,000 workers begin largest healthcare strike in US\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 390 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2023\par 22:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  workers start three-day strike, demanding wage increases and better staffing, after union contracts expire\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seiu-uhw.org/press/more-than-75000-kaiser-permanente-healthcare-workers-to-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 75,000  }}}\uc2  healthcare workers at \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  have started a three-day strike on Wednesday in the largest demonstration of its kind by healthcare workers in US history.\par \par \uc2 The workers, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/03/kaiser-permanente-healthcare-strike-hospital-union-california-washington-dc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 represented  }}}\uc2  by the Coalition of Kaiser Permanente Unions, are currently bargaining for new union contracts after their current contracts expired on 1 October. Workers are demanding significant wage increases and substantive improvements to what they say has been severe understaffing in healthcare facilities that worsened throughout the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The strike has hit the non-profit private healthcare provider\u8217\'20\'19s operations in California, Oregon, Washington, Colorado, Virginia and Washington DC.\par \par \uc2 Unions pointed to \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s recent profits of $3bn in the first half of 2023 and decried exorbitant salaries of executives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRecord profits should mean record contracts that invest in workers and the patients we serve,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dawn Martin, a medical assistant in Battle Ground, Washington. \u8220\'20\'1CKaiser executives are refusing to listen to frontline healthcare workers like me and are bargaining in bad faith over the solutions we need to end the Kaiser short-staffing crisis.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The coalition said that in a bargaining session the summer, \uc2 Kaiser Permanente{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.unioncoalition.org/2023-session4/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed  }}}\uc2  workers at the non-profit \u8220\'20\'1Cmake too much money\u8221\'20\'1D while the CEO is paid a salary of $16m a year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers across race and background are taking on Kaiser, saying it\u8217\'20\'19s time to bargain in good faith and respect us, protect us and pay us the living wages we need to thrive. They\u8217\'20\'19re demanding what\u8217\'20\'19s necessary for patients to get the quality care they need and deserve,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Mary Kay Henry\uc2  , international president of the 2 million-member \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  (\uc2 SEIU\uc2  ) which represents many of the workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are burnt out, scared for their patients and fed up. It\u8217\'20\'19s time for Kaiser to act with the urgency this staffing crisis demands and to settle a fair contract that includes longterm solutions to the crisis like livable wages and benefits.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  claimed they have been addressing staffing issues that have hit the healthcare industry as a whole and said they pay above market rates in wages.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231004eja40020a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
