\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 What have we learned from the Hollywood writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike?\b0\par\par\uc2 Kathleen Sharp \par \uc2 969 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2023\par 20:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Top executives are terrible at reading the room and unions are stronger when workers stick together. Now let\u8217\'20\'19s sign that contract\par \par \uc2 This year was supposed to have been a showbiz love fest. \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Warner Brothers\uc2  and the boosters of the world-famous Hollywood sign had planned to celebrate their centennials, with a special thanks to the \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/disney-debuts-super-bowl-lvii-commercial-celebrating-100-years-of-storytelling-and-shared-memories-301744717.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 storytellers who have sparked the joy}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D of movies.\par \par \uc2 There were so many actors, writers and other workers buzzing around the soundstages in Burbank, Studio City and Culver City that you could practically hear the hum of a $134bn TV-and-film hive.\par \par \uc2 By spring, however, the buzz had died. After negotiating for two months with the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  for better pay, job security, and more staffing, the Writers Guild of America went on strike in May.\par \par \uc2 Now the writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike is over and the 11,500 members of the WGA are voting on the contract. Since the union got everything it asked for back in May, ratifying the three-year-contract is a foregone conclusion.\par \par \uc2 So, let\u8217\'20\'19s review the lessons that were learned by both sides in this historic event.\par \par \uc2 Moguls need to read the room\par \par \uc2 CEOs in Hollywood like to think that they are skilled at reading the room. But if they sit too long in the captain\u8217\'20\'19s seat, they can turn myopic and see only what\u8217\'20\'19s in front of them and not 10ft (or 10 years) ahead.\par \par \uc2 Had they been paying attention, they would have known that, nationwide, there have been 275 strikes so far this year. Teachers, graduate students, baristas, farm workers, nurses, bell boys, hotel maids, longshoremen \u8211\'20\'13 the list goes on. Even strippers at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abc7.com/strippers-dancers-union-north-hollywood/13270084/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 North Hollywood topless club}}}\uc2  joined a union in May after a 15-month battle.\par \par \uc2 The union surge is due partly to the pandemic. It showed workers how indispensable they are to the economy, how underpaid they are for their work and how cavalierly most employers treat them. It hasn\u8217\'20\'19t helped that some firms have price-gouged their customers, blaming it on inflation. In fact, the cable unit owned by NBC Universal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thestreet.com/technology/comcast-has-a-nasty-surprise-for-cable-customers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Comcast has tripled its monthly}}}\uc2  fees in the past few years, prompting consumers to cut the cord. These multinationals seem out of touch with most Americans.\par \par \uc2 Writers should stick together\par \par \uc2 In the last strike of 2007, better-paid showrunners pushed lesser-paid screenwriters to settle for an inferior deal. They didn\u8217\'20\'19t, but the division did not bode well for solidarity.\par \par \uc2 This year, the WGA agreed to stick together. This summer, when temperatures in Los Angeles reached 104F (40C), WGA members picketed nearly every day. They also joined the picket lines of service workers, hotel strikers, actors, teachers and anyone else who was fighting for a fair shake.\par \par \uc2 Outsiders call Los Angeles a fractured city. But it\u8217\'20\'19s a union town with a remarkable level of cross-union support that cuts across radically different workplaces. Watching the camaraderie among workers made it easy for bystanders to cheer them all on.\par \par \uc2 Disdain and disrespect aren\u8217\'20\'19t labor strategies\par \par \uc2 For months, the tycoons talked to content creators as though they were troubled teens. \uc2 Disney\uc2  chief \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2  chastised writers for not being \u8220\'20\'1Crealistic\u8221\'20\'1D while he was at the grossly impractical Sun Valley retreat for the elite. \uc2 Warner Bros Discovery\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2  bragged that he was saving $300m by not paying writers on strike. \uc2 Netflix\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2  unctuously claimed that he was \u8220\'20\'1Ca union man through and through, and feels the (strikers\u8217\'20\'19) pain\u8221\'20\'1D although no one believed him.\par \par \uc2 Anonymously, the chiefs had used the trades to plant threats about dragging things out until writers lost their homes. They complained about the writers\u8217\'20\'19 lack of civility and tried to bully them into accepting a bad deal. In the end, it made the leaders look bad.\par \par \uc2 This is not your daddy\u8217\'20\'19s union\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  is led by Carol Lombardini, who comes from the old school of labor negotiations. The 68-year-old can be heavy-handed and lacks the speed, continuity and nimbleness to match the skillful scribes. She\u8217\'20\'19s married to a corporate attorney who\u8217\'20\'19s spent his career defending companies against employee labor disputes and who has also worked for the studios.\par \par \uc2 Early in negotiations, Lombardini would throw out a crumb of what the WGA was seeking. When the union tried to negotiate for a whole slice, negotiations would stop for several weeks \u8211\'20\'13 until the next crumb-tossing event. Granted, the woman is responsible for 58 other Hollywood union deals. But for her annual pay of $3m, she could have done better with WGA.\par \par \uc2 When things turn toxic, hire a snake-charmer\par \par \uc2 By late August, relations between employers and unions had soured so much, a Gallup poll showed that Americans favored the WGA over \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  by 72% to 19%.\par \par \uc2 Enter Molly Levinson, an expensive crisis manager. Her job was to \u8220\'20\'1Creframe the big picture\u8221\'20\'1D about the studio chiefs who have been called greedy, imperious and out of touch. In the past, Levinson has \u8220\'20\'1Creset\u8221\'20\'1D the images of predatory student lender Better Future Forward and of \uc2 Theranos\uc2  fraudster \uc2 Elizabeth Holmes\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Levinson told the moguls to settle. And they did. In September, the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  gave the WGA pretty much what it had given the \uc2 Directors Guild of America\uc2  in June. After a five-month-long industry shutdown, the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s brash gamble cost California\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marketwatch.com/story/striking-actors-and-writers-have-cost-california-5-billion-says-analyst-b97f69"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 economy about $5bn,}}}\uc2  New York another $1.6bn, and who knows how much more in other states.\par \par \uc2 But at least we can now celebrate the centennial of the movies\u8217\'20\'19 magic.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Sharp is the author of Mr & Mrs Hollywood: Edie and Lew Wasserman and Their Entertainment Empire which has been optioned\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231004eja4002s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
