\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Anti-Reagan cartoons and a jacket from Cesar Chavez: inside the \uc2 UAW\uc2  archive\b0\par\par\uc2 Alaina Demopoulos \par \uc2 1,037 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2023\par 17:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the United Autoworkers strike continues, a collection ranging from board games to defensive weapons tells the story of organizing \u8211\'20\'13 and how little has changed\par \par \uc2 A Hawaiian shirt branded with union logos that wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t look out of place on a hypebeast runway. Political cartoons roasting Ronald Reagan\u8217\'20\'19s anti-labor policies. Rusty weapons recovered from violent strikes. All of that and more can be found in the Reuther archives, an exhaustive repository of the \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW) union\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s artifacts from a century of strikes.\par \par \uc2 The US is in the midst of the first strike by workers at all the big three car companies of Detroit in 80 years. It\u8217\'20\'19s been a galvanizing moment for autoworkers, with Joe Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/26/biden-joins-uaw-strike-update-detroit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 making a historic visit}}}\uc2  to the picket line.\par \par \uc2 But Gavin Strassel, an archivist at \uc2 Wayne State University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Walter P Reuther Library who catalogues \uc2 UAW\uc2  history, says he\u8217\'20\'19s seen it all before. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are certain things that unions bargain for, and maybe the details change, but overall it stays the same.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One might expect to find plenty of leaflets and union publications in the archives. But there are hidden gems, too: one of Strassel\u8217\'20\'19s favorite artifacts is a \uc2 UAW\uc2 -produced board game called Union Power. It\u8217\'20\'19s a take on Monopoly, featuring dark humor about bad bosses and structural racism at work. From the instructions: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you roll a six, that means you\u8217\'20\'19re a non-white teenager with a 25% unemployment rate, start two turns after everyone else.\u8221\'20\'1D Roll a five, and you\u8217\'20\'19re a woman \u8211\'20\'13 you have to wait one turn after everyone else.\par \par \uc2 The game comes from an unprocessed collection of items, but Strassel guesses it was made by the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s education department. \u8220\'20\'1CSocial gatherings are such a big part of union locals, and I can imagine workers playing this together,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The pieces in the archive do more than simply show what it was like to be a \uc2 UAW\uc2  member. Some of the items reflect the history of other 20th-century progressive movements. For many members, joining the labor movement was the first step to becoming activists for causes such as civil rights and women\u8217\'20\'19s liberation.\par \par \uc2 The library is named after the 20th-century labor leader and civil rights activist who ran the union from 1946 until his death in 1970. During his tenure, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  became one of the most powerful unions in the country. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of people say that the American middle class as we know it was created because of the \uc2 UAW\uc2  during that time,\u8221\'20\'1D Strassel said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBeyond those bread-and-butter issues for workers, Reuther was also the biggest proponent of social unionism during his time, which is the belief that unions need to do more than work for higher wages \u8211\'20\'13 they need to improve things for greater society, too,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 A photo book in the archive shows the clash between Henry Ford\u8217\'20\'19s strikebreakers, known for roughing up workers, and organizers. The guards surround \uc2 UAW\uc2  members from all sides, kicking and punching them, and also attacking a women\u8217\'20\'19s auxiliary group. \u8220\'20\'1CThe incident really shattered some myths about the public\u8217\'20\'19s perception of Ford,\u8221\'20\'1D Strassel said. Historians credit a surge in support for unions to the publication of these photos.\par \par \uc2 To underscore the dangerous nature of early \uc2 UAW\uc2  organizing, the archive also contains a small arsenal of weapons used in clashes between strikebreakers and workers defending themselves. One wooden blackjack taken from a strikebreaker in 1940 appears to be crafted from a piece of furniture, perhaps a chair leg. A large club was made with thick leather strips, probably taken from assembly line scraps used to make car seats.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese weapons were made with spare parts from around the factory,\u8221\'20\'1D Strassel said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a good weight to the blackjack, so it would have hurt a little bit more than you might expect.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Reuther campaigned for civil rights at the 1963 March on Washington, counted \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2  as a friend, and campaigned for passing of the Civil Rights Act. A bright yellow jacket stamped with the \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 Aztec eagle insignia, given to Reuther by Cesar Chavez, reveals the allegiance between the two leaders.\par \par \uc2 Though not all rank-and-file \uc2 UAW\uc2  members may have been as accepting, union publications call for solidarity among different races and sexes. One cartoon from a 1960 political ad in Solidarity magazine encouraged members to vote Democratic to advance civil rights legislation. In the image, the Statue of \uc2 Liberty\uc2  stands next to a hooded Klansman, asking: \u8220\'20\'1CWhich do you choose, liberty or bigotry?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A woman\u8217\'20\'19s role in the \uc2 UAW\uc2  began with auxiliary groups, where female family members showed their support for male relatives working in plants. One massive recruitment poster from 1935 reads: \u8220\'20\'1CA union man has a happy life when he\u8217\'20\'19s got a union wife.\u8221\'20\'1D Woody Guthrie later added that slogan to Union Maid, which he wrote in 1940 to include the female perspective in his labor songbook.\par \par \uc2 But ladies\u8217\'20\'19 groups weren\u8217\'20\'19t sewing circles. During the 1936 sit-down strike in Flint, Michigan, where \uc2 General Motors\uc2  workers took over the plant during the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s first successful strike, women guarded the doors. They used pots and pans to deter strikebreakers, Strassel said, \u8220\'20\'1Cputting their lives on the line, even though they weren\u8217\'20\'19t officially in the union\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The current \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, Shawn Fain, keeps re-creations of vintage labor posters in his office. The originals are housed in the Reuther archive. One of the banners quotes a Reuther saying that calls for pensions: \u8220\'20\'1CToo old to work, too young to die.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The line remains relevant, as one of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s current demands is for auto companies to bring back pensions for new employees. (Workers hired after 2008 have received 401(k) retirement plans instead of pensions.) For Strassel, the messaging is a connection between past and present.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s interesting to see how relevant the content in our records is, even though some of it happened almost 100 years ago,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt still speaks to what we see today.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020231003eja30018q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
