\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Biden is right to praise the auto strike. His climate agenda depends on it\b0\par\par\uc2 Kate Aronoff \par \uc2 962 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2023\par 13:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The president has a golden opportunity to prove that green jobs will bear dividends for the working class\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had to choose a side in the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 contract fight with the \u8220\'20\'1Cbig three\u8221\'20\'1D American automakers, and he did. This week, he became the first US president to walk a picket line while in office when he joined strikers in Belleville, Michigan, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/speeches-remarks/2023/09/26/remarks-by-president-biden-at-united-auto-workers-picket-line/#:~:text=Folks%2C%20look%2C%20one%20thing%20is,the%20companies%20were%20in%20trouble."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 offering}}}\uc2  enthusiastic support for their demands. Biden should be thanking the \uc2 UAW\uc2  for handing him a golden opportunity: to prove that the green jobs his administration is creating will be good, union jobs, too, and that climate policy will bear dividends for the working class.\par \par \uc2 Republicans cosplaying solidarity have tried to exploit the strike to score cheap political points. As Republican presidential hopefuls debated this week, \uc2 Donald Trump{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/trump-uaw-detroit-biden-strike-autoworkers-debate-165c2d45cb43992814b23a1f6c7572f1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  a rally {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jacobin.com/2023/09/trump-michigan-visit-drake-enterprises-uaw-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at a non-union plant}}}\uc2  in Michigan that the strike wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8220\'20\'1Cmake a damn bit of difference\u8221\'20\'1D because the car industry was \u8220\'20\'1Cbeing assassinated\u8221\'20\'1D by \u8220\'20\'1CEV mandates\u8221\'20\'1D. (Whether there were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/28/trump-michigan-fake-signs-auto-workers-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 any union members or even autoworkers}}}\uc2  in the room isn\u8217\'20\'19t clear.) Ohio senator JD Vance has similarly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vance.senate.gov/press-releases/senator-vance-bidens-forced-transition-to-electric-vehicles-is-crushing-uaw-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blamed}}}\uc2  autoworkers\u8217\'20\'19 plight on \u8220\'20\'1Cthe premature transition to electric vehicles\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CBiden\u8217\'20\'19s war on American cars\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 These are cynical, false talking points from politicians who couldn\u8217\'20\'19t care less about autoworkers \u8211\'20\'13 but they aren\u8217\'20\'19t going away. (Although similar lines are old hat in the US, they\u8217\'20\'19re finding new purchase in places like 10 Downing Street: \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 , the British prime minister, has recently taken a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2023/sep/21/rishi-sunak-defends-climate-policy-u-turn-despite-criticism"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 U-turn}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D on climate goals, citing \u8220\'20\'1Cunacceptable costs\u8221\'20\'1D for \u8220\'20\'1Chard-working British people\u8221\'20\'1D.) Optimistically, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike could be a chance to dismantle the rightwing myth that reducing emissions hurts working people \u8211\'20\'13 not by pointing to the jobs that will trickle down from the bosses of the energy transition, but by standing with the unions fighting to make those jobs better.\par \par \uc2 Being willing to go on offense against automakers\u8217\'20\'19 bad behavior is a great start and a big shift. The Biden administration has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/09/14/fact-sheet-president-bidens-economic-plan-drives-americas-electric-vehicle-manufacturing-boom/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 routinely}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/speeches-remarks/2022/09/14/remarks-by-president-biden-on-the-electric-vehicle-manufacturing-boom-in-america/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 praised}}}\uc2  car manufacturers as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/business/autos-transportation/biden-promote-shift-evs-visit-detroit-auto-show-2022-09-14/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 climate heroes}}}\uc2  poised to decarbonize the country and create millions of middle-class jobs along the way, turning the industry into a sort of mascot for its climate agenda. \u8220\'20\'1CYou changed the whole story, Mary,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2023/09/19/biden-gm-uaw-strike-00116652"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2 General Motors\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 chief executive, \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 , a frequent White House guest, in 2021. \u8220\'20\'1CYou electrified the entire automobile industry. I\u8217\'20\'19m serious.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 White House climate policy will be good for Barra and her colleagues at the top. The business-side tax credits and government-backed loans furnished by the Inflation Reduction Act (IRA) are already helping the big three retool factories to produce EVs and their component parts. The IRA\u8217\'20\'19s consumer-side subsidies for American-made electric cars \u8211\'20\'13 worth up to $7,500 \u8211\'20\'13 will boost demand.\par \par \uc2 GM and Ford knew for decades that their products fueled climate change, and proceeded to double down\par \par \uc2 Yet no one should confuse companies taking advantage of tax breaks with a commitment to the climate fight. The big three {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/05/23/electric-vehicles-us-china-europe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lag}}}\uc2  well behind their competition in the US and abroad; federal incentives are helping them play catch-up. They\u8217\'20\'19ve {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2019/oct/10/exclusive-carmakers-opponents-climate-action-us-europe-emissions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lobbied}}}\uc2  to undermine fuel efficiency and clean car standards. Like oil and gas companies, GM and Ford knew for decades that their products fueled climate change, and proceeded to double down on gas-guzzling models and political attacks on laws and regulations that might hem in their emissions. They still {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.opensecrets.org/industries/indus.php?ind=t2100"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bankroll}}}\uc2  the campaigns of Republicans dead-set on stopping climate policy.\par \par \uc2 Neither is it a given that EV subsidies benefiting companies will benefit workers there, too. Automakers are already using electrification as an excuse to supercharge attempts to ship jobs to less union-friendly states, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jacobin.com/2023/08/uaw-general-motors-work-tiers-electric-vehicles-green-transition"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 split workers}}}\uc2  off from their master agreement with the big three.\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s decision to join the strike would be remarkable on its own. Beyond the obvious symbolism, his presence there lends tangible material support to workers\u8217\'20\'19 demands, handing the union leverage over companies that might otherwise reasonably assume he\u8217\'20\'19d have their backs.\par \par \uc2 It could also usher in a broader shift in the way he and other Democrats talk about climate policy. Impressive as the IRA is, its most direct benefits accrue largely to companies and consumers with enough cash on hand to afford up-front payments for big-ticket items like solar panels and heat pumps. Like Bidenomics more generally, its goal isn\u8217\'20\'19t to reduce emissions so much as to build out domestic supply chains for clean energy goods, making US companies less reliant on and more competitive against Chinese firms in sectors that will be increasingly important over the coming decades.\par \par \uc2 Targeting climate policy at corporations and affluent consumers doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make a great counterargument to Republicans eager to frame it all as elitist virtue signaling, and win elections accordingly. What the Republican party can be reliably expected to do, though, is side with the bosses. That\u8217\'20\'19s where even self-professed \u8220\'20\'1Ccar guy\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  might be able to set himself apart \u8211\'20\'13 by being willing to offend the automakers so that the rewards of America\u8217\'20\'19s green industrial policy aren\u8217\'20\'19t hoarded at the top.\par \par \uc2 Standing alongside Biden in Belleville this week, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, Shawn Fain, offered as good a framing for that approach as any. \u8220\'20\'1CThis industry is of our making,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we withhold our labor, we can unmake it. And as we\u8217\'20\'19re going to continue to show: when we win this fight with the big three, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to remake it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Kate Aronoff is a staff writer at the New Republic and the author of Overheated: How Capitalism Broke the Planet \u8211\'20\'13 And How We Fight Back\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230929ej9t001mp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
