\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump to woo blue-collar Michigan workers after Biden\u8217\'20\'19s stop at \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Tait in Washington \par \uc2 607 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2023\par 15:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Former president will address 500 workers in key election state in attempt to upstage Biden\u8217\'20\'19s appeal to autoworkers on Tuesday\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will attempt to woo blue-collar workers in Michigan in an intensifying political tug-of-war with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a day after the sitting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/26/biden-joins-uaw-strike-update-detroit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 president visited a picket line}}}\uc2  to declare support for a trade union strike against the US\u8217\'20\'19s three flagship carmakers.\par \par \uc2 Targeting working-class voters in the key battleground, the former president will attempt to upstage Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s appeal by Biden to union members on the frontline of the \uc2 United Auto Workers (UAW) \uc2 strike, which is turning into a proxy struggle for next year\u8217\'20\'19s presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Trump will address about 500 workers, including \uc2 UAW\uc2  members, at Drake Enterprises, a non-unionised car parts maker in Macomb county, a few miles from where Biden spoke to striking employees picketing a Ford facility.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s speech is timed to coincide with the second Republican primary debate at the \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  library in Sima Valley, California, which Trump is avoiding to cement his self-anointed status as the party\u8217\'20\'19s presidential candidate-in-waiting. Seven Republican candidates, all of them trailing Trump by wide margins in opinion surveys, will take part in the debate.\par \par \uc2 Trump has emitted a sympathetic feeling towards workers striking against Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  without explicitly endorsing their demands for a 40% pay rise, shorter working hours and better pensions \u8211\'20\'13 which polls show a majority of Americans support.\par \par \uc2 Instead, he has accused the \uc2 UAW\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s leadership of selling its members \u8220\'20\'1Cdown the river\u8221\'20\'1D and attacked Biden\u8217\'20\'19s clean energy policy of incentivizing the three car giants to convert to manufacturing electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CREMEMBER, HE [Biden] WANTS TO TAKE YOUR JOBS AWAY AND GIVE THEM TO CHINA AND OTHER FOREIGN COUNTRIES,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump wrote on his Truth Social site. \u8220\'20\'1CI WILL KEEP YOUR JOBS AND MAKE YOU RICH.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/sep/26/who-is-shawn-fain-uaw-strike-union-president"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Shawn Fain}}}\uc2 , the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, who stood beside Biden on Tuesday after inviting him to join the picket line despite having so far withheld his union\u8217\'20\'19s endorsement of the president\u8217\'20\'19s re-election bid, has unequivocally dismissed Trump\u8217\'20\'19s efforts at drumming up union support.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery fibre of our union is being poured into fighting the billionaire class and an economy that enriches people like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the expense of workers,\u8221\'20\'1D he said when the strike started on 15 September.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t keep electing billionaires and millionaires that don\u8217\'20\'19t have any understanding of what it is like to live paycheck to paycheck and struggle to get by and expecting them to solve the problems of the working class.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Trump has a track record of winning electoral support among union members \u8211\'20\'13 despite implementing a tax cut as president in 2017 that was overwhelmingly viewed as favoring the rich and corporate America.\par \par \uc2 He narrowly won Michigan, seen as a must-win state in the 2024 race, in 2016 before losing it in his defeat to Biden in 2020.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNowadays, social issues seem to move union members as much as anything and posturing works, as Trump has proved,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Larry Sabato\uc2 , a politics professor at the \uc2 University of Virginia in Charlottesville\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CJust playing the tough guy, the way Trump does, excites labour union members in many states. He isn\u8217\'20\'19t going to win an outright majority of blue-collar workers, but he could persuade enough of them to repeat the success he had in 2016 in the right sort of circumstances \u8211\'20\'13 and they won\u8217\'20\'19t be particularly influenced by criminal convictions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230927ej9r001ms\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
