\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 Hollywood writers agree to end five-month strike after new studio deal\b0\par\par\uc2 Dani Anguiano in Los Angeles \par \uc2 828 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2023\par 14:18 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Writers Guild of America said its members could return to work while a ratification vote takes place for fresh three-year contract\par \par \uc2 Hollywood writers are officially ending their five-month strike, after union leaders approved an agreement made with the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  and sent the full details of the new contract to union members for ratification.\par \par \uc2 The Writers Guild of America (WGA) said in a statement on Tuesday evening that writers would be free to work starting after midnight local time, while a ratification vote takes place on a new three-year contract with Hollywood studios that won concessions on writers\u8217\'20\'19 payment, terms with streaming shows, and the use of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Negotiators for the Writers Guild of America (WGA) and an alliance of studios, streaming services and production companies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/sep/25/hollywood-writers-strike-end-wga-deal-details-negotiations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reached a tentative agreement}}}\uc2  on Sunday after five marathon days of talks.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, the WGA hailed the three-year agreement as \u8220\'20\'1Cexceptional \u8211\'20\'13 with meaningful gains and protections for writers in every sector of the membership\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Late-night and daytime television shows are expected to return to the air quickly. But most TV and film productions cannot yet resume as the actors\u8217\'20\'19 strike is still under way, and the strikes have already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/jul/14/the-hollywood-actors-strike-everything-you-need-to-know"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 affected the production}}}\uc2  and release schedules for the coming months.\par \par \uc2 The move brings a dramatic chapter in Hollywood to a close after the strike \u8211\'20\'13 one of the longest in the industry\u8217\'20\'19s history \u8211\'20\'13brought productions to a halt, sending shockwaves {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/jul/29/los-angeles-actors-writers-strike-businesses-affected"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 across Los Angeles}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/aug/13/already-vulnerable-uk-film-and-tv-workers-feel-the-pinch-from-us-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 beyond}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In May, the WGA voted overwhelmingly to authorize its 11,500 members to strike for the first time in 15 years amid stalled negotiations with major studios. Writers argued they were receiving worse pay and less stable work in the online-streaming era. The union also demanded additional protections around studios\u8217\'20\'19 use of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Writers spent months on picket lines outside major studios from Los Angeles to New York, including \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , Paramount and \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 . A few months after the writers began their strike, the Hollywood actors\u8217\'20\'19 union \uc2 Sag-Aftra\uc2  undertook similar action, in what amounted to a historic \u8220\'20\'1Cdouble strike\u8221\'20\'1D, the first such action in over six decades.\par \par \uc2 The writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike, which lasted 146 days, was just five days shy of becoming the longest strike in the guild\u8217\'20\'19s history.\par \par \uc2 A summary {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wgacontract2023.org/the-campaign/summary-of-the-2023-wga-mba"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 of the deal}}}\uc2  with the studios was made public on Tuesday. Writers won concessions across multiple areas they said were crucial, including residual payments for shows on streaming platforms, more transparency on viewership numbers for streaming platforms such as \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , minimum numbers of writers for pre-development \u8220\'20\'1Cmini rooms\u8221\'20\'1D, and guidelines for the use of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Members are set to vote on the deal between 2 and 9 October.\par \par \uc2 The agreement with the WGA came after negotiators renewed talks following a months-long impasse. Four top industry executives \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , the \uc2 Disney\uc2  CEO; \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2 , the \uc2 Warner Bros Discovery\uc2  CEO; \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2 , the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  co-CEO; and \uc2 Donna Langley\uc2 , the \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2  Studio group chair \u8211\'20\'13 took part in this week\u8217\'20\'19s negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The end of the Hollywood actors\u8217\'20\'19 strike may not be on the horizon yet, but the deal for writers has ignited a new spirit of optimism and animated those who are still picketing. The deal could provide a path forward to resolve the actors\u8217\'20\'19 strike, as the WGA agreement addresses concerns similar.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor a hot second, I really thought that this was going to go on until next year,\u8221\'20\'1D said Marissa Cuevas, an actor who has appeared on the TV series Kung Fu and The Big Bang Theory. \u8220\'20\'1CKnowing that at least one of us has gotten a good deal gives a lot of hope that we will also get a good deal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Writers\u8217\'20\'19 picket lines had been suspended, but they were encouraged to walk in solidarity with actors, and many were on the lines on Tuesday, including Mad Men creator Matthew Weiner, who picketed alongside friend and ER actor Noah Wyle, as he has throughout the strikes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe would never have had the leverage we had if Sag had not gone out,\u8221\'20\'1D Weiner said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey were very brave to do it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Striking actors have also voted to authorize their leadership to potentially expand their walkout to include the lucrative video-game market, a step that could put new pressure on Hollywood studios to make a deal with performers who provide voices and stunts for games.\par \par \uc2 Sag-Aftra, the Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Radio and Television Artists, announced the move late Monday, saying that 98% of its members voted to go on strike against video-game companies if negotiations are not successful.\par \par \uc2 Some of the same issues are at play in the video-game negotiations as in the broader actors\u8217\'20\'19 strike, including wages, safety measures and protections on the use of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Lois Beckett and agencies contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230927ej9r0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
