\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Trump stabbed labor over and over. Now he says he supports striking auto workers?\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,319 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2023\par 18:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s claim that he \u8216\'20\'18always\u8217\'20\'19 has workers\u8217\'20\'19 backs is laughable. As president he undercut them at every opportunity\par \par \uc2 Strikes are painful affairs for unions and companies alike, but that hasn\u8217\'20\'19t stopped \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from making plans to go to Detroit next week to try to make political hay out of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 historic strike. The former president is planning a prime-time speech before 500 workers that will trumpet the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/labor-union-auto-workers-trump-strike-dfcb805fd4e749b13aaf827e1463da73"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 message}}}\uc2  that he has \u8220\'20\'1Calways had their back\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As with so many Trump pronouncements, that is appalling poppycock. During Trump\u8217\'20\'19s four years as president, he and his administration did far more to stab workers in their backs.\par \par \uc2 Trump didn\u8217\'20\'19t lift a finger to increase the federal minimum wage, which has been stuck at a pathetically low $7.25 an hour since 2009. And he certainly didn\u8217\'20\'19t have workers\u8217\'20\'19 backs when he scrapped \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s move to expand overtime coverage, thereby {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/press/more-than-8-million-workers-will-be-left-behind-by-the-trump-administration-overtime-proposal-poll-shows-support-for-stronger-overtime-protections/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 denying}}}\uc2  8 million workers the ability to receive time-and-a-half overtime pay.\par \par \uc2 If Trump is a friend of workers, why did his administration repeatedly do what corporate lobbyists asked for instead of what worker advocates wanted? Trump made it easier for Wall Street to cheat workers by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2018/06/18/492463.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scrapping}}}\uc2  the \u8220\'20\'1Cfiduciary\u8221\'20\'1D rule that required investment firms to act in the best interests of workers and retirees in handling their 401ks. Showing little concern about worker safety, Trump moved to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloombergenvironment.com/safety/trump-administration-fails-to-loosen-mine-inspection-rule"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 relax}}}\uc2  rules for coal mine inspections and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/national/health-science/trump-administration-to-overhaul-safety-monitoring-rules-for-offshore-drilling/2017/12/28/37cb40bc-ec20-11e7-9f92-10a2203f6c8d_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 roll back}}}\uc2  safety requirements for oil rig workers. His administration even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/29/us/politics/epa-insecticide-chlorpyrifos.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reversed}}}\uc2  the ban on a toxic pesticide, chlorpyrifos, that causes acute reactions in farmworkers and neurological damage to children.\par \par \uc2 In a big favor to corporate America, Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2017/03/trump-lets-federal-contractors-abuse-their-workers-again.html?gtm=bottom"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rolled back}}}\uc2  rules that made it harder to award federal contracts to companies that were repeat violators of wage laws, sexual harassment laws, racial discrimination laws, or laws protecting workers\u8217\'20\'19 right to unionize. Then, in a favor to financial firms, Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/feb/07/payday-loans-rule-consumer-financial-protection-roll-back"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 repealed}}}\uc2  regulations that aimed to prevent payday lenders from preying on financially strapped workers.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s appointees were far more pro-business than pro-worker. He named a string of corporate water carriers to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  who often seemed to see their role as undermining unions and making it harder for workers to unionize. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 NLRB\uc2  appointees said, for instance, that gig economy workers like \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/14/business/economy/nlrb-uber-drivers-contractors.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 should be considered}}}\uc2  independent contractors, not employees, thus preventing them from unionizing under federal law.\par \par \uc2 Labor leaders should issue a warning: workers of the world, unite against Trump\u8217\'20\'19s con\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s appointees to the supreme court have shown scant sympathy toward workers and outright hostility toward unions. Remember that \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  once ruled that a truck driver {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2017/mar/23/neil-gorsuch-supreme-court-frozen-trucker-alphonse-maddin"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deserved to be fired}}}\uc2  for disobeying his boss\u8217\'20\'19s order and leaving his broken-down vehicle in sub-zero weather \u8211\'20\'13 a move that probably saved the driver\u8217\'20\'19s life. It was Gorsuch who delivered the deciding vote in the 5-4 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/27/us/politics/supreme-court-unions-organized-labor.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Janus v AFSCME case}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the most important anti-union decision in decades. In that ruling, the court\u8217\'20\'19s rightwing majority said that teachers, firefighters and other government employees couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be required to pay any dues or fees to the unions that bargained for them and won raises for them.\par \par \uc2 All this makes clear that Trump\u8217\'20\'19s claim that he \u8220\'20\'1Calways\u8221\'20\'1D has workers\u8217\'20\'19 back is laughable. Labor leaders should issue a warning: workers of the world, unite against Trump\u8217\'20\'19s con.\par \par \uc2 Trump doesn\u8217\'20\'19t like labor unions, but he loves the idea of union members supporting him. Indeed, Trump has repeatedly worked to undermine unions. He {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jacobin.com/2023/09/donald-trump-uaw-ev-biden-big-three-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recently said}}}\uc2  that workers shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t pay their union dues; only a person who wants to hobble unions would say such a thing. Not only that, Trump has repeatedly sought to turn union members against their leaders, a strategy that would undoubtedly weaken unions and make them less effective in pressuring powerful corporations to agree to better pay and conditions. In one {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://finance.yahoo.com/news/autoworkers-not-jobs-donald-trump-163000903.html#:~:text=The%20electric%20cars%2C%20automatically%2C%20are,%2C%22%20the%20former%20president%20said."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 divide-and-conquer}}}\uc2  statement about unions, Trump said, in a recent Meet the Press interview: \u8220\'20\'1CThe auto workers are being sold down the river by their leadership.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump also has a record of attacking labor leaders, including, most recently, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  president, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/feb/07/payday-loans-rule-consumer-financial-protection-roll-back"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Shawn Fain}}}\uc2  ; but also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/realDonaldTrump/status/1036637078640058368?s=20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Richard Trumka}}}\uc2 , the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s late, hugely respected president; and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/realDonaldTrump/status/1107335457006866435"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dave Green}}}\uc2 , a popular \uc2 UAW\uc2  official in Ohio who fought to keep \uc2 GM\uc2  from closing its giant \uc2 Lordstown\uc2  plant.\par \par \uc2 While running for president in 2016, Trump sought to woo blue-collar workers by boasting that he\u8217\'20\'19d enact a colossal, $1tn infrastructure plan. As president, he trumpeted one \u8220\'20\'1Cinfrastructure week\u8221\'20\'1D after another, but that devolved into a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/22/us/politics/trump-infrastructure-week.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pathetic joke}}}\uc2  because he failed abysmally in enacting an infrastructure plan or creating the hundreds of thousands of jobs construction workers hoped for.\par \par \uc2 Trump did get Congress to pass one important piece of legislation, but it certainly wasn\u8217\'20\'19t worker-friendly. It was $1.5tn in tax cuts that went overwhelmingly to corporations and the richest 1%, while giving peanuts to typical workers like the \uc2 UAW\uc2  members now out on strike.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more Trump poppycock: he likes nothing more than to brag that \u8220\'20\'1Cwe had the best economy ever\u8221\'20\'1D when he was president, something that \uc2 John F Kennedy\uc2 , \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and many other presidents would quarrel with. Trump won\u8217\'20\'19t admit that the day he was inaugurated, the economy was already on third base, and Trump thought he had hit a triple.\par \par \uc2 Under Obama, the jobless rate fell from a peak of 10.0% to a low of 4.8% when he left office. Under Trump, the jobless rate slid from 4.8% to 3.5% (before it soared during the pandemic). Moreover, job growth was far weaker under Trump than under Biden. During Biden\u8217\'20\'19s first 31 months in office, through August 2023, nonfarm payroll employment rose by 433,000 jobs a month on average. During Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first 31 months, it rose by less than half that, 176,000 jobs a month (that was before the pandemic hit).\par \par \uc2 When Trump seeks to woo auto workers in his extravaganza next week in Detroit, he will undoubtedly assert yet again that electric vehicles (EVs) are bad for auto workers and are destroying jobs. At the same time, Trump will attack Biden, \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders and Detroit\u8217\'20\'19s automakers for not agreeing to slam the brakes on EVs. Trump has unfortunately bought into the hoax, long promoted by fossil fuel companies and rightwing interests, that the climate crisis isn\u8217\'20\'19t real. The world\u8217\'20\'19s automakers disagree, however; they recognize that the climate crisis is very real and very dangerous, which is part of why they\u8217\'20\'19re racing to make the transition to EVs.\par \par \uc2 Detroit\u8217\'20\'19s automakers and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  realize that if \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  pull up the rear in developing EVs, that will threaten their long-term viability along with tens of thousands of \uc2 UAW\uc2  jobs. Auto workers should resist Trump\u8217\'20\'19s dire, demagogic warnings that the transition to EVs will be hugely dangerous for them (even though it\u8217\'20\'19s true that it takes fewer workers to make an EV than an internal combustion vehicle). In the worldwide competition to produce cars, Detroit\u8217\'20\'19s automakers know that embracing Trump\u8217\'20\'19s anti-EV mantra and ending up in last place in developing electric vehicles could doom their future and lead to bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 Workers shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t let Trump take them for fools. When Trump tells workers he has their back, he thinks he\u8217\'20\'19s a clever wolf trying to reassure a flock of sheep that he has their back.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Steven Greenhouse, a senior fellow at the \uc2 Century Foundation\uc2 , is a longtime American labor and workplace journalist and writer, and the author of Beaten Down, Worked Up: The Past, Present, and Future of American Labor\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230925ej9p001mk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
