\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s fantastic that Biden is joining the \uc2 UAW\uc2  picket line. But he should go further\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Reich \par \uc2 950 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2023\par 10:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Biden has pushed key policies to fight soaring US inequality. Yet he\u8217\'20\'19s been reluctant to name and shame its causes\par \par \uc2 Kudos to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for joining the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  picket line on Tuesday. He\u8217\'20\'19s the first president to join a picket line in living memory.\par \par \uc2 But he shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t stop there.\par \par \uc2 He should criticize the CEOs of America\u8217\'20\'19s big corporations who are now raking in more than 350 times what the average American worker is earning. Blast corporations that are monopolizing their industries. Condemn firms that are using their profits to buy back shares of stock, polluting the planet with carbon emissions and polluting our democracy with big money.\par \par \uc2 He wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be the first Democratic president to do this.\par \par \uc2 On the eve of the 1936 election, President \uc2 Franklin D Roosevelt\uc2  warned America that business and financial monopolies and war profiteers had begun to consider the \uc2 US government\par \par \uc2 as a mere appendage to their own affairs. We know now that Government by organized money is just as dangerous as Government by organized mob \u8230\'20\'26 Never before in all our history have these forces been so united against one candidate as they stand today. They are unanimous in their hate for me \u8211\'20\'13 and I welcome their hatred.\par \par \uc2 The US is again in a populist age, when a vast army of Americans have been shafted by big corporations, Wall Street and a government corrupted by monied interests.\par \par \uc2 The biggest change over the last three decades \u8211\'20\'13 the change lurking behind the insecurities and resentments of the working middle class \u8211\'20\'13 has nothing to do with identity politics, \u8220\'20\'1Cwoke\u8221\'20\'1D-ism, immigration, critical race theory, transgender kids or any other current Republican bogeymen.\par \par \uc2 It has directly to do with a huge upward shift in the distribution of income and wealth.\par \par \uc2 Although total wealth is much greater now than it was four decades ago, the distribution of that wealth is far more unequal. The bottom 50% hasn\u8217\'20\'19t budged. Wealth at the top has exploded.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a declining share of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s wealth has been going to workers, and an exponentially rising share to CEOs and big investors.\par \par \uc2 This change didn\u8217\'20\'19t happen because of so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cneutral market forces\u8221\'20\'1D. It happened because of policy decisions made over the last four decades. For example:\par \par \uc2 To open the American economy wide to imports from China. To deregulate Wall Street and allow it to make bets with other people\u8217\'20\'19s money.\par \par \uc2 To dramatically cut taxes on big corporations and the rich. To let corporations bash unions and fire workers who try to organize.\par \par \uc2 To encourage activist investors and private equity companies to take over \u8220\'20\'1Cunderperforming\u8221\'20\'1D companies and then promptly fire workers and sell off assets.\par \par \uc2 To allow big corporations to become far larger, monopolizing entire industries.\par \par \uc2 To allow pharmaceutical companies to extend their patents and jack up the prices of critical drugs. To allow oil companies access to federal lands and to benefit from special tax write-offs.\par \par \uc2 To bail out the biggest banks but not homeowners who get caught in the downdrafts. To privatize higher education and force students to take out massive loans.\par \par \uc2 To Biden\u8217\'20\'19s credit, he and most Democratic lawmakers in Congress have pushed for policies that will make the nation more equitable\par \par \uc2 To encourage corporations to buy back their shares of stock rather than reinvest profits.\par \par \uc2 These policy decisions didn\u8217\'20\'19t just happen, either. They were pushed by wealthy elites on Wall Street and in C-suites who made mammoth donations to politicians on both sides of the aisle \u8211\'20\'13 mostly but not exclusively Republican \u8211\'20\'13 to ensure that their wishes would be honored.\par \par \uc2 To Biden\u8217\'20\'19s credit, he and most Democratic lawmakers in Congress have pushed for policies that will make the nation more equitable, such as childcare and eldercare subsidies, student-loan forgiveness and negotiated drug prices. Kudos.\par \par \uc2 But Biden seems reluctant to blame CEOs, Wall Street moguls and the super-rich for what\u8217\'20\'19s happened.\par \par \uc2 Yet they are to blame, as are their lackeys in Washington.\par \par \uc2 They have turned their growing wealth into increasing political power to change the rules of the game in ways that further enlarge their wealth and power, while neglecting and exploiting the bottom half.\par \par \uc2 Biden should condemn them, as did FDR. He should name the CEOs, leaders of finance, heads of pharmaceutical companies, defense contractors, internet moguls and \u8220\'20\'1Cactivist\u8221\'20\'1D investors who have profited at the expense of the rest of the US.\par \par \uc2 He should unambiguously be on the side of workers in their struggle for better pay and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 He should attack corporate welfare \u8211\'20\'13 the special tax loopholes, bank bailouts, unconditional subsidies, loan guarantees and no-bid contracts that have lined the pockets of the wealthy, paid for by the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 Let Republicans criticize corporate \u8220\'20\'1Cwokeness\u8221\'20\'1D. Biden should campaign against corporate greed.\par \par \uc2 Let Republicans obsess about critical race theory, immigration and sex. Biden should campaign against how obscenely unfair and unequal the US has become.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s good that Biden\u8217\'20\'19s joining the \uc2 UAW\uc2  picket line. But if he and other Democrats don\u8217\'20\'19t tell the economic truth about what\u8217\'20\'19s happened and place the blame squarely where it\u8217\'20\'19s deserved, the lies of Republicans will fill the void.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 , a former US secretary of labor, is a professor of public policy at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , and the author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/saving-capitalism-for-the-many-not-the-few/9780345806222"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Saving Capitalism: For the Many, Not the Few}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-common-good-9780525436379/9780525436379"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Common Good}}}\uc2 . His newest book, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-system-who-rigged-it-how-we-fix-it/9780525659044"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The System: Who Rigged It, How We Fix It}}}\uc2 , is out now. He is a Guardian US columnist. His newsletter is at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://robertreich.substack.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 robertreich.substack.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230926ej9q0018o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
