\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 Is the Hollywood writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike over? The provisional deal explained\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 1,309 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2023\par 20:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A tentative agreement has been announced but what does it mean for the industry and the viewers?\par \par \uc2 After nearly five months \u8211\'20\'13 146 days, to be exact \u8211\'20\'13 the Hollywood writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike appears to be nearing its end, as the Writers Guild of America (WGA) reached {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/sep/25/hollywood-writers-strike-end-wga-deal-details-negotiations"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a provisional deal}}}\uc2  with the group representing the studios and streamers. If ratified by union membership, the deal would conclude one of the longest work stoppages in the union\u8217\'20\'19s history; the current record was set in 1988, when the WGA struck for 154 days.\par \par \uc2 The tentative agreement was announced on Sunday night after a marathon weekend of negotiations. \u8220\'20\'1CWe can say, with great pride, that this deal is exceptional \u8211\'20\'13 with meaningful gains and protections for writers in every sector of the membership,\u8221\'20\'1D the WGA said in an email to strike captains on Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 While there is still much that is unclear \u8211\'20\'13 the nature of the deal, which still needs to be ironed out in contractual language and ratified by union leadership and its 11,500 members, has yet to be revealed \u8211\'20\'13 the picture of a post-strike Hollywood is coming into focus. Here is what we know so far:\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s in the deal?\par \par \uc2 The provisional, three-year contract between the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  (\uc2 AMPTP\uc2 ) and the WGA will address the main three issues undergirding the strike: protections against the encroachment of artificial intelligence on writers\u8217\'20\'19 work, residual payments for shows on streaming platforms and staffing minimums for writers\u8217\'20\'19 rooms on TV shows.\par \par \uc2 Concerns around the future of AI emerged as arguably the leading issue and, for many, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jul/22/sag-aftra-wga-strike-artificial-intelligence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most charged}}}\uc2  ; Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s push into AI is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/aug/21/ai-jobs-hollywood-writers-actors-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 already under way}}}\uc2  and the dual strike with the actors\u8217\'20\'19 union offered one of the first and strongest labor opportunities to shape the path ahead for technology that will undoubtedly shape many employment sectors. The WGA was particularly concerned about the possibility that streamers and studios could use generative AI \u8211\'20\'13 the type of machine-learning systems capable of creating text, images and video, such as \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ChatGPT \u8211\'20\'13 to cut costs by foregoing human writers for AI-produced scripts.\par \par \uc2 The details on the AI concessions are still scarce, but the Hollywood Reporter {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/writers-guild-of-america-deal-studios-end-strike-1235597573/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 noted}}}\uc2  that negotiations over AI regulations were the final sticking point heading into the weekend. According to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/09/25/business/wga-writers-strike-deal-explained.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2 , the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  proposed this weekend to add a few paragraphs to the contract about AI and old scripts owned by studios. The two sides negotiated the language for several hours on the final night of talks.\par \par \uc2 The new agreement also reportedly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/writers-guild-deal-reached-studios-end-of-strike-1235403981/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 includes}}}\uc2  a plan for residual compensations tied to streaming show performance that is not tied to health and pension funds. Residuals for shows that succeed on streaming platforms \u8211\'20\'13 such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2023/aug/17/suits-netflix-america-most-watched-summer-show"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this summer\u8217\'20\'19s hit Suits}}}\uc2 , a 2010s legal drama that broke streaming records on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 have lagged far behind those of shows on linear networks.\par \par \uc2 Another sticking point was the WGA\u8217\'20\'19s proposal for TV staffing minimums \u8211\'20\'13 six to 12 writers a show, depending on the number of episodes per season \u8212\'20\'14\u8211\'20\'13 which the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  had refused to consider and some within the guild {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2023/biz/news/writers-strike-screenwriters-oppose-staff-mininum-1235694323/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 privately opposed}}}\uc2 . In the final weeks of negotiations, the studios reportedly budged by offering showrunners flexibility to hire a certain number of writers based on the budget of the show. It remains unclear the precise compromise reached by the two sides on staffing minimums and the regulation of \u8220\'20\'1Cmini-rooms\u8221\'20\'1D, seen by many as a way to avoid the cost of a full writers\u8217\'20\'19 room.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s next?\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s still possible that the strike could go on; the deal must now be ratified by the guild\u8217\'20\'19s several leadership boards (including the union membership committee, \uc2 WGA West\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s board and \uc2 WGA East\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s council), which are set for a vote on Tuesday. After that, members will receive a summary of the agreement for a ratification vote.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the WGA has suspended picketing, though it reminded members that no one is to return to work until given the green light and the lawyerly contract is formalized. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat remains now is for our staff to make sure everything we have agreed to is codified in final contract language,\u8221\'20\'1D WGA reps said in the email to strike captains on Sunday night. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd though we are eager to share the details of what has been achieved with you, we cannot do that until the last \u8216\'20\'18i\u8217\'20\'19 is dotted.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Writers could return to work quickly if union leadership approves the deal, as they can also vote to end the strike while the rank-and-file vote is still underway. \u8220\'20\'1CThis would allow writers to return to work during the ratification vote, but would not affect the membership\u8217\'20\'19s right to make a final determination on contract approval,\u8221\'20\'1D WGA leaders {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/live/2023/09/24/business/hollywood-writers-strike-news/4d5332ef-25d1-51a9-846a-da6ef56018e7?smid=url-share"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, most TV and film production will be unable to resume, as the actors\u8217\'20\'19 strike is still under way and the joint strikes have already altered the production and release schedule for months to come. With performers still out, there will probably be long delays between script and screen and months before Hollywood work returns to pre-strike levels. Studios have pushed the release of major titles, such as Dune: Part Two and Luca Guadagnino\u8217\'20\'19s Challengers, until 2024. Some TV shows, such as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Prime\u8217\'20\'19s A League of Their Own and The Peripheral, went from indefinitely delayed to cancelled, and the production on many others has been delayed.\par \par \uc2 Without such popular shows as Abbott Elementary and Young Sheldon, which shuttered their writers\u8217\'20\'19 rooms for the strike in May, the major networks have already filled their fall TV schedules with reality programming and reruns. Fox developed a new game show called Snake Oil, while ABC expanded its Bachelor franchise with The Golden Bachelor, premiering this week. \uc2 CBS\uc2  is airing old episodes of Yellowstone in prime time and ABC {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2023-09-18/abc-adds-monday-night-football-games-strikes-bench-entertainment-shows-nfl-writers-strike-actors-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 began simulcasting}}}\uc2 ESPN\uc2  broadcasts of Monday Night Football.\par \par \uc2 Some scripted network shows filmed before the strike, such as Quantum Leap and Magnum PI, will return with new seasons this fall; others, such as Grey\u8217\'20\'19s Anatomy, Abbott Elementary and Law and Order, will not. With the WGA expected to ratify the deal, at least some of the 2023-2024 broadcast season could be saved, with production resuming for some shows as early as Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 Late-night and daytime television \u8211\'20\'13 the first casualties of the work stoppage in May \u8211\'20\'13 could get back on the air quickly. And Deadline {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2023/09/saturday-night-live-expected-to-return-next-month-1235555570/#recipient_hashed=58e80303eb86ef6e1590496f3313a95182cb7e47e4431739bdb854b95b0768c7&recipient_salt=7e749829dd8fdacaf3affd4f51643a323740a6b6883a45b85c04133fdf95e512"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  on Monday that Saturday Night Live, which usually begins at the end of September, will get most of its season; the show is expected to return on 7 or 14 October, pending the WGA vote, with non-actor hosts and potentially without some cast members in solidarity with the actors.\par \par \uc2 Will this\par \par \uc2 affect the actors\u8217\'20\'19 strike?\par \par \uc2 Not directly \u8211\'20\'13 the Screen Actors Guild (Sag-Aftra), the union that represents more than 160,000 performers, has stressed that it will not be governed by other unions\u8217\'20\'19 contract deals (as did the WGA, which was on strike after the directors\u8217\'20\'19 guild reached a non-strike agreement with the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2 ). The actors have been on a separate strike since 14 July, though many of its demands overlap with writers and many A-listers have picketed jointly with them.\par \par \uc2 Still, a deal with the WGA could provide a blueprint for resolving the actors\u8217\'20\'19 strike, which has taken a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2023-09-23/hollywood-crew-members-fight-to-hang-on-as-the-strikes-drag-on-were-the-collateral-damage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mounting toll}}}\uc2  on performers and below-the-line staff. The tentative writers\u8217\'20\'19 agreement addresses similar concerns to actors, namely strong guardrails against the encroachment of AI \u8211\'20\'13 actors are worried about the rights to their digital likenesses, for example \u8211\'20\'13 and streaming residuals.\par \par \uc2 While championing its own deal, the WGA has encouraged members to continue supporting actors on their picket lines when they resume on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230925ej9p002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
