\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The writers\u8217\'20\'19 strikes aren\u8217\'20\'19t just about Hollywood \u8211\'20\'13 British workers like me are now out of work\b0\par\par\uc2 Elise Tyson \par \uc2 823 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2023\par 16:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The film and TV industry has always relied on financial precarity for the people who make it tick. It only takes one shock to put freelancers into hardship\par \par \uc2 I can pinpoint the exact moment I knew I wanted to be a film-maker. I\u8217\'20\'19d received The Fellowship of the Ring extended edition DVD for my 11th birthday and devoured the behind-the-scenes footage faster the film itself. I felt my chest flutter with excitement as I watched the crew recount how important the experience was to them. I wanted to follow in their footsteps and make people feel the way I had watching that film.\par \par \uc2 Years later, that ambition became a reality. But now, when I think of film-making, I feel knots of dread in my stomach instead of excitement. I\u8217\'20\'19m one of the thousands of workers in the UK who have lost their jobs as a direct result of the actors\u8217\'20\'19 strikes in the US. In July, actors joined writers on the picket line fighting for more equitable residuals in an ever-changing streaming landscape, and for fair compensation for the use of their likeness by AI.\par \par \uc2 A recent survey of members by the UK Broadcasting, Entertainment, Communications and Theatre Union (\uc2 Bectu\uc2 ) revealed that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bectu.org.uk/news/three-quarters-of-uk-film-and-tv-workers-currently-out-of-work-bectu-survey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three-quarters of us are now unemployed}}}\uc2 . As a contractor on a large US production shooting in London, it felt inevitable. Somehow, we\u8217\'20\'19d survived writers going on strike but you can\u8217\'20\'19t shoot a TV series without actors.\par \par \uc2 Industry freelance contracts give just one week\u8217\'20\'19s notice of termination. Our workforce is ineligible for severance or redundancy pay. So, once negotiations broke down between the joint Screen Actors Guild and American Federation of Television and Radio Artists (Sag-Aftra) and the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  (\uc2 AMPTP\uc2 ) in the US, 200 crew members on our production here in the UK received our notice. With just one week\u8217\'20\'19s pay and unused holiday leave, we then faced a seemingly endless period of uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 It was so depressingly familiar. When Covid hit, our industry was the first on the chopping block. We were deemed \u8220\'20\'1Cnon-essential workers\u8221\'20\'1D while locked-down audiences devoured content at unprecedented levels. Many left the industry. A year on, we\u8217\'20\'19re collateral damage again. For a country with such a rich cinematic history, Britain is precariously dependent on the Hollywood machine.\par \par \uc2 Film and TV work no longer feels viable to me. I\u8217\'20\'19m a millennial who works minimum 50-hour weeks (longer during filming) but struggles due to the contractual nature of my income, especially during the cost of living crisis.In the UK, \uc2 Bectu\uc2  recommends entry-level roles start at \u163\'00\'A312.50 an hour. It\u8217\'20\'19s technically above minimum wage, but doesn\u8217\'20\'19t go far when a contract only lasts a week or a month and it takes another three months to secure the next one. God forbid I should ever apply for a mortgage or become a mother, without paid parental leave or part-time options.\par \par \uc2 My spare time now \u8211\'20\'13 and there\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of it \u8211\'20\'13 is spent hopping between temporary admin jobs and pursuing entry-level roles in advertising. Now in my thirties, I\u8217\'20\'19ll be starting at the bottom again. Other friends are working in cafes, or as receptionists. We feel as if we\u8217\'20\'19re regressing, but at two months out of work and counting, we have little choice \u8211\'20\'13 35% of UK crew are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/aug/13/already-vulnerable-uk-film-and-tv-workers-feel-the-pinch-from-us-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struggling to pay household bills, rent or mortgages}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The most frustrating aspect is how little the outside world understands. Most people I speak to are vaguely aware of the strikes, but admit they had no idea it affected those outside Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s elite. \u8220\'20\'1CTone deaf rich celebrity complains about being less rich,\u8221\'20\'1D says one comment under a social media post about the strike. \u8220\'20\'1CNobody gives af,\u8221\'20\'1D says another. It\u8217\'20\'19s one thing to lose your job through no fault of your own. It\u8217\'20\'19s another to have that experience invalidated.\par \par \uc2 There is one way in which the commenters on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  are right: this industry is for the rich, but the studios are the ones hoarding the wealth. If they continue prioritising greed, more workers will leave. The elite will stay and call the shots as they always have; AI will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/aug/21/ai-jobs-hollywood-writers-actors-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 replace writers}}}\uc2  and those in power will churn out uninspired, unoriginal content as disconnected as the people greenlighting it. Audiences will lose more respect for the industry, and the cycle will endure.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ll always love cinema and the transformative power of storytelling. But not this storyline. Without change, workers like me will continue to be discarded on the cutting room floor. Another grim reminder that this industry, frankly, my dear, doesn\u8217\'20\'19t give a damn.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Elise Tyson is a film-maker and writer from Australia. She lives in London\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230925ej9p00209\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
