\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b \uc2 Hollywood writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike: WGA reaches \u8216\'20\'18tentative\u8217\'20\'19 deal to end 146-day strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Lois Beckett in Los Angeles and agencies \par \uc2 1,343 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2023\par 12:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 After five days of negotiations with studios and streaming services, union says it has an \u8216\'20\'18exceptional\u8217\'20\'19 deal for members to vote on\par \par \uc2 Hollywood writers are poised to end their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/aug/21/hollywood-strike-affected-movies-tv-shows"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly five-month strike}}}\uc2  after reaching a tentative agreement with studios, the writers\u8217\'20\'19 union and the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  said Sunday night, though the full details of agreement still have to be formally approved.\par \par \uc2 The deal came after five marathon days of renewed talks by negotiators for the Writers Guild of America (WGA) and an alliance of studios, streaming services and production companies, and must be approved by the guild\u8217\'20\'19s board and members before the strike officially ends.\par \par \uc2 The terms of the deal were not immediately announced, but the WGA called the deal \u8220\'20\'1Cexceptional \u8211\'20\'13 with meaningful gains and protections for writers in every sector of the membership,\u8221\'20\'1D in an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/adamconover/status/1706139212519706784"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 email to members}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The WGA and \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  have reached a tentative agreement. This was made possible by the enduring solidarity of WGA members and extraordinary support of our union siblings who stood with us for over 146 days. More details coming after contract language is finalized. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/WGAStrike?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #WGAStrike}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/GBg2wZBwGB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/GBg2wZBwGB}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Writers Guild of America West\uc2  (@WGAWest) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WGAWest/status/1706136145019683010?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 25, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Four top industry executives \u8211\'20\'13 Iger, \uc2 Warner Bros Discovery\uc2  CEO \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2  co-CEO \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2  and NBCUniversal Studio Group Chair \uc2 Donna Langley\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 joined negotiations this week, helping to break the months-long impasse.\par \par \uc2 California Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  touted his support for striking workers in a statement Sunday night, saying writers \u8220\'20\'1Cwent on strike over existential threats to their careers and livelihoods \u8211\'20\'13 expressing real concerns over the stress and anxiety workers are feeling. I am grateful that the two sides have come together.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The agreement comes just five days before the strike would\u8217\'20\'19ve broken the record of the union\u8217\'20\'19s 1988 strike to become the longest in the guild\u8217\'20\'19s history, and the longest Hollywood strike in decades. Union leaders are expected to vote on the full terms of the new three-year contract on Tuesday, according to the union\u8217\'20\'19s email to members, which was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/adamconover/status/1706139212519706784"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted on social media}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 California\u8217\'20\'19s entertainment industry would not be what it is today without our world class writers.\par \par \uc2 We are grateful that the two sides have come together to reach an agreement that benefits all parties involved, and can put a major piece of California\u8217\'20\'19s economy back to work. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/nNUxoc6hnR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/nNUxoc6hnR}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 Office of the Governor of California (@CAgovernor) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CAgovernor/status/1706145260953546924?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 25, 2023}}}\par \par \uc2 Late-night television shows are expected to resume production shortly, and could even return to the air within days, but Hollywood actors, who joined the industry\u8217\'20\'19s historic \u8220\'20\'1Cdouble strike\u8221\'20\'1D in July, remain on strike, meaning that many new film and television projects are expected to stay on hold, and crew members affected by the work stoppage will remain unemployed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe remain on strike in our TV/Theatrical contract and continue to urge the studio and streamer CEOs and the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  to return to the table and make the fair deal that our members deserve and demand,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 Screen Actors Guild{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sagaftra.org/sag-aftra-congratulates-wga-reaching-tentative-agreement-amptp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said in a statement}}}\uc2  congratulating the writers\u8217\'20\'19 union on its tentative agreement.\par \par \uc2 The ongoing work stoppages have taken a toll on camera operators, carpenters, production assistants and other crew members, as well as the caterers, florists, costume suppliers and other small businesses that support film and television production. But unions for other workers affected by the double strike, including the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and the union for stagehands and technical workers, stood in solidarity with the writers and actors\u8217\'20\'19 union throughout the strike.\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles mayor Karen Bass hailed the tentative agreement as a \u8220\'20\'1Cfair deal\u8221\'20\'1D and said she hoped \u8220\'20\'1Cthe same will happen soon for \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D citing the need to get the city\u8217\'20\'19s entertainment industry, a central part of LA\u8217\'20\'19s economy, back on its feet.\par \par \uc2 The 146-day writers\u8217\'20\'19 strike was driven by Hollywood workers\u8217\'20\'19 frustrations with their share of the profits in an online-streaming era, and spotlighted artists\u8217\'20\'19 concerns about the threats artificial intelligence might pose to their industry. While \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , the \uc2 Disney\uc2  CEO, called the writers\u8217\'20\'19 and actors\u8217\'20\'19 demands \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2023-07-13/disney-bob-iger-says-actors-strike-writers-strike-unrealistic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 just not realistic}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D in July, the strike ultimately highlighted the solidarity among unions within Hollywood - and the broad public support for workers\u8217\'20\'19 demands, as the Tinseltown labor disputes kicked off a \u8220\'20\'1Chot labor summer\u8221\'20\'1D of strikes across {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/video/2023/sep/15/were-not-asking-to-be-millionaires-workers-strike-at-us-car-giants-video"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 multiple industries}}}\uc2 . Iger subsequently struck a conciliatory note, citing his \u8220\'20\'1Cdeep respect\u8221\'20\'1D for creative professionals.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis fight was won by the writers\u8217\'20\'19 guild months ago, and the billionaires and the CEOs have just been denying the reality of the situation,\u8221\'20\'1D Alex O\u8217\'20\'19Keefe, a WGA member who has been outspoken about the industry\u8217\'20\'19s toll on early career writers, told the Guardian Sunday night. \u8220\'20\'1CWe had to give the entire industry a reality check.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis was never a fight about money. It was a fight about power. It was a fight about disrespect,\u8221\'20\'1D O\u8217\'20\'19Keefe added, saying he hoped the deal marked \u8220\'20\'1Ca new era in Hollywood.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Striking actors and writers have marched together outside studios across Los Angeles, from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2  to \uc2 Disney\uc2 . To improve morale, some of the picketers have come dressed in themed costumes of the day, like on \u8220\'20\'1CBridgerton Day\u8221\'20\'1D outside \uc2 Netflix\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s studios.\par \par \uc2 Both actors and writers described a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/jul/14/its-a-constant-hustle-actors-join-writers-on-the-picket-line"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shared frustration}}}\uc2  with an industry that increasingly made it difficult for the average worker to earn a middle-class living, and that seemed poised to turn all but the most successful stars into gig workers. And both said they feared that studios would try to replace writers, background actors, designers and other creatives with a smaller number of workers supplemented by artificial intelligence technologies.\par \par \uc2 Variety {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://variety.com/2023/biz/news/writers-strike-ends-wga-amptp-deal-2-1235733452/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  that the language around \u8220\'20\'1Cthe use of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/aug/21/ai-jobs-hollywood-writers-actors-strike"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 generative AI }}}\uc2  in content production\u8221\'20\'1D was one of the final aspects of the new contract the writers and the studios worked on before they reached their tentative deal.\par \par \uc2 In July, Deadline, an industry publication, sparked outrage when it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2023/07/writers-strike-hollywood-studios-deal-fight-wga-actors-1235434335/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quoted an anonymous studio executive}}}\uc2  as saying that studios did not plan to negotiate with writers until the fall, saying \u8220\'20\'1CThe endgame is to allow things to drag on until union members start losing their apartments and losing their houses.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The economic pain of the writers\u8217\'20\'19 146-day strike was met with shows of solidarity from some of the industry\u8217\'20\'19s most powerful celebrities.\par \par \uc2 A-list Hollywood actors made their support for the strikes clear even before the actors\u8217\'20\'19 union had authorized a strike. Some wealthy directors and producers announced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/general-news/ryan-murphy-strike-fund-cast-crews-hollywood-strikes-1235585581/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six-figure donations}}}\uc2  to strike funds to support former cast and crew members, while other stars {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/sep/14/strike-hollywood-crew-ebay-auction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 auctioned off Zoom meetings }}}\uc2  and one-on-one experiences to raise money for a crew healthcare fund.\par \par \uc2 The few celebrities who attempted to flout strike rules \u8211\'20\'13 including Drew Barrymore, who announced she would resume her popular talkshow {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/tv/story/2023-09-11/drew-barrymore-show-backlash-picket-writers-strike-wga"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 while her writers were still on strike}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 faced broad public condemnation, and quickly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2023/sep/18/drew-barrymore-postpones-talk-show-return-writers-strike-backlash"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reversed course}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In May, \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2  CEO \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2  was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/general-news/david-zaslav-booed-boston-university-graduation-writers-strike-1235497440/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 booed and jeered at}}}\uc2  by students at \uc2 Boston University\uc2  as he delivered a commencement address in the early weeks of the writers strike. \u8220\'20\'1CPay your writers!\u8221\'20\'1D the students {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/general-news/david-zaslav-booed-boston-university-graduation-writers-strike-1235497440/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly chanted}}}\uc2  at him.\par \par \uc2 In an email shared on social media on Sunday, the writers guild told its 11,500 members that the strike was not officially over and no one was to return to work. While WGA picketing has been suspended, the guild said, it encouraged writers to continue to march with \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  on its picket lines, which will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sagaftra/status/1706096360313803218"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 resume Tuesday}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to be wild on the picket line,\u8221\'20\'1D O\u8217\'20\'19Keefe, the WGA member, said. This coming week, he said he planned to join the actors\u8217\'20\'19 picket line, and also to show up to picket with the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , who are also on strike. Hollywood writers are \u8220\'20\'1Cnot going anywhere,\u8221\'20\'1D O\u8217\'20\'19Keefe said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are part of the labour movement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press and Reuters contributed reporting\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020230925ej9p00132\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
